window.MATHCOURSE = {"title": "General Math Academy — FAA AMT General", "subtitle": "Everything math on the General written · FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3 · ACS AM.I.H", "passMark": 0.7, "storeKey": "math-academy-v1", "modules": [{"n": 1, "day": "Unit 1", "title": "Whole Numbers & Order of Operations", "ref": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3 pp. 3-1 to 3-2, 3-17 to 3-18 · AM.I.H.K13, R1", "sections": [{"id": "mau1s1", "title": "The four operations & place value", "lesson": "\n<p><b>Why this course exists.</b> Nobody is born knowing this, and if nobody ever\nsat down and taught it to you properly, that is a gap in your <i>schooling</i> —\nnot in you. We start from zero and climb one rung at a time. Every single skill\nhere shows up on the FAA General written (the ACS calls this subject\n<b>AM.I.H — Mathematics</b>), so nothing in this course is filler.</p>\n<p><b>The Big Idea.</b> Whole numbers are the counting numbers: 0, 1, 2, 3…\nThe four operations each have a NAME for their answer, and the FAA loves asking\nfor those names:</p>\n<table>\n<tr><th>Operation</th><th>The answer is called</th><th>Example</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Addition (+)</td><td><b>Sum</b></td><td>4 + 3 = 7</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Subtraction (−)</td><td><b>Difference</b></td><td>9 − 4 = 5</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Multiplication (×)</td><td><b>Product</b></td><td>4 × 3 = 12</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Division (÷)</td><td><b>Quotient</b></td><td>12 ÷ 3 = 4</td></tr>\n</table>\n<p><b>Analogy.</b> Multiplication is just <b>repeated addition</b> wearing a fast\nsuit: 4 × 3 means \"add three 4s\" (4 + 4 + 4 = 12). Division is the reverse\nquestion: \"how many 3s fit inside 12?\" In <b>Dividend ÷ Divisor = Quotient</b>,\nthe dividend is the number being divided up, the divisor does the dividing.</p>\n<h3>Place value — the address of every digit</h3>\n<p>Reading a big number is like reading a street address. From the right:\n<b>ones, tens, hundreds, thousands, ten-thousands</b>. In 35,741 the 5 lives at\nthe thousands address, so it is worth 5,000.</p>\n<div class=\"box ex\"><span class=\"bt\">Worked example — shop stock</span>\n<ol class=\"ladder\"><li><span class=\"step\">1 · Write what you know (with units)</span>12 cases of rivets · 144 rivets per case.</li><li><span class=\"step\">2 · Pick the method — say it in English</span>Total = number of groups × size of each group — that is multiplication.</li><li><span class=\"step\">3 · Set it up</span>12 × 144</li><li><span class=\"step\">4 · One operation per line</span>12 × 100 = 1,200<br>12 × 44 = 528<br>1,200 + 528 = <b>1,728 rivets</b></li><li><span class=\"step\">5 · Calculator keystrokes</span><span class=\"keys\">12 × 144 = 1728</span></li><li><span class=\"step\">6 · Sanity check</span>Ten cases would be 1,440 — twelve cases should be a bit more. 1,728 fits.</li></ol>\n</div>\n<div class=\"box key\"><span class=\"bt\">Must-Know</span>\n<ul>\n<li>Answers by name: add → <b>sum</b>, subtract → <b>difference</b>, multiply → <b>product</b>, divide → <b>quotient</b>.</li>\n<li><b>Dividend ÷ Divisor = Quotient.</b></li>\n<li>Multiplication = repeated addition.</li>\n<li>Place values from the right: ones, tens, hundreds, thousands, ten-thousands.</li>\n</ul></div>\n<p class=\"cite\">Source: FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, pp. 3-1 to 3-2 (Whole Numbers) · ACS AM.I.H.K13.</p>\n", "quiz": [{"q": "In an addition problem, the answer is called the:", "options": ["Quotient", "Sum", "Product"], "correct": 1, "short": "Sum", "expl": "Know: addition combines values. Method: name the parts. Add -> sum, subtract -> difference, multiply -> product, divide -> quotient. Sanity: 'sum' and 'add' both mean putting together.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-1 (Addition of Whole Numbers) · AM.I.H.K13", "topic": "Whole numbers"}, {"q": "In the problem 97,588 − 3,461, the answer is called the:", "options": ["Remainder factor", "Difference", "Dividend"], "correct": 1, "short": "Difference", "expl": "Know: subtraction takes one value from another. Method: name the parts — the answer to a subtraction is the difference. Calc: 97,588 − 3,461 = 94,127. Sanity: the answer is smaller than what you started with.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-1 (Subtraction of Whole Numbers) · AM.I.H.K13", "topic": "Whole numbers"}, {"q": "Multiplication is best described as the process of:", "options": ["Inverting and adding", "Repeated subtraction", "Repeated addition"], "correct": 2, "short": "Repeated addition", "expl": "Know: 4 × 3 means three 4s. Method: 4 + 4 + 4 = 12, same answer as 4 × 3. The result is called the product. Sanity: multiplying by 3 tripled the number.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-1 (Multiplication of Whole Numbers) · AM.I.H.K13", "topic": "Whole numbers"}, {"q": "In the division statement Dividend ÷ Divisor = ?, the answer is called the:", "options": ["Numerator", "Quotient", "Product"], "correct": 1, "short": "Quotient", "expl": "Know: division finds how many times one number fits into another. Method: Dividend ÷ Divisor = Quotient. Sanity: 12 ÷ 3 = 4 — the 4 is the quotient.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-1 to 3-2 (Division of Whole Numbers) · AM.I.H.K13", "topic": "Whole numbers"}, {"q": "A hangar stocks 12 cases of rivets with 144 rivets per case. How many rivets are on hand?", "options": ["1,278 rivets", "1,728 rivets", "1,872 rivets"], "correct": 1, "short": "1,728 rivets", "expl": "Know: 12 cases, 144 per case. Method: total = groups × per-group. Setup: 12 × 144. Calc: 12 × 100 = 1,200; 12 × 44 = 528; 1,200 + 528 = 1,728. Keys: 12 × 144 = . Sanity: a bit more than 10 cases (1,440) — yes.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-1 (Multiplication of Whole Numbers) · AM.I.H.K13", "topic": "Whole numbers"}, {"q": "An aircraft flies 1,250 miles and burns 250 gallons of fuel. Its fuel mileage is:", "options": ["5 miles per gallon", "6 miles per gallon", "4 miles per gallon"], "correct": 0, "short": "5 miles per gallon", "expl": "Know: 1,250 miles, 250 gallons. Method: miles per gallon = miles ÷ gallons. Setup: 1,250 ÷ 250. Calc: 1,250 ÷ 250 = 5. Keys: 1250 ÷ 250 = . Sanity: 250 × 5 = 1,250 — checks.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-1 to 3-2 (Division of Whole Numbers) · AM.I.H.K13", "topic": "Whole numbers"}, {"q": "In the number 35,741, the digit 5 sits in which place?", "options": ["Ten thousands", "Hundreds", "Thousands"], "correct": 2, "short": "Thousands", "expl": "Know: places from the right are ones, tens, hundreds, thousands, ten-thousands. Method: count from the right: 1 ones, 4 tens, 7 hundreds, 5 thousands. Sanity: the 3 is ten-thousands, so the 5 (one step right) is thousands.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-2, Figure 3-1 (Place Values) · AM.I.H.K13", "topic": "Whole numbers"}, {"q": "A torque wrench check uses three readings: 48, 51, and 45 ft-lb. Their sum is:", "options": ["144 ft-lb", "154 ft-lb", "134 ft-lb"], "correct": 0, "short": "144 ft-lb", "expl": "Know: three whole numbers. Method: line up the columns and add. Calc: 48 + 51 = 99; 99 + 45 = 144. Keys: 48 + 51 + 45 = . Sanity: three numbers near 50 should total near 150 — 144 fits.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-1 (Addition of Whole Numbers) · AM.I.H.K13", "topic": "Whole numbers"}]}, {"id": "mau1s2", "title": "PEMDAS — the order of operations", "lesson": "\n<p><b>The Big Idea.</b> When a problem mixes +, −, ×, ÷, exponents, and\nparentheses, everyone must do the steps in the SAME order or we all get\ndifferent answers from the same numbers. That agreed order is\n<b>PEMDAS</b>: <b>P</b>arentheses, <b>E</b>xponents, <b>M</b>ultiplication and\n<b>D</b>ivision, <b>A</b>ddition and <b>S</b>ubtraction —\n<i>\"Please Excuse My Dear Aunt Sally.\"</i></p>\n<p><b>Analogy.</b> PEMDAS is the traffic law of arithmetic. Every driver at the\nintersection knows who goes first, so nobody crashes. Ignore the right-of-way\nand you crash even though your driving (each little calculation) was perfect —\nthat is exactly the risk the ACS flags as <b>AM.I.H.R1</b>.</p>\n<h3>The two traps</h3>\n<p><b>Trap 1 — reading order.</b> 6 + 2 × 5 is NOT 40. Multiplication outranks\naddition: 2 × 5 = 10 first, then 6 + 10 = <b>16</b>.</p>\n<p><b>Trap 2 — splitting M and D (or A and S).</b> Multiplication and division are\nEQUAL rank — you sweep <b>left to right in one pass</b>, doing whichever comes\nnext. Same for addition and subtraction. So 8 ÷ 2 × 4 = 4 × 4 = 16, not 1.</p>\n<h3>Parentheses are the boss</h3>\n<p>Parentheses override everything: (6 + 2) × 5 = 8 × 5 = <b>40</b>. Same numbers\nas Trap 1, different answer — the parentheses are load-bearing. Roots count as\nexponents (a square root is just the ½ power — more in Unit 7).</p>\n<div class=\"box ex\"><span class=\"bt\">Worked example</span>\n<ol class=\"ladder\"><li><span class=\"step\">1 · Write what you know (with units)</span>Solve N = 2 × (3 + 5) − 6 ÷ 3. No units — pure arithmetic.</li><li><span class=\"step\">2 · Pick the method — say it in English</span>March through PEMDAS: Parentheses, then ×/÷ left-to-right, then −.</li><li><span class=\"step\">3 · Set it up</span>N = 2 × (3 + 5) − 6 ÷ 3</li><li><span class=\"step\">4 · One operation per line</span>(3 + 5) = 8<br>2 × 8 = 16<br>6 ÷ 3 = 2<br>16 − 2 = <b>14</b></li><li><span class=\"step\">5 · Calculator keystrokes</span><span class=\"keys\">3 + 5 = 8 · × 2 = 16 · 6 ÷ 3 = 2 · 16 − 2 = 14</span></li><li><span class=\"step\">6 · Sanity check</span>Both multiplication-type jobs got done before the final subtraction — order held.</li></ol>\n</div>\n<div class=\"box key\"><span class=\"bt\">Must-Know</span>\n<ul>\n<li><b>PEMDAS</b> — Parentheses, Exponents, Multiply/Divide, Add/Subtract.</li>\n<li>×/÷ are equal rank; so are +/−. Sweep <b>left to right in one pass</b>.</li>\n<li>Parentheses first, innermost set first. Roots behave like exponents.</li>\n<li>6 + 2 × 5 = <b>16</b>, but (6 + 2) × 5 = <b>40</b>.</li>\n</ul></div>\n<p class=\"cite\">Source: FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, pp. 3-17 to 3-18 (Order of Operation, Use of Parentheses) · ACS AM.I.H.R1.</p>\n", "quiz": [{"q": "In the order of operations (PEMDAS), which is always evaluated FIRST?", "options": ["Exponents", "Operations inside parentheses", "Multiplication and division"], "correct": 1, "short": "Operations inside parentheses", "expl": "Know: PEMDAS = Parentheses, Exponents, Multiply/Divide, Add/Subtract ('Please Excuse My Dear Aunt Sally'). Method: parentheses always come first, starting with the innermost set. Sanity: the P is first in the acronym.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-17 to 3-18 (Order of Operation) · AM.I.H.R1", "topic": "Order of operations"}, {"q": "Under PEMDAS, multiplication and division are worked:", "options": ["After addition and subtraction", "All multiplication first, then all division", "Left to right in one sweep, together"], "correct": 2, "short": "Left to right in one sweep, together", "expl": "Know: M and D are equal partners; so are A and S. Method: sweep left to right doing whichever of ×/÷ appears next — NOT all × first. Sanity: 8 ÷ 2 × 4 = 16 (left to right), not 1.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-18 (Order of Operation) · AM.I.H.R1", "topic": "Order of operations"}, {"q": "Solve: 6 + 2 × 5 =", "options": ["40", "16", "22"], "correct": 1, "short": "16", "expl": "Know: mixed + and ×. Method: PEMDAS — multiply before adding. Calc: 2 × 5 = 10; then 6 + 10 = 16. Keys: 2 × 5 = 10, + 6 = 16. Sanity: 40 is the left-to-right trap (6+2 first) — PEMDAS forbids it.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-17 to 3-18 (Order of Operation) · AM.I.H.R1", "topic": "Order of operations"}, {"q": "Solve: (6 + 2) × 5 =", "options": ["40", "16", "13"], "correct": 0, "short": "40", "expl": "Know: same numbers, but now parentheses. Method: parentheses first. Calc: 6 + 2 = 8; then 8 × 5 = 40. Keys: 6 + 2 = 8, × 5 = 40. Sanity: parentheses changed the answer from 16 to 40 — that is why they matter.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-17 (Use of Parentheses) · AM.I.H.R1", "topic": "Order of operations"}, {"q": "Solve: 20 − 12 ÷ 4 =", "options": ["2", "8", "17"], "correct": 2, "short": "17", "expl": "Know: mixed − and ÷. Method: PEMDAS — divide before subtracting. Calc: 12 ÷ 4 = 3; then 20 − 3 = 17. Keys: 12 ÷ 4 = 3; 20 − 3 = 17. Sanity: 2 is the trap (20−12 first).", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-18 (Order of Operation) · AM.I.H.R1", "topic": "Order of operations"}, {"q": "Solve: 4 + 3² × 2 =", "options": ["22", "98", "26"], "correct": 0, "short": "22", "expl": "Know: exponent, ×, +. Method: PEMDAS — exponent, then multiply, then add. Calc: 3² = 9; 9 × 2 = 18; 4 + 18 = 22. Keys: 3 x² × 2 = 18, + 4 = 22. Sanity: 98 comes from adding first ((4+3)²×2) — wrong order.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-17 to 3-18 (Order of Operation) · AM.I.H.R1", "topic": "Order of operations"}, {"q": "The main risk of ignoring the precedence (order) of operations when solving an equation is:", "options": ["The answer comes out with the wrong units", "Getting a wrong answer even though every individual calculation was done correctly", "The calculation takes longer than necessary"], "correct": 1, "short": "Getting a wrong answer even though every individual calculation was done correctly", "expl": "Know: PEMDAS exists because the ORDER changes the result. Method: 6 + 2 × 5 is 16 done right, 40 done in reading order — same numbers, different answers. Sanity: that is exactly the error the ACS risk element AM.I.H.R1 targets.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-17 to 3-18 (Order of Operation) · AM.I.H.R1", "topic": "Order of operations"}, {"q": "Solve: 2 × (3 + 5) − 6 ÷ 3 =", "options": ["10", "8", "14"], "correct": 2, "short": "14", "expl": "Know: parentheses, ×, ÷, −. Method: P first, then ×/÷ left-to-right, then −. Calc: (3+5) = 8; 2 × 8 = 16; 6 ÷ 3 = 2; 16 − 2 = 14. Keys: 3 + 5 = 8, × 2 = 16; 6 ÷ 3 = 2; 16 − 2 = 14. Sanity: both products done before the final subtraction.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-17 to 3-18 (Order of Operation) · AM.I.H.R1", "topic": "Order of operations"}]}]}, {"n": 2, "day": "Unit 2", "title": "Fractions — Pies, Rulers & the LCD", "ref": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3 pp. 3-2 to 3-4 · AM.I.H.K13", "sections": [{"id": "mau2s1", "title": "What a fraction is · adding & subtracting with the LCD", "lesson": "\n<p><b>The Big Idea.</b> A fraction is just a <b>pie order</b>. Written N/D, the\nbottom number (<b>denominator</b>) says how many equal slices the pie was cut\ninto; the top number (<b>numerator</b>) says how many slices are yours.\n3/4 = pie cut in 4, you get 3.</p>\n\n<figure class=\"fig\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 340 120\" width=\"340\" height=\"120\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Pie diagrams for fractions\">\n<g transform=\"translate(60,60)\">\n<circle r=\"48\" fill=\"#f3f7fc\" stroke=\"#15436e\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n<path d=\"M0,0 L48,0 A48,48 0 0,0 0,-48 Z\" fill=\"#ffce4d\" stroke=\"#15436e\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n<line x1=\"0\" y1=\"0\" x2=\"-48\" y2=\"0\" stroke=\"#15436e\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n<line x1=\"0\" y1=\"0\" x2=\"0\" y2=\"48\" stroke=\"#15436e\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n</g>\n<text x=\"60\" y=\"118\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" fill=\"#1c2330\">1/4 of the pie</text>\n<g transform=\"translate(230,60)\">\n<circle r=\"48\" fill=\"#f3f7fc\" stroke=\"#15436e\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n<path d=\"M0,0 L48,0 A48,48 0 1,0 0,48 Z\" fill=\"#ffce4d\" stroke=\"#15436e\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n</g>\n<text x=\"230\" y=\"118\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" fill=\"#1c2330\">3/4 of the pie</text>\n</svg>\n<figcaption>The bottom number cuts the pie; the top number counts your slices.</figcaption></figure>\n\n<p><b>Vocabulary you'll be tested on.</b> Top ≥ bottom (like 15/8) =\n<b>improper fraction</b> — more than one whole pie. A whole number plus a\nfraction (1 7/8) = <b>mixed number</b>. Same amount of pie, two spellings.</p>\n\n<figure class=\"fig\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 360 90\" width=\"360\" height=\"90\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"One inch of a ruler divided into sixteenths\">\n<rect x=\"20\" y=\"20\" width=\"320\" height=\"34\" fill=\"#fffbe9\" stroke=\"#15436e\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n<line x1=\"20\" y1=\"20\" x2=\"20\" y2=\"54\" stroke=\"#15436e\" stroke-width=\"3\"/>\n<line x1=\"340\" y1=\"20\" x2=\"340\" y2=\"54\" stroke=\"#15436e\" stroke-width=\"3\"/>\n<line x1=\"180\" y1=\"20\" x2=\"180\" y2=\"46\" stroke=\"#15436e\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/>\n<line x1=\"100\" y1=\"20\" x2=\"100\" y2=\"41\" stroke=\"#15436e\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n<line x1=\"260\" y1=\"20\" x2=\"260\" y2=\"41\" stroke=\"#15436e\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n<line x1=\"60\" y1=\"20\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"36\" stroke=\"#5a6678\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n<line x1=\"140\" y1=\"20\" x2=\"140\" y2=\"36\" stroke=\"#5a6678\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n<line x1=\"220\" y1=\"20\" x2=\"220\" y2=\"36\" stroke=\"#5a6678\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n<line x1=\"300\" y1=\"20\" x2=\"300\" y2=\"36\" stroke=\"#5a6678\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n<line x1=\"40\" y1=\"20\" x2=\"40\" y2=\"30\" stroke=\"#9aa6b8\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><line x1=\"80\" y1=\"20\" x2=\"80\" y2=\"30\" stroke=\"#9aa6b8\" stroke-width=\"1\"/>\n<line x1=\"120\" y1=\"20\" x2=\"120\" y2=\"30\" stroke=\"#9aa6b8\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><line x1=\"160\" y1=\"20\" x2=\"160\" y2=\"30\" stroke=\"#9aa6b8\" stroke-width=\"1\"/>\n<line x1=\"200\" y1=\"20\" x2=\"200\" y2=\"30\" stroke=\"#9aa6b8\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><line x1=\"240\" y1=\"20\" x2=\"240\" y2=\"30\" stroke=\"#9aa6b8\" stroke-width=\"1\"/>\n<line x1=\"280\" y1=\"20\" x2=\"280\" y2=\"30\" stroke=\"#9aa6b8\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><line x1=\"320\" y1=\"20\" x2=\"320\" y2=\"30\" stroke=\"#9aa6b8\" stroke-width=\"1\"/>\n<text x=\"20\" y=\"70\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"12\" fill=\"#1c2330\">0</text>\n<text x=\"180\" y=\"70\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"12\" fill=\"#1c2330\">1/2</text>\n<text x=\"100\" y=\"70\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"12\" fill=\"#1c2330\">1/4</text>\n<text x=\"260\" y=\"70\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"12\" fill=\"#1c2330\">3/4</text>\n<text x=\"340\" y=\"70\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"12\" fill=\"#1c2330\">1 in</text>\n<circle cx=\"300\" cy=\"20\" r=\"4\" fill=\"#d6453d\"/>\n<text x=\"300\" y=\"12\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#d6453d\" font-weight=\"bold\">15/16</text>\n</svg>\n<figcaption>One inch cut into 16ths — the tick at 15/16 is one small step short of the full inch. Your ruler is a fraction chart you already own.</figcaption></figure>\n\n<h3>The one rule of adding and subtracting: same-size slices</h3>\n<p>You can't add quarter-slices to tenth-slices — they're different sizes.\nFirst re-cut both pies into the same size slice: the <b>Least Common\nDenominator (LCD)</b>, the smallest number both denominators divide into\nevenly. List each denominator's multiples and take the first match\n(5: 5, 10, 15… and 10: 10, 20… → LCD is 10). Multiplying the denominators\ntogether always gives <i>a</i> common denominator, just not always the\n<i>least</i> one — you'll just have to reduce at the end.</p>\n<p><b>Reducing:</b> divide top and bottom by the same number until nothing\ndivides both. 6/16 → ÷2 → <b>3/8</b>. Same pie, simplest spelling.</p>\n<div class=\"box ex\"><span class=\"bt\">Worked example — the panel (handbook's own)</span>\n<ol class=\"ladder\"><li><span class=\"step\">1 · Write what you know (with units)</span>Aluminum sheet 3/32 in thick + paint coat 1/64 in thick. Want: total thickness.</li><li><span class=\"step\">2 · Pick the method — say it in English</span>Adding fractions → same-size slices first. LCD of 32 and 64 is 64.</li><li><span class=\"step\">3 · Set it up</span>3/32 + 1/64 → convert 3/32 to 64ths</li><li><span class=\"step\">4 · One operation per line</span>3/32 × 2/2 = 6/64<br>6/64 + 1/64 = <b>7/64 inch</b></li><li><span class=\"step\">5 · Calculator keystrokes</span><span class=\"keys\">3 ÷ 32 + 1 ÷ 64 = 0.109  (7 ÷ 64 = 0.109 ✓)</span></li><li><span class=\"step\">6 · Sanity check</span>Paint is thin, so the total should be a hair over the bare 3/32 (= 6/64). It is.</li></ol>\n</div>\n<div class=\"box key\"><span class=\"bt\">Must-Know</span>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Denominator</b> = slices in the whole pie; <b>numerator</b> = your slices.</li>\n<li>Add/subtract needs the <b>LCD</b> — never add straight across.</li>\n<li>Improper fraction: top ≥ bottom. Mixed number: whole + fraction.</li>\n<li>Reduce by dividing top and bottom by the same factor.</li>\n</ul></div>\n<p class=\"cite\">Source: FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, pp. 3-2 to 3-3 (Fractions, LCD, Addition/Subtraction, Reducing) · ACS AM.I.H.K13.</p>\n", "quiz": [{"q": "In a fraction, the DENOMINATOR (bottom number) represents:", "options": ["The number of equal parts that make up the whole", "The number of parts being counted", "The decimal equivalent of the fraction"], "correct": 0, "short": "The number of equal parts that make up the whole", "expl": "Know: a fraction is N/D. Method: think pie — the bottom says how many slices the pie was cut into; the top says how many slices you have. Sanity: in 3/4, the pie has 4 slices and you hold 3.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-2 (Fractions) · AM.I.H.K13", "topic": "Fractions"}, {"q": "A fraction whose numerator is larger than its denominator (such as 15/8) is called:", "options": ["A mixed number", "A reduced fraction", "An improper fraction"], "correct": 2, "short": "An improper fraction", "expl": "Know: top bigger than bottom = more than one whole. Method: that is the definition of an improper fraction; written as 1 7/8 it becomes a mixed number. Sanity: 15/8 is nearly 2 — more than one whole pie.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-2 (Fractions) · AM.I.H.K13", "topic": "Fractions"}, {"q": "To add 1/5 + 1/10, the least common denominator (LCD) is:", "options": ["5", "50", "10"], "correct": 2, "short": "10", "expl": "Know: denominators 5 and 10. Method: list multiples — 5: 5,10,15…; 10: 10,20… Setup: smallest shared multiple = 10. Sanity: 50 works but is not the LEAST; the LCD keeps the answer in simplest form.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-2 (Finding the Least Common Denominator) · AM.I.H.K13", "topic": "Fractions"}, {"q": "Solve: 1/5 + 1/10 =", "options": ["2/15", "3/10", "2/5"], "correct": 1, "short": "3/10", "expl": "Know: unlike denominators. Method: convert to the LCD (10). Setup: 1/5 = 2/10. Calc: 2/10 + 1/10 = 3/10. Sanity: 2/15 is the trap of adding tops and bottoms straight across — never do that for + or −.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-2 (Addition of Fractions) · AM.I.H.K13", "topic": "Fractions"}, {"q": "Multiplying all the denominators together when adding fractions will always produce:", "options": ["A common denominator, but not necessarily the least common denominator", "The least common denominator every time", "The numerator of the answer"], "correct": 0, "short": "A common denominator, but not necessarily the least common denominator", "expl": "Know: 4 and 6 multiplied give 24, but the LCD is 12. Method: multiplying denominators always works as a common denominator; it just may force extra reducing at the end. Sanity: both 12 and 24 hold 1/4 and 1/6 evenly — 12 is smaller.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-2 (Finding the Least Common Denominator) · AM.I.H.K13", "topic": "Fractions"}, {"q": "A panel is made of 3/32-inch aluminum with a 1/64-inch paint coating. The total thickness is:", "options": ["7/64 inch", "5/64 inch", "1/8 inch"], "correct": 0, "short": "7/64 inch", "expl": "Know: 3/32 in metal + 1/64 in paint. Method: convert to 64ths (the LCD). Setup: 3/32 = 6/64. Calc: 6/64 + 1/64 = 7/64 inch. Keys: 3 ÷ 32 + 1 ÷ 64 = 0.109 ≈ 7/64. Sanity: slightly thicker than the bare metal — as paint should be.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-2 (Addition of Fractions — panel example) · AM.I.H.K13", "topic": "Fractions"}, {"q": "The total travel of a jackscrew is 13/16 inch. If travel in one direction from neutral is 7/16 inch, travel in the opposite direction is:", "options": ["3/8 inch", "5/16 inch", "7/16 inch"], "correct": 0, "short": "3/8 inch", "expl": "Know: total 13/16, one side 7/16. Method: subtract, then reduce. Calc: 13/16 − 7/16 = 6/16; 6 ÷ 2 = 3 and 16 ÷ 2 = 8, so 3/8. Sanity: 7/16 + 6/16 = 13/16 — the two sides rebuild the total.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-3 (Subtraction of Fractions / Reducing Fractions — jackscrew example) · AM.I.H.K13", "topic": "Fractions"}, {"q": "The fraction 6/16 reduced to lowest terms is:", "options": ["6/16 (already in lowest terms)", "3/8", "2/3"], "correct": 1, "short": "3/8", "expl": "Know: 6 and 16 share a factor. Method: divide top and bottom by the same number. Calc: 6 ÷ 2 = 3; 16 ÷ 2 = 8 → 3/8. Sanity: 3 and 8 share no common factor, so it is fully reduced.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-3 (Reducing Fractions) · AM.I.H.K13", "topic": "Fractions"}]}, {"id": "mau2s2", "title": "Multiplying, dividing & mixed numbers", "lesson": "\n<p><b>The Big Idea.</b> Multiplying and dividing fractions is EASIER than adding\nthem — no LCD needed at all.</p>\n<h3>Multiply: straight across</h3>\n<p>Tops together, bottoms together. 3/4 × 2/3 = 6/12 = <b>1/2</b>.\n<b>Sanity habit:</b> multiplying by a fraction smaller than 1 makes things\nSMALLER — \"three-quarters of two-thirds\" is less than either.</p>\n<h3>Divide: Keep–Change–Flip</h3>\n<p><b>KEEP</b> the first fraction, <b>CHANGE</b> ÷ to ×, <b>FLIP</b> the second.\n7/8 ÷ 1/4 = 7/8 × 4/1 = 28/8 = <b>3 1/2</b>. <b>Analogy:</b> \"how many\nquarter-cups fit in 7/8 of a cup?\" Quarter-cups are small, so the answer is\nbigger than 1 — dividing by a small fraction gives a BIG answer.</p>\n<h3>Mixed numbers: add the wholes, add the parts</h3>\n<p>6 3/4 + 4 1/2 → wholes: 6 + 4 = 10; parts: 3/4 + 2/4 = 5/4 = 1 1/4;\ntotal <b>11 1/4</b>. If the parts overflow past 1, carry it into the wholes —\nexactly like carrying in ordinary addition.</p>\n<p><b>Subtracting mixed numbers — borrowing.</b> When the top fraction is too\nsmall, borrow 1 whole and break it into slices, just like borrowing a ten in\ngrade-school subtraction.</p>\n<div class=\"box ex\"><span class=\"bt\">Worked example — bolt grip (handbook's own)</span>\n<ol class=\"ladder\"><li><span class=\"step\">1 · Write what you know (with units)</span>Bolt shank 3 1/8 in · threaded portion 15/16 in. Want: grip = shank − threads.</li><li><span class=\"step\">2 · Pick the method — say it in English</span>Subtract mixed numbers → same slices (16ths), borrow if needed.</li><li><span class=\"step\">3 · Set it up</span>3 1/8 − 15/16 → 3 2/16 − 15/16</li><li><span class=\"step\">4 · One operation per line</span>3 2/16 = 2 18/16 (borrowed one whole = 16/16)<br>2 18/16 − 15/16 = <b>2 3/16 inches</b></li><li><span class=\"step\">5 · Calculator keystrokes</span><span class=\"keys\">3.125 − 0.9375 = 2.1875  (= 2 3/16 ✓)</span></li><li><span class=\"step\">6 · Sanity check</span>The threads are almost an inch, so the grip should be about an inch less than 3 1/8. It is.</li></ol>\n</div>\n<div class=\"box key\"><span class=\"bt\">Must-Know</span>\n<ul>\n<li>Multiply: <b>straight across</b>, then reduce. No LCD.</li>\n<li>Divide: <b>Keep–Change–Flip</b>, then multiply.</li>\n<li>Dividing by a fraction under 1 makes the answer <b>bigger</b>.</li>\n<li>Mixed subtraction: convert to same slices, <b>borrow a whole</b> as needed.</li>\n<li>Mixed → improper: wholes × denominator + numerator (2 5/8 = 21/8).</li>\n</ul></div>\n<p class=\"cite\">Source: FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, pp. 3-3 to 3-4 (Multiplication/Division of Fractions, Mixed Numbers) · ACS AM.I.H.K13.</p>\n", "quiz": [{"q": "Which is the FIRST rule for multiplying two fractions?", "options": ["Find the least common denominator first", "Invert the second fraction, then add", "Multiply numerator × numerator and denominator × denominator — no common denominator needed"], "correct": 2, "short": "Multiply numerator × numerator and denominator × denominator — no common denominator needed", "expl": "Know: × is the easy one. Method: straight across — tops together, bottoms together. Sanity: the LCD is only for + and −; inverting is only for ÷.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-3 (Multiplication of Fractions) · AM.I.H.K13", "topic": "Fractions"}, {"q": "Solve: 3/4 × 2/3 =", "options": ["1/2", "5/7", "9/8"], "correct": 0, "short": "1/2", "expl": "Know: multiply straight across. Calc: 3 × 2 = 6; 4 × 3 = 12; 6/12 reduces to 1/2. Keys: 3 ÷ 4 × 2 ÷ 3 = 0.5. Sanity: multiplying by 2/3 makes a number SMALLER — 1/2 is smaller than 3/4. 5/7 is the add-straight-across trap.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-3 (Multiplication of Fractions) · AM.I.H.K13", "topic": "Fractions"}, {"q": "To divide one fraction by another, you:", "options": ["Invert (flip) the second fraction, then multiply", "Divide numerators and add denominators", "Invert the first fraction, then multiply"], "correct": 0, "short": "Invert (flip) the second fraction, then multiply", "expl": "Know: division by a fraction = multiplication by its flip. Method: keep-change-flip — KEEP the first, CHANGE ÷ to ×, FLIP the second. Sanity: 1/2 ÷ 1/4 = 1/2 × 4/1 = 2 — there are two quarters in a half.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-3 (Division of Fractions) · AM.I.H.K13", "topic": "Fractions"}, {"q": "Solve: 7/8 ÷ 1/4 =", "options": ["7/32", "3 1/2", "2 1/3"], "correct": 1, "short": "3 1/2", "expl": "Know: keep-change-flip. Setup: 7/8 × 4/1. Calc: 7 × 4 = 28; 8 × 1 = 8; 28/8 = 3 4/8 = 3 1/2. Keys: 7 ÷ 8 ÷ ( 1 ÷ 4 ) = 3.5. Sanity: quarters are small, so many of them fit into 7/8 — the answer must be bigger than 1. 7/32 is the forgot-to-flip trap.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-3 (Division of Fractions) · AM.I.H.K13", "topic": "Fractions"}, {"q": "A mixed number is:", "options": ["A whole number combined with a fraction, such as 3 1/2", "A fraction that has been reduced to lowest terms", "Any fraction whose numerator is larger than its denominator"], "correct": 0, "short": "A whole number combined with a fraction, such as 3 1/2", "expl": "Know: 'mixed' = whole part + fraction part. Sanity: 15/8 is IMPROPER; rewritten as 1 7/8 it becomes MIXED — same value, different clothes.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-3 (Mixed Numbers) · AM.I.H.K13", "topic": "Mixed numbers"}, {"q": "The cargo area of an aircraft is 6 3/4 feet long, and removing the rear seats adds 4 1/2 feet. The total usable length is:", "options": ["11 1/2 feet", "10 1/4 feet", "11 1/4 feet"], "correct": 2, "short": "11 1/4 feet", "expl": "Know: 6 3/4 + 4 1/2. Method: add wholes and fractions separately. Setup: 6 + 4 = 10; 3/4 + 2/4 = 5/4 = 1 1/4. Calc: 10 + 1 1/4 = 11 1/4 ft. Keys: 6.75 + 4.5 = 11.25. Sanity: a bit more than 6 + 4 = 10 — yes.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-3 to 3-4 (Addition of Mixed Numbers) · AM.I.H.K13", "topic": "Mixed numbers"}, {"q": "A bolt has a shank length of 3 1/8 inches and a threaded portion 15/16 inch long. The grip length is:", "options": ["2 3/16 inches", "2 9/16 inches", "1 13/16 inches"], "correct": 0, "short": "2 3/16 inches", "expl": "Know: grip = shank − threads. Setup: 3 1/8 − 15/16 → 16ths: 3 2/16 − 15/16. Method: borrow one whole: 3 2/16 = 2 18/16. Calc: 2 18/16 − 15/16 = 2 3/16 in. Keys: 3.125 − 0.9375 = 2.1875 = 2 3/16. Sanity: just under 3 1/8 by about an inch — reasonable.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-4 (Subtraction of Mixed Numbers — bolt grip example) · AM.I.H.K13", "topic": "Mixed numbers"}, {"q": "Expressed as an improper fraction, 2 5/8 is:", "options": ["10/8", "25/8", "21/8"], "correct": 2, "short": "21/8", "expl": "Know: 2 wholes + 5/8. Method: wholes × denominator + numerator, over the same denominator. Calc: 2 × 8 = 16; 16 + 5 = 21 → 21/8. Sanity: 21/8 ≈ 2.6 — matches 2 5/8. 25/8 is the digits-mashed trap.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-3 (Mixed Numbers) · AM.I.H.K13", "topic": "Mixed numbers"}]}]}, {"n": 3, "day": "Unit 3", "title": "Decimals, Rounding & Fraction ↔ Decimal", "ref": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3 pp. 3-4 to 3-7 · AM.I.H.K9, R3, S5", "sections": [{"id": "mau3s1", "title": "Decimal arithmetic (add, subtract, multiply, divide)", "lesson": "\n<p><b>The Big Idea.</b> The decimal system is Base-10: every place is worth ten\ntimes its right-hand neighbor. Left of the point: ones, tens, hundreds. Right of\nthe point: <b>tenths, hundredths, thousandths, ten-thousandths</b> — the language\nof every micrometer reading you will ever take.</p>\n<p><b>Analogy.</b> A decimal is money. 2.34 is 2 dollars, 3 dimes (tenths),\n4 pennies (hundredths). If you can count change, you can already do decimals.</p>\n<h3>Add / subtract: line up the points</h3>\n<p>Stack the numbers so the decimal points sit in one vertical line, then work\nnormally and bring the point straight down. That's it — the whole skill.</p>\n<div class=\"box ex\"><span class=\"bt\">Worked example — series resistance (handbook's own)</span>\n<ol class=\"ladder\"><li><span class=\"step\">1 · Write what you know (with units)</span>Resistors of 2.34 Ω, 37.5 Ω, and 0.09 Ω in series. Want: total resistance (series just adds).</li><li><span class=\"step\">2 · Pick the method — say it in English</span>Adding decimals → align the decimal points, add columns.</li><li><span class=\"step\">3 · Set it up</span>2.34 + 37.50 + 0.09</li><li><span class=\"step\">4 · One operation per line</span>2.34 + 37.50 = 39.84<br>39.84 + 0.09 = <b>39.93 Ω</b></li><li><span class=\"step\">5 · Calculator keystrokes</span><span class=\"keys\">2.34 + 37.5 + 0.09 = 39.93</span></li><li><span class=\"step\">6 · Sanity check</span>The 37.5 dominates; the answer should sit just under 40. It does.</li></ol>\n</div>\n<h3>Multiply: ignore the point, then count places</h3>\n<p>Multiply as if there were no decimal points, then give the answer as many\ndecimal places as BOTH factors had combined. 3.12 (2 places) × 0.4 (1 place):\n312 × 4 = 1248, place 3 → <b>1.248</b>.</p>\n<h3>Divide: make the divisor whole first</h3>\n<p>Hop the divisor's decimal point right until it's a whole number, and hop the\ndividend the SAME number of places (you're multiplying both by 10 — the\nanswer doesn't change). 262.6 ÷ 40.4 → 2,626 ÷ 404 = <b>6.5</b> — that's the\nhandbook's mean-chord example (wing area 262.6 sq ft ÷ span 40.4 ft).</p>\n<div class=\"box key\"><span class=\"bt\">Must-Know</span>\n<ul>\n<li>Add/subtract: <b>line up the decimal points</b>.</li>\n<li>Multiply: count total decimal places of both factors — the answer gets them all.</li>\n<li>Divide: hop BOTH points until the divisor is whole.</li>\n<li>Decimal = money: tenths are dimes, hundredths are pennies.</li>\n</ul></div>\n<p class=\"cite\">Source: FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, pp. 3-4 to 3-6 (The Decimal Number System) · ACS AM.I.H.K13.</p>\n", "quiz": [{"q": "The decimal number system is based on:", "options": ["Fractions with a denominator of 100 only", "Powers of ten (a Base-10 system)", "Powers of two (a Base-2 system)"], "correct": 1, "short": "Powers of ten (a Base-10 system)", "expl": "Know: 'decimal' comes from decem, Latin for ten. Method: each place is 10× its neighbor — ones, tens, hundreds left of the point; tenths, hundredths right of it. Sanity: Base-2 is the BINARY system.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-4 (Origin and Definition, The Decimal Number System) · AM.I.H.K13", "topic": "Decimals"}, {"q": "The FIRST step when adding or subtracting decimal numbers by hand is:", "options": ["Count the total decimal places in both numbers", "Move both decimal points to make whole numbers", "Line up the decimal points vertically"], "correct": 2, "short": "Line up the decimal points vertically", "expl": "Know: place values must sit over each other. Method: stack the numbers with the points aligned, then add/subtract normally, bringing the point straight down. Sanity: counting decimal places is the rule for MULTIPLYING, not adding.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-4 (Addition of Decimal Numbers) · AM.I.H.K13", "topic": "Decimals"}, {"q": "A series circuit has resistors of 2.34 ohms, 37.5 ohms, and 0.09 ohms. Total resistance is:", "options": ["40.23 ohms", "38.93 ohms", "39.93 ohms"], "correct": 2, "short": "39.93 ohms", "expl": "Know: series resistance adds. Method: align the decimal points and add. Calc: 2.34 + 37.5 = 39.84; 39.84 + 0.09 = 39.93 Ω. Keys: 2.34 + 37.5 + 0.09 = . Sanity: slightly under 40 — the big resistor dominates.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-4 (Addition of Decimal Numbers — circuit example) · AM.I.H.K13", "topic": "Decimals"}, {"q": "A bolt's overall length is 2.75 inches and its threaded portion is 1.25 inches. The unthreaded portion is:", "options": ["1.5 inches", "1.55 inches", "1.05 inches"], "correct": 0, "short": "1.5 inches", "expl": "Know: whole minus part. Setup: 2.75 − 1.25. Calc: 2.75 − 1.25 = 1.50 in. Keys: 2.75 − 1.25 = . Sanity: 1.25 + 1.5 = 2.75 — the parts rebuild the whole.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-4 to 3-5 (Subtraction of Decimal Numbers) · AM.I.H.K13", "topic": "Decimals"}, {"q": "Using watts = amperes × voltage, an electric drill pulling 9.45 amps from a 120-volt source uses:", "options": ["1,134 watts", "11,340 watts", "113.4 watts"], "correct": 0, "short": "1,134 watts", "expl": "Know: P = A × V; 9.45 A, 120 V. Method: multiply ignoring the point, then place it — 2 decimal places total. Calc: 945 × 120 = 113,400 → 1,134.00. Keys: 9.45 × 120 = . Sanity: about 9 × 120 ≈ 1,080 — 1,134 fits; the wrong answers are decimal-slip traps.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-5 (Multiplication of Decimal Numbers — drill example) · AM.I.H.K13", "topic": "Decimals"}, {"q": "When multiplying 3.12 × 0.4, how many decimal places belong in the answer, and what is it?", "options": ["Three places — 1.248", "Two places — 12.48", "One place — 1.2"], "correct": 0, "short": "Three places — 1.248", "expl": "Know: 3.12 has 2 places, 0.4 has 1. Method: multiply 312 × 4 = 1248, then count 2 + 1 = 3 places from the right. Calc: 1.248. Keys: 3.12 × .4 = . Sanity: 3 × 0.4 = 1.2 — the answer should sit near 1.2.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-5 (Multiplication of Decimal Numbers) · AM.I.H.K13", "topic": "Decimals"}, {"q": "The wing area of an aircraft is 262.6 square feet and its span is 40.4 feet. The mean chord (area ÷ span) is:", "options": ["5.6 feet", "65 feet", "6.5 feet"], "correct": 2, "short": "6.5 feet", "expl": "Know: chord = area ÷ span. Method: move the divisor's decimal to make it whole (40.4 → 404), move the dividend the same (2,626). Calc: 2,626 ÷ 404 = 6.5 ft. Keys: 262.6 ÷ 40.4 = . Sanity: 6.5 × 40 = 260 — matches the area.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-6 (Division of Decimal Numbers — mean chord example) · AM.I.H.K13", "topic": "Decimals"}, {"q": "When dividing by a decimal (e.g., 37.26 ÷ 2.7), the standard first move is to:", "options": ["Move the decimal point of BOTH numbers right until the divisor is a whole number", "Round both numbers to whole numbers first", "Move only the dividend's decimal point"], "correct": 0, "short": "Move the decimal point of BOTH numbers right until the divisor is a whole number", "expl": "Know: dividing by a whole number is easy. Method: 2.7 → 27 (one hop), so 37.26 → 372.6 (same hop). Calc: 372.6 ÷ 27 = 13.8. Sanity: moving BOTH the same amount keeps the answer unchanged — like scaling a fraction.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-6 (Division of Decimal Numbers) · AM.I.H.K13", "topic": "Decimals"}]}, {"id": "mau3s2", "title": "Rounding & converting fraction ↔ decimal", "lesson": "\n<p><b>The Big Idea.</b> Fractions and decimals are the same value in two\nlanguages, and an aircraft mechanic translates constantly: the drawing says\n15/16, the micrometer says 0.9375. This section is the phrasebook.</p>\n<h3>Rounding — the 5-or-more rule</h3>\n<p>Find the digit at the place you're rounding to; look ONE door to the right.\nThat neighbor 5 or bigger? Round up. 4 or smaller? Leave it. 2.1938 to the\nnearest tenth: tenths digit 1, neighbor 9 → <b>2.2</b>.\n<b>How much should you round?</b> Whatever the job's accuracy demands\n(ACS risk AM.I.H.R3): a fuel load can round to whole gallons; a turbine\nclearance measured in ten-thousandths must NOT be rounded to tenths.</p>\n<h3>Fraction → decimal: divide top by bottom</h3>\n<p>A fraction IS a division problem in a trench coat. 7/8 means 7 ÷ 8 =\n<b>0.875</b>. Some never end (2/3 = 0.666…) — just round them sensibly.</p>\n<h3>Decimal → fraction: multiply by the slice count</h3>\n<p>Shop trick for 64ths, 32nds, 16ths: multiply the decimal by the slice count\nand round. 0.3123 × 16 = 4.997 ≈ 5 → <b>5/16</b>. Or \"read\" the decimal aloud:\n0.375 = \"375 thousandths\" = 375/1000 = <b>3/8</b>.</p>\n<div class=\"box ex\"><span class=\"bt\">Worked example — drill-and-ream (handbook's own)</span>\n<ol class=\"ladder\"><li><span class=\"step\">1 · Write what you know (with units)</span>Finished reamed hole must be 0.763 in. Shop rule: drill 1/64 in undersized, then ream.</li><li><span class=\"step\">2 · Pick the method — say it in English</span>Turn the decimal into 64ths, then step down one 64th for the drill.</li><li><span class=\"step\">3 · Set it up</span>0.763 × 64 = finished size in 64ths</li><li><span class=\"step\">4 · One operation per line</span>0.763 × 64 = 48.8 ≈ 49 → hole = 49/64<br>49/64 − 1/64 = 48/64<br>48/64 = <b>3/4-inch drill</b></li><li><span class=\"step\">5 · Calculator keystrokes</span><span class=\"keys\">.763 × 64 = 48.8</span></li><li><span class=\"step\">6 · Sanity check</span>The drill (0.750) must be SMALLER than the finished hole (0.763) — it is, by 13 thousandths.</li></ol>\n</div>\n<div class=\"box key\"><span class=\"bt\">Must-Know</span>\n<ul>\n<li>Rounding: look one digit right — <b>5 or more rounds up</b>. Round only as much as the job allows.</li>\n<li>Fraction → decimal: <b>top ÷ bottom</b>. (7/8 = 0.875; 2/3 = 0.667.)</li>\n<li>Decimal → 16ths/32nds/64ths: <b>× the slice count</b>, round, reduce.</li>\n<li>Memorize the anchors: 1/16 = 0.0625 · 1/8 = 0.125 · 1/4 = 0.25 · 3/8 = 0.375 · 1/2 = 0.5 · 5/8 = 0.625 · 3/4 = 0.75 · 7/8 = 0.875.</li>\n</ul></div>\n<p class=\"cite\">Source: FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, pp. 3-6 to 3-7 (Rounding, Converting Decimals ↔ Fractions, Decimal Equivalent Chart) · ACS AM.I.H.K9, R3, S5.</p>\n", "quiz": [{"q": "An actuator shaft is 2.1938 inches in diameter. Rounded to the nearest tenth, that is:", "options": ["2.19 inches", "2.2 inches", "2.1 inches"], "correct": 1, "short": "2.2 inches", "expl": "Know: tenths digit is 1; next digit right is 9. Method: 5 or more rounds up. Calc: the 1 becomes 2 → 2.2 in. Sanity: 2.1938 is closer to 2.2 than to 2.1. 2.19 is rounded to hundredths, not tenths.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-6 (Rounding Off Decimal Numbers) · AM.I.H.K9, AM.I.H.R3", "topic": "Rounding"}, {"q": "Round 0.3625 to the nearest hundredth:", "options": ["0.363", "0.36", "0.37"], "correct": 1, "short": "0.36", "expl": "Know: hundredths digit is 6; the next digit is 2. Method: below 5 rounds DOWN — the 6 stays. Calc: 0.36. Sanity: 0.3625 sits closer to 0.36 than 0.37.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-6 (Rounding Off Decimal Numbers) · AM.I.H.K9, AM.I.H.R3", "topic": "Rounding"}, {"q": "What determines how many digits you should keep when rounding a maintenance calculation?", "options": ["Always round to two decimal places", "The degree of accuracy the job requires", "The size of the numbers being multiplied"], "correct": 1, "short": "The degree of accuracy the job requires", "expl": "Know: the handbook ties rounding to required accuracy. Method: turbine fits measured to ten-thousandths must NOT be rounded to tenths; fuel-load gallons can be. Sanity: rounding too early or too coarsely is the risk element AM.I.H.R3 warns about.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-6 (Rounding Off Decimal Numbers) · AM.I.H.K9, AM.I.H.R3", "topic": "Rounding"}, {"q": "To convert the fraction 7/8 to a decimal:", "options": ["Divide 7 by 8, giving 0.875", "Divide 8 by 7, giving 1.143", "Multiply 7 by 8 and shift the decimal, giving 0.56"], "correct": 0, "short": "Divide 7 by 8, giving 0.875", "expl": "Know: a fraction IS a division — top ÷ bottom. Setup: 7 ÷ 8. Calc: 0.875. Keys: 7 ÷ 8 = . Sanity: 7/8 is just under 1, and 0.875 is just under 1. Never flip it: 8 ÷ 7 gives more than 1.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-7 (Converting Fractions to Decimals) · AM.I.H.S5", "topic": "Fraction ↔ decimal"}, {"q": "Expressed as a decimal, the fraction 2/3 is:", "options": ["0.23", "0.66 repeating (0.666…)", "0.32"], "correct": 1, "short": "0.66 repeating (0.666…)", "expl": "Know: top ÷ bottom. Calc: 2 ÷ 3 = 0.6666… — the 6 repeats forever, so it is usually rounded (0.67, or 0.6667). Keys: 2 ÷ 3 = . Sanity: 2/3 is more than half, and 0.667 is more than 0.5.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-7 (Converting Fractions to Decimals) · AM.I.H.S5", "topic": "Fraction ↔ decimal"}, {"q": "The decimal 0.375 written as a fraction in lowest terms is:", "options": ["3/8", "3/75", "375/100"], "correct": 0, "short": "3/8", "expl": "Know: 'read' the decimal — 375 thousandths. Setup: 375/1000. Method: divide top and bottom by 125. Calc: 375 ÷ 125 = 3; 1000 ÷ 125 = 8 → 3/8. Sanity: 3 ÷ 8 = 0.375 — round trip confirms.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-6 (Converting Decimal Numbers to Fractions) · AM.I.H.S5", "topic": "Fraction ↔ decimal"}, {"q": "The width of a hex-head bolt measures 0.3123 inch. The best-fit socket, in 16ths, is:", "options": ["1/4 inch", "3/8 inch", "5/16 inch"], "correct": 2, "short": "5/16 inch", "expl": "Know: sockets come in 16ths. Method: multiply the decimal by 16 and round. Calc: 0.3123 × 16 = 4.997 ≈ 5 → 5/16 in. Keys: .3123 × 16 = . Sanity: 5 ÷ 16 = 0.3125, within half a thousandth of the measurement.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-6 to 3-7 (Converting Decimal Numbers to Fractions — socket example) · AM.I.H.S5", "topic": "Fraction ↔ decimal"}, {"q": "Holes are drilled 1/64 inch undersized, then reamed to final size. For a finished hole of 0.763 inch, the drill to use is:", "options": ["25/32 inch", "3/4 inch", "49/64 inch"], "correct": 1, "short": "3/4 inch", "expl": "Know: finished size in 64ths, then subtract 1/64. Calc: 0.763 × 64 = 48.8 ≈ 49 → finished = 49/64; drill = 49/64 − 1/64 = 48/64 = 3/4 in. Keys: .763 × 64 = . Sanity: the drill must be SMALLER than the finished hole — 3/4 (0.750) < 0.763. 49/64 is the forgot-to-subtract trap.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-7 (Converting Decimal Numbers to Fractions — drill/ream example) · AM.I.H.S5", "topic": "Fraction ↔ decimal"}, {"q": "A dimension calls for 0.0625 inch. As a fraction, that is:", "options": ["1/32 inch", "1/16 inch", "1/8 inch"], "correct": 1, "short": "1/16 inch", "expl": "Know: common shop equivalents. Method: multiply by 16: 0.0625 × 16 = 1 → 1/16. Keys: 1 ÷ 16 = 0.0625 confirms. Sanity: 1/8 = 0.125 (double) and 1/32 = 0.03125 (half) — only 1/16 lands on it.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-7 (Decimal Equivalent Chart, Figure 3-6) · AM.I.H.S5", "topic": "Fraction ↔ decimal"}]}]}, {"n": 4, "day": "Unit 4", "title": "Ratio & Proportion — Gears, Glide & Compression", "ref": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3 pp. 3-7 to 3-9 · AM.I.H.K4, K5, S6, S7", "sections": [{"id": "mau4s1", "title": "Ratios — the aviation five", "lesson": "\n<p><b>The Big Idea.</b> A <b>ratio</b> compares two quantities: 5:7, 5/7, or\n\"5 to 7\" — three spellings, one comparison. Aviation runs on ratios, and the\nFAA written pulls straight from this list:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Compression ratio</b> — cylinder volume at <b>bottom</b> dead center vs. volume at <b>top</b> dead center. A 10:1 engine squeezes the mixture into a tenth of its space. <i>(Big number first — the squeeze.)</i></li>\n<li><b>Aspect ratio</b> — wing span vs. mean chord (a long thin glider wing has a high one).</li>\n<li><b>Air-fuel ratio</b> — <b>weight</b> of air vs. weight of fuel (e.g., 14.3:1). Weight, not volume.</li>\n<li><b>Glide ratio</b> — forward distance vs. altitude lost. 10,560 ft forward for 1,000 ft down = 10.56:1.</li>\n<li><b>Gear ratio</b> — teeth vs. teeth. <b>Speed ratio</b> — the gear ratio FLIPPED, because fewer teeth spin faster.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><b>Analogy.</b> A ratio is a recipe: 2 cups flour to 1 cup water. Double both\nand the bread tastes the same — which is why 8:28 IS 2:7 (divide both sides by\n4). Reducing a ratio never changes the comparison, only the spelling.</p>\n<p><b>The \"…to 1\" trick.</b> Exams love ratios written with a 1 on the right.\nDivide both sides by the second number: 9:5 → 9 ÷ 5 = <b>1.8:1</b>. That's how\nevery compression ratio you'll ever quote gets written.</p>\n<div class=\"box ex\"><span class=\"bt\">Worked example — gear speed (handbook's own)</span>\n<ol class=\"ladder\"><li><span class=\"step\">1 · Write what you know (with units)</span>Pinion: 10 teeth. Spur: 40 teeth, turning 160 rpm. Want: pinion speed.</li><li><span class=\"step\">2 · Pick the method — say it in English</span>Teeth and speed trade places (inverse): teeth-pinion/teeth-spur = speed-SPUR/speed-PINION. Cross-multiply.</li><li><span class=\"step\">3 · Set it up</span>10/40 = 160/SP</li><li><span class=\"step\">4 · One operation per line</span>40 × 160 = 6,400<br>6,400 ÷ 10 = <b>640 rpm</b></li><li><span class=\"step\">5 · Calculator keystrokes</span><span class=\"keys\">40 × 160 ÷ 10 = 640</span></li><li><span class=\"step\">6 · Sanity check</span>The pinion is 4× smaller, so it must spin 4× faster: 4 × 160 = 640. ✓</li></ol>\n</div>\n<div class=\"box key\"><span class=\"bt\">Must-Know</span>\n<ul>\n<li>Ratio = comparison. Reduce it like a fraction; the comparison survives.</li>\n<li><b>Compression ratio = BDC volume : TDC volume.</b></li>\n<li><b>Speed ratio = gear ratio flipped</b> (fewer teeth → faster).</li>\n<li>\"…to 1\" form: divide both sides by the second number.</li>\n</ul></div>\n<p class=\"cite\">Source: FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, pp. 3-7 to 3-8 (Ratio, Aviation Applications) · ACS AM.I.H.K4, S6, S7.</p>\n", "quiz": [{"q": "A ratio is best defined as:", "options": ["A part of a whole expressed in hundredths", "A statement of equality between two fractions", "A comparison of two numbers or quantities"], "correct": 2, "short": "A comparison of two numbers or quantities", "expl": "Know: ratio = comparison (5:7, 5/7, or '5 to 7'). Sanity: equality between two ratios is a PROPORTION; hundredths is a PERCENTAGE.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-7 (Ratio) · AM.I.H.K4", "topic": "Ratio"}, {"q": "The compression ratio of a reciprocating engine is the ratio of:", "options": ["Intake pressure to exhaust pressure", "Bore diameter to stroke length", "Cylinder volume with the piston at the bottom of the stroke to the volume with the piston at the top"], "correct": 2, "short": "Cylinder volume with the piston at the bottom of the stroke to the volume with the piston at the top", "expl": "Know: compression ratio compares the cylinder's BIGGEST volume (piston at bottom dead center) to its SMALLEST (top dead center). Sanity: a 10:1 engine squeezes the mixture into one-tenth of its original space.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-7 (Ratio — Aviation Applications) · AM.I.H.K4, AM.I.H.S7", "topic": "Compression ratio"}, {"q": "A cylinder holds 84 cubic inches with the piston at bottom dead center and 12 cubic inches at top dead center. The compression ratio is:", "options": ["6:1", "7:1", "12:1"], "correct": 1, "short": "7:1", "expl": "Know: BDC volume 84, TDC volume 12. Method: CR = BDC ÷ TDC. Setup: 84/12. Calc: 84 ÷ 12 = 7 → 7:1. Keys: 84 ÷ 12 = . Sanity: 7 × 12 = 84 — checks.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-7 (Ratio — compression ratio) · AM.I.H.S7", "topic": "Compression ratio"}, {"q": "An aircraft descends 1,000 feet while traveling 10,560 feet forward without power. Its glide ratio is:", "options": ["10.56 to 1", "1 to 10.56", "9.56 to 1"], "correct": 0, "short": "10.56 to 1", "expl": "Know: glide ratio = forward distance : vertical drop. Setup: 10,560:1,000. Calc: divide both by 1,000 → 10.56:1. Keys: 10560 ÷ 1000 = . Sanity: gliders go far FORWARD per foot down, so the big number comes first.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-8 (Ratio — glide ratio example) · AM.I.H.K4", "topic": "Ratio"}, {"q": "A pinion gear has 8 teeth and the spur gear it meshes with has 28 teeth. In lowest terms the gear ratio is:", "options": ["2:7", "7:2", "8:28 (cannot be reduced)"], "correct": 0, "short": "2:7", "expl": "Know: gear ratio = teeth : teeth = 8:28. Method: divide both sides by the common factor 4. Calc: 8÷4 = 2, 28÷4 = 7 → 2:7. Sanity: 2×4=8 and 7×4=28 — same comparison, smaller numbers.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-8 (Ratio — gear ratio, Figure 3-7) · AM.I.H.K4, AM.I.H.S6", "topic": "Ratio"}, {"q": "If two gears have a gear (teeth) ratio of 2:9, their speed ratio is:", "options": ["4.5:1 in favor of the large gear", "9:2", "2:9"], "correct": 1, "short": "9:2", "expl": "Know: fewer teeth = spins faster. Method: speed ratio is the INVERSE of the gear ratio. Calc: 2:9 flips to 9:2. Sanity: the little 2-tooth-share gear must turn 4.5 times for one turn of the big one — the speed number for the small gear is the big one.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-8 (Ratio — speed ratio) · AM.I.H.K4", "topic": "Ratio"}, {"q": "A pinion gear with 10 teeth drives a spur gear with 40 teeth. The spur gear turns at 160 rpm. The pinion's speed is:", "options": ["400 rpm", "640 rpm", "40 rpm"], "correct": 1, "short": "640 rpm", "expl": "Know: teeth-pinion/teeth-spur = speed-spur/speed-pinion. Setup: 10/40 = 160/SP. Method: cross-multiply. Calc: 40 × 160 = 6,400; 6,400 ÷ 10 = 640 rpm. Keys: 40 × 160 ÷ 10 = . Sanity: small gear, 4× the teeth difference → 4 × 160 = 640.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-8 (Ratio — pinion/spur worked example) · AM.I.H.K4, AM.I.H.S6", "topic": "Ratio"}, {"q": "An airplane cruises at 200 knots and its maximum speed is 250 knots. The ratio of cruising speed to maximum speed is:", "options": ["4:5", "2:5", "5:4"], "correct": 0, "short": "4:5", "expl": "Know: order matters — cruise first. Setup: 200/250. Method: reduce by 50. Calc: 200÷50 = 4; 250÷50 = 5 → 4:5. Sanity: cruise is slower than max, so the first number must be smaller.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-8 (Ratio — cruising speed example) · AM.I.H.S6", "topic": "Ratio"}, {"q": "Expressed as a ratio with a denominator of 1, the ratio 9:5 becomes:", "options": ["1.8:1", "0.56:1", "1:1.8"], "correct": 0, "short": "1.8:1", "expl": "Know: divide both sides by the second number. Calc: 9 ÷ 5 = 1.8 → 1.8:1. Keys: 9 ÷ 5 = . Sanity: 9 is nearly double 5, and 1.8 is nearly double 1. (This is exactly how compression ratios get written.)", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-8 (Ratio — converting to a denominator of 1) · AM.I.H.S6", "topic": "Ratio"}]}, {"id": "mau4s2", "title": "Proportions — cross-multiply and conquer", "lesson": "\n<p><b>The Big Idea.</b> A <b>proportion</b> is two ratios holding hands:\n3/4 = 6/8, read \"3 is to 4 as 6 is to 8.\" It's the most useful tool in this\nwhole course, because almost every shop problem is \"I know the rate, find the\nmissing amount.\"</p>\n<p><b>Names the FAA tests:</b> in 3:4 = 6:8 the outside terms (3 and 8) are the\n<b>extremes</b>; the inside terms (4 and 6) are the <b>means</b>. In every true\nproportion, <b>product of extremes = product of means</b> (3×8 = 24 = 4×6).\nThat equality is WHY cross-multiplying works.</p>\n<h3>The diagonal rule (cross-multiply)</h3>\n<p>Three numbers known, one unknown: <b>multiply the two numbers on the diagonal,\ndivide by the third.</b> Draw the X and the answer falls out.</p>\n<div class=\"box ex\"><span class=\"bt\">Worked example — fuel burn (handbook's own)</span>\n<ol class=\"ladder\"><li><span class=\"step\">1 · Write what you know (with units)</span>300 miles used 24 gallons. Want: gallons for 750 miles.</li><li><span class=\"step\">2 · Pick the method — say it in English</span>Miles-to-gallons is a steady rate → set two equal ratios, keeping miles on top BOTH times.</li><li><span class=\"step\">3 · Set it up</span>300/24 = 750/G</li><li><span class=\"step\">4 · One operation per line</span>750 × 24 = 18,000  (the diagonal)<br>18,000 ÷ 300 = <b>60 gallons</b></li><li><span class=\"step\">5 · Calculator keystrokes</span><span class=\"keys\">750 × 24 ÷ 300 = 60</span></li><li><span class=\"step\">6 · Sanity check</span>750 is 2.5 × 300, so fuel should be 2.5 × 24 = 60. ✓</li></ol>\n</div>\n<p><b>The one way to blow it:</b> mixing the order. If miles ride on top of the\nleft ratio, miles must ride on top of the right ratio. Label both tops and both\nbottoms in English before touching the calculator.</p>\n<p><b>Where you'll use this:</b> drawing scales (1 in = 24 in), fuel/oil premix\n(50:1), speed-distance-time, converting units, gear problems, and the\npercentage method in the next unit — they are all this one move.</p>\n<div class=\"box key\"><span class=\"bt\">Must-Know</span>\n<ul>\n<li>Proportion = <b>statement of equality between two ratios</b>.</li>\n<li><b>Extremes</b> = outside terms, <b>means</b> = inside; their products are equal.</li>\n<li>Solve: <b>multiply the diagonal, divide by the third number.</b></li>\n<li>Keep the same quantity on top of both ratios — label in English first.</li>\n</ul></div>\n<p class=\"cite\">Source: FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, pp. 3-8 to 3-9 (Proportion, Extremes and Means, Solving Proportions) · ACS AM.I.H.K5.</p>\n", "quiz": [{"q": "A proportion is:", "options": ["A statement of equality between two or more ratios", "The decimal form of any ratio", "A comparison of two unlike quantities"], "correct": 0, "short": "A statement of equality between two or more ratios", "expl": "Know: 3/4 = 6/8, read '3 is to 4 as 6 is to 8.' Two equal ratios, one equals sign — that is a proportion. Sanity: one ratio alone is just a ratio.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-8 (Proportion) · AM.I.H.K5", "topic": "Proportion"}, {"q": "In the proportion 3:4 = 6:8, the EXTREMES are:", "options": ["3 and 6", "3 and 8", "4 and 6"], "correct": 1, "short": "3 and 8", "expl": "Know: extremes = the outside (first and last) terms; means = the inside terms. Rule: product of extremes = product of means. Calc: 3 × 8 = 24 and 4 × 6 = 24 — equal, as every true proportion must be.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-8 (Extremes and Means) · AM.I.H.K5", "topic": "Proportion"}, {"q": "In the proportion 65/80 = X/100, X equals:", "options": ["123.08", "52", "81.25"], "correct": 2, "short": "81.25", "expl": "Know: three knowns, one unknown. Method: multiply along the diagonal, divide by the third. Setup: X = (65 × 100) ÷ 80. Calc: 65 × 100 = 6,500; 6,500 ÷ 80 = 81.25. Keys: 65 × 100 ÷ 80 = . Sanity: 65/80 is about 81%, and X is 'per 100' — matches.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-8 (Solving Proportions) · AM.I.H.K5", "topic": "Proportion"}, {"q": "An airplane flying 300 miles used 24 gallons of gasoline. To fly 750 miles it needs:", "options": ["75 gallons", "60 gallons", "48 gallons"], "correct": 1, "short": "60 gallons", "expl": "Know: miles-to-gallons stays constant. Setup: 300/24 = 750/G. Method: cross-multiply. Calc: 750 × 24 = 18,000; 18,000 ÷ 300 = 60 gal. Keys: 750 × 24 ÷ 300 = . Sanity: 750 is 2.5 × 300, and 2.5 × 24 = 60.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-8 to 3-9 (Solving Proportions — fuel example) · AM.I.H.K5", "topic": "Proportion"}, {"q": "An engine burns 9 gallons of oil in 36 flight hours. At the same rate, in 60 flight hours it will burn:", "options": ["20 gallons", "12 gallons", "15 gallons"], "correct": 2, "short": "15 gallons", "expl": "Know: gallons-to-hours stays constant. Setup: 9/36 = N/60. Method: cross-multiply. Calc: 9 × 60 = 540; 540 ÷ 36 = 15 gal. Keys: 9 × 60 ÷ 36 = . Sanity: 9 gal per 36 hr = 1 gal per 4 hr; 60 ÷ 4 = 15.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-8 (Solving Proportions) · AM.I.H.K5", "topic": "Proportion"}, {"q": "A drawing uses a scale of 1 inch = 24 inches. A part measuring 2.5 inches on the drawing is actually:", "options": ["60 inches long", "9.6 inches long", "48 inches long"], "correct": 0, "short": "60 inches long", "expl": "Know: scale is a ratio, 1:24. Setup: 1/24 = 2.5/L. Method: cross-multiply. Calc: 2.5 × 24 = 60 in. Keys: 2.5 × 24 = . Sanity: each drawing inch is 2 feet, and 2.5 × 2 ft = 5 ft = 60 in.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-8 (Solving Proportions) · AM.I.H.K5", "topic": "Proportion"}, {"q": "A fuel-to-oil premix calls for 50:1. For 25 gallons of fuel, the oil required is:", "options": ["0.5 gallon", "1.25 gallons", "2 gallons"], "correct": 0, "short": "0.5 gallon", "expl": "Know: 50 parts fuel to 1 part oil. Setup: 50/1 = 25/N. Method: cross-multiply. Calc: 25 × 1 = 25; 25 ÷ 50 = 0.5 gal. Keys: 25 ÷ 50 = . Sanity: 25 gal is half of 50, so the oil is half of 1 gallon.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-8 (Solving Proportions) · AM.I.H.K5", "topic": "Proportion"}, {"q": "A propeller reduction gearbox turns the propeller at 2/3 of crankshaft speed. With the crankshaft at 2,700 rpm, the propeller turns at:", "options": ["4,050 rpm", "900 rpm", "1,800 rpm"], "correct": 2, "short": "1,800 rpm", "expl": "Know: prop = 2/3 of crank. Setup: 2/3 = P/2,700. Method: cross-multiply. Calc: 2 × 2,700 = 5,400; 5,400 ÷ 3 = 1,800 rpm. Keys: 2700 × 2 ÷ 3 = . Sanity: the prop must turn SLOWER than the crank — 1,800 < 2,700. 4,050 is the flipped-ratio trap.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-8 (Solving Proportions) · AM.I.H.K5", "topic": "Proportion"}]}]}, {"n": 5, "day": "Unit 5", "title": "Percentage — Including Power-Loss Problems", "ref": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3 pp. 3-9 to 3-11 · AM.I.H.K5", "sections": [{"id": "mau5s1", "title": "Percent ↔ decimal ↔ fraction (decimal hops)", "lesson": "\n<p><b>The Big Idea.</b> <b>Percent means \"per hundred.\"</b> 90% is 90 parts out\nof 100 — nothing deeper. Since \"out of 100\" is just a decimal wearing a badge,\nconverting is pure <b>decimal hops</b>:</p>\n<table>\n<tr><th>Direction</th><th>Move</th><th>Example</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Decimal → percent</td><td>2 hops <b>RIGHT</b>, add %</td><td>0.90 → 90% · 0.335 → 33.5% · 1.25 → 125%</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Percent → decimal</td><td>2 hops <b>LEFT</b>, drop %</td><td>5% → 0.05 · 90% → 0.90 · 150% → 1.5</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Fraction → percent</td><td>top ÷ bottom, then 2 hops right</td><td>5/8 → 0.625 → 62.5%</td></tr>\n</table>\n<p><b>Analogy.</b> Percent-to-decimal is taking the number's costume off. 5% looks\nbig and official, but under the costume it's 0.05 — a nickel on the dollar. The\ncalculator only ever works with the undressed (decimal) number.</p>\n<p><b>Direction check that never fails:</b> percentages under 100% are pieces of\na whole, so their decimals are under 1. If you convert 7% and get 0.7, stop —\n0.7 is 70%. Small percent, small decimal.</p>\n<div class=\"box ex\"><span class=\"bt\">Worked example</span>\n<ol class=\"ladder\"><li><span class=\"step\">1 · Write what you know (with units)</span>A magneto check allows a 7% rpm drop on a 2,000-rpm runup. Want: the allowed drop in rpm.</li><li><span class=\"step\">2 · Pick the method — say it in English</span>Undress 7% to a decimal, then take that much OF 2,000 (of = multiply).</li><li><span class=\"step\">3 · Set it up</span>7% → 0.07;  drop = 0.07 × 2,000</li><li><span class=\"step\">4 · One operation per line</span>7% = 0.07  (2 hops left)<br>0.07 × 2,000 = <b>140 rpm</b></li><li><span class=\"step\">5 · Calculator keystrokes</span><span class=\"keys\">2000 × .07 = 140</span></li><li><span class=\"step\">6 · Sanity check</span>10% of 2,000 would be 200; 7% should be a bit less. 140 fits.</li></ol>\n</div>\n<div class=\"box key\"><span class=\"bt\">Must-Know</span>\n<ul>\n<li>Percent = <b>parts per hundred</b>. 100% = 1.00 = the whole thing.</li>\n<li>Decimal ↔ percent = <b>two decimal hops</b> (right to %, left to decimal).</li>\n<li>Fraction → percent: divide, then hop. 5/8 = 62.5%.</li>\n<li>Over 100% is legal: 150% = 1.5 (one-and-a-half times).</li>\n</ul></div>\n<p class=\"cite\">Source: FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, pp. 3-9 to 3-10 (Percentage, Expressing Decimals/Percentages/Fractions) · ACS AM.I.H.K5.</p>\n", "quiz": [{"q": "Percentage means:", "options": ["Parts out of one hundred", "Parts out of ten", "Parts out of the whole, whatever its size"], "correct": 0, "short": "Parts out of one hundred", "expl": "Know: per-cent = per hundred (Latin centum). Method: 90% = 90 parts of 100 = 0.90. Sanity: 100% = the whole thing = 1.00.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-9 (Percentage) · AM.I.H.K5", "topic": "Percentage"}, {"q": "To express the decimal 0.90 as a percentage:", "options": ["Move the decimal point two places RIGHT and add % — 90%", "Move the decimal point two places LEFT and add % — 0.009%", "Multiply by 10 and add % — 9%"], "correct": 0, "short": "Move the decimal point two places RIGHT and add % — 90%", "expl": "Know: decimal → percent = ×100. Method: two hops right. Calc: 0.90 → 90%. Sanity: 0.9 of something is nearly all of it, and 90% is nearly all — direction check passed.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-9 (Expressing a Decimal Number as a Percentage) · AM.I.H.K5", "topic": "Percentage"}, {"q": "Expressed as a decimal, 5% is:", "options": ["5.0", "0.05", "0.5"], "correct": 1, "short": "0.05", "expl": "Know: percent → decimal = ÷100 (two hops LEFT, drop the %). Calc: 5% → 0.05. Sanity: 5% is a small slice; 0.5 would be half — ten times too big.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-10 (Expressing a Percentage as a Decimal Number) · AM.I.H.K5", "topic": "Percentage"}, {"q": "Expressed as a decimal, 150% is:", "options": ["0.15", "1.5", "15.0"], "correct": 1, "short": "1.5", "expl": "Know: more than 100% = more than 1. Method: two hops left. Calc: 150% → 1.50. Sanity: 150% of a load means one-and-a-half times the load — 1.5.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-10 (Expressing a Percentage as a Decimal Number) · AM.I.H.K5", "topic": "Percentage"}, {"q": "Expressed as a percentage, the fraction 5/8 is:", "options": ["80%", "62.5%", "58%"], "correct": 1, "short": "62.5%", "expl": "Know: fraction → decimal → percent. Setup: 5 ÷ 8. Calc: 0.625; two hops right → 62.5%. Keys: 5 ÷ 8 = 0.625. Sanity: 5/8 is a bit more than half, 62.5% is a bit more than 50%.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-10 (Expressing a Fraction as a Percentage) · AM.I.H.K5", "topic": "Percentage"}, {"q": "Expressed as a percentage, 0.335 is:", "options": ["3.35%", "33.5%", "0.335%"], "correct": 1, "short": "33.5%", "expl": "Know: two hops right. Calc: 0.335 → 33.5%. Sanity: 0.335 is about a third, and a third is about 33%.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-9 (Expressing a Decimal Number as a Percentage) · AM.I.H.K5", "topic": "Percentage"}, {"q": "A magneto drop check allows a maximum 7% rpm drop. As a decimal multiplier, 7% is:", "options": ["0.7", "7.0", "0.07"], "correct": 2, "short": "0.07", "expl": "Know: percent → decimal for calculator work. Method: two hops left. Calc: 7% → 0.07. Sanity: 7% of 2,000 rpm should be a small number (140), and 2,000 × 0.07 = 140 — 0.7 would give 1,400, absurd.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-10 (Expressing a Percentage as a Decimal Number) · AM.I.H.K5", "topic": "Percentage"}]}, {"id": "mau5s2", "title": "The three percent problems + percent change", "lesson": "\n<p><b>The Big Idea.</b> Every percent problem on the FAA written is one of THREE\nquestions. Learn to spot which one you're holding and the math is one line:</p>\n<table>\n<tr><th>Question</th><th>English</th><th>Move</th></tr>\n<tr><td>1. Find a percent OF a number</td><td>\"15% of 80 is what?\"</td><td>percent × whole → 0.15 × 80 = <b>12</b></td></tr>\n<tr><td>2. What percent is A of B?</td><td>\"10.75 is what % of 12?\"</td><td>part ÷ whole → 10.75 ÷ 12 = 0.8958 = <b>89.58%</b></td></tr>\n<tr><td>3. Find the whole from a percent</td><td>\"80 is 52% of what?\"</td><td>part ÷ percent → 80 ÷ 0.52 = <b>153.846</b></td></tr>\n</table>\n<p><b>The word \"of\" means multiply. The word \"is\" marks the part.</b>\nThose three handbook examples above (defective wingtip lights, motor efficiency,\nmicrophone resistance) are the FAA's own — know them cold.</p>\n<p><b>Analogy.</b> Type 3 trips everyone, so picture it as a receipt torn in\nhalf: you can see the tip ($80) and the tip rate (52%), and you're\nreconstructing the bill. The bill must be BIGGER than the tip — so you divide.\nIf your \"whole\" comes out smaller than the part you started with, you\nmultiplied when you should have divided.</p>\n<h3>Percent change (engine power loss, price increase)</h3>\n<p><b>change ÷ ORIGINAL</b>, then hop to percent. Takeoff rpm falls\n2,500 → 2,350: change 150; 150 ÷ 2,500 = 0.06 = <b>6% drop</b>. A $150 part up\n12%: 150 × 1.12 = <b>$168</b> (the ×1.12 trick adds the 12% in one move).</p>\n<div class=\"box ex\"><span class=\"bt\">Worked example — engine efficiency (handbook's own)</span>\n<ol class=\"ladder\"><li><span class=\"step\">1 · Write what you know (with units)</span>Engine rated 12 hp, actually delivering 10.75 hp. Want: efficiency in %.</li><li><span class=\"step\">2 · Pick the method — say it in English</span>Type 2 — what percent IS 10.75 OF 12? Part ÷ whole.</li><li><span class=\"step\">3 · Set it up</span>10.75 ÷ 12</li><li><span class=\"step\">4 · One operation per line</span>10.75 ÷ 12 = 0.8958<br>0.8958 → <b>89.58%</b>  (2 hops right)</li><li><span class=\"step\">5 · Calculator keystrokes</span><span class=\"keys\">10.75 ÷ 12 = 0.8958</span></li><li><span class=\"step\">6 · Sanity check</span>It delivers almost its full rating, so the answer should sit just under 100%. ✓</li></ol>\n</div>\n<div class=\"box key\"><span class=\"bt\">Must-Know</span>\n<ul>\n<li>Type 1: percent OF number → <b>multiply</b>.</li>\n<li>Type 2: what % is A of B → <b>part ÷ whole</b>.</li>\n<li>Type 3: find the whole → <b>part ÷ percent</b> (answer is BIGGER than the part).</li>\n<li>Percent change = <b>change ÷ original</b>.</li>\n</ul></div>\n<p class=\"cite\">Source: FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, pp. 3-10 to 3-11 (the three percentage problem types) · ACS AM.I.H.K5.</p>\n", "quiz": [{"q": "In a shipment of 80 wingtip lights, 15% were defective. The number of defective lights is:", "options": ["12", "8", "15"], "correct": 0, "short": "12", "expl": "Know: find a percent OF a number. Method: percent × whole. Setup: 0.15 × 80. Calc: 12. Keys: 80 × .15 = . Sanity: 10% of 80 is 8, so 15% must be a little more — 12 fits.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-10 (Finding a Percentage of a Given Number) · AM.I.H.K5", "topic": "Percentage"}, {"q": "A small engine rated at 12 horsepower delivers only 10.75 horsepower. Its efficiency is:", "options": ["98.5%", "85.9%", "89.58%"], "correct": 2, "short": "89.58%", "expl": "Know: find what percent one number is of another. Method: part ÷ whole, then ×100. Setup: 10.75 ÷ 12. Calc: 0.8958 → 89.58%. Keys: 10.75 ÷ 12 = . Sanity: 10.75 is just under 12, so the answer sits just under 100%.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-10 (Finding What Percentage One Number is of Another) · AM.I.H.K5", "topic": "Percentage"}, {"q": "Eighty ohms represents 52% of a microphone's total resistance. The total resistance is:", "options": ["160 ohms", "153.846 ohms", "41.6 ohms"], "correct": 1, "short": "153.846 ohms", "expl": "Know: find the WHOLE when a percent of it is known. Method: part ÷ percent. Setup: 80 ÷ 0.52. Calc: 153.846 Ω. Keys: 80 ÷ .52 = . Sanity: 80 is about half of the total, and 52% ≈ half — so the whole ≈ 160ish. 41.6 is the multiplied-instead trap.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-11 (Finding a Number When a Percentage of it is Known) · AM.I.H.K5", "topic": "Percentage"}, {"q": "An engine develops 85% of its rated 200 horsepower on a hot day. It is producing:", "options": ["115 horsepower", "170 horsepower", "185 horsepower"], "correct": 1, "short": "170 horsepower", "expl": "Know: percent OF a number. Setup: 0.85 × 200. Calc: 170 hp. Keys: 200 × .85 = . Sanity: 85% is most-but-not-all, and 170 is most of 200.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-10 (Finding a Percentage of a Given Number) · AM.I.H.K5", "topic": "Percentage"}, {"q": "An engine's takeoff rpm drops from 2,500 to 2,350 during a power check. The percent drop is:", "options": ["15%", "9.4%", "6%"], "correct": 2, "short": "6%", "expl": "Know: percent change = change ÷ ORIGINAL. Setup: (2,500 − 2,350) ÷ 2,500. Calc: 150 ÷ 2,500 = 0.06 → 6%. Keys: 150 ÷ 2500 = . Sanity: 10% would be 250 rpm; the drop of 150 is a bit over half of that — 6% fits.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-10 (Finding What Percentage One Number is of Another) · AM.I.H.K5", "topic": "Percentage"}, {"q": "A part that cost $150 goes up 12% in price. The new price is:", "options": ["$180", "$168", "$162"], "correct": 1, "short": "$168", "expl": "Know: increase = original × percent, then add. Setup: 150 × 0.12 = 18. Calc: 150 + 18 = $168. Keys: 150 × 1.12 = . Sanity: 10% would add $15; 12% adds a bit more — $18.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-10 (Finding a Percentage of a Given Number) · AM.I.H.K5", "topic": "Percentage"}, {"q": "A battery's capacity has fallen to 80% of its rated 35 ampere-hours. Its usable capacity is:", "options": ["24 ampere-hours", "28 ampere-hours", "31.5 ampere-hours"], "correct": 1, "short": "28 ampere-hours", "expl": "Know: percent OF a number. Setup: 0.80 × 35. Calc: 28 Ah. Keys: 35 × .8 = . Sanity: 80% = lose one-fifth; one-fifth of 35 is 7, and 35 − 7 = 28. (Airworthiness capacity checks are stated exactly this way.)", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-10 (Finding a Percentage of a Given Number) · AM.I.H.K5", "topic": "Percentage"}, {"q": "Twenty-four of the 30 questions on a quiz were answered correctly. The score is:", "options": ["80%", "24%", "75%"], "correct": 0, "short": "80%", "expl": "Know: part ÷ whole. Setup: 24 ÷ 30. Calc: 0.80 → 80%. Keys: 24 ÷ 30 = . Sanity: missing 6 of 30 = missing one-fifth = 20% off a perfect score.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-10 (Finding What Percentage One Number is of Another) · AM.I.H.K5", "topic": "Percentage"}, {"q": "If 60 gallons is 40% of a fuel tank's capacity, the full tank holds:", "options": ["100 gallons", "24 gallons", "150 gallons"], "correct": 2, "short": "150 gallons", "expl": "Know: find the whole. Method: part ÷ percent. Setup: 60 ÷ 0.40. Calc: 150 gal. Keys: 60 ÷ .4 = . Sanity: 40% ≈ 2/5, and 2/5 of 150 = 60 — checks. 24 is the multiplied trap.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-11 (Finding a Number When a Percentage of it is Known) · AM.I.H.K5", "topic": "Percentage"}]}]}, {"n": 6, "day": "Unit 6", "title": "Signed (Positive & Negative) Numbers", "ref": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3 pp. 3-11 to 3-12 · AM.I.H.K12, R2", "sections": [{"id": "mau6s1", "title": "Adding & subtracting — the money-and-debt model", "lesson": "\n<p><b>The Big Idea.</b> Numbers live on a line. Zero is home plate; positives run\nright, negatives run left. Signed numbers (integers) show up on every W&amp;B\nsheet and every cold-weather temp problem you'll touch.</p>\n<p><b>Analogy — the one that makes it all easy: money and debt.</b>\nA negative number is money you OWE. −100 means $100 in debt.</p>\n<h3>Adding</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Two positives → positive (two deposits).</li>\n<li>Two negatives → more negative (two withdrawals: −100 + −13 = −113).</li>\n<li>Mixed signs → <b>subtract the sizes, keep the sign of the bigger one.</b>\n−100 + 45: you owed 100, paid 45, still owe <b>−55</b>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><b>W&amp;B convention (handbook's own example):</b> weight REMOVED gets a minus\nsign, weight added gets a plus. Aircraft at 2,000 lb, remove a 3-lb rack and a\n10-lb transceiver: 2,000 + (−3) + (−10) = <b>1,987 lb</b>.</p>\n<h3>Subtracting: add the opposite</h3>\n<p>The handbook's rule: change \"−\" to \"+\" and flip the second number's sign,\nthen add. So <b>minus-a-minus becomes a plus</b>: 15 − (−10) = 15 + 10 = 25.\n<i>Taking away a debt is the same as handing you cash.</i></p>\n<div class=\"box ex\"><span class=\"bt\">Worked example — temperature aloft (handbook's own)</span>\n<ol class=\"ladder\"><li><span class=\"step\">1 · Write what you know (with units)</span>Denver surface temp −6°. At 15,000 ft it is 20° COLDER. Want: temp aloft.</li><li><span class=\"step\">2 · Pick the method — say it in English</span>Colder = subtract 20. Subtracting → add the opposite.</li><li><span class=\"step\">3 · Set it up</span>−6 − 20 = −6 + (−20)</li><li><span class=\"step\">4 · One operation per line</span>−6 + (−20) = <b>−26°</b></li><li><span class=\"step\">5 · Calculator keystrokes</span><span class=\"keys\">6 +/− − 20 = −26</span></li><li><span class=\"step\">6 · Sanity check</span>Start below zero and get colder — the answer must be deeper negative, not warmer. −14 would be the wrong-direction trap.</li></ol>\n</div>\n<div class=\"box key\"><span class=\"bt\">Must-Know</span>\n<ul>\n<li>Mixed-sign addition: subtract sizes, <b>keep the bigger number's sign</b>.</li>\n<li>Subtraction rule: <b>add the opposite</b>. Minus-a-minus = plus.</li>\n<li>W&amp;B: removed weight is negative, added weight is positive.</li>\n<li>Temperature difference across zero: count through zero (from −35 to +15 is 50°).</li>\n</ul></div>\n<p class=\"cite\">Source: FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-11 (Addition/Subtraction of Positive &amp; Negative Numbers) · ACS AM.I.H.K12, R2.</p>\n", "quiz": [{"q": "The sum of two negative numbers is always:", "options": ["Negative", "Positive", "Zero"], "correct": 0, "short": "Negative", "expl": "Know: adding debt to debt. Method: −100 plus −50 is 150 in the hole → −150. Sanity: two withdrawals can never make a deposit.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-11 (Addition of Positive & Negative Numbers) · AM.I.H.K12, AM.I.H.R2", "topic": "Signed numbers"}, {"q": "Solve: −100 + 45 =", "options": ["−55", "+55", "−145"], "correct": 0, "short": "−55", "expl": "Know: debt picture — $100 in debt, deposit $45. Method: opposite signs → subtract sizes, keep the sign of the larger. Calc: 100 − 45 = 55; larger size is negative → −55. Keys: 45 − 100 = −55. Sanity: you paid off less than the debt, so you are still negative.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-11 (Addition of Positive & Negative Numbers) · AM.I.H.K12", "topic": "Signed numbers"}, {"q": "An aircraft weighs 2,000 pounds. A 3-pound radio rack and a 10-pound transceiver are removed. The new weight is:", "options": ["1,990 pounds", "1,987 pounds", "2,013 pounds"], "correct": 1, "short": "1,987 pounds", "expl": "Know: removed weight takes a minus sign (W&B convention). Setup: 2,000 + (−3) + (−10). Calc: 2,000 − 13 = 1,987 lb. Keys: 2000 − 3 − 10 = . Sanity: taking things OFF must make it lighter — 2,013 is the sign-flip trap.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-11 (Addition of Positive & Negative Numbers — W&B example) · AM.I.H.K12, AM.I.H.R2", "topic": "Signed numbers"}, {"q": "To subtract signed numbers, the handbook's rule is:", "options": ["Add both numbers and always keep a negative sign", "Change the subtraction to addition, flip the sign of the second number, then add", "Subtract the smaller number from the larger and keep the first sign"], "correct": 1, "short": "Change the subtraction to addition, flip the sign of the second number, then add", "expl": "Know: subtracting is adding the opposite. Method: a − b = a + (−b). Sanity: 5 − (−3) becomes 5 + 3 = 8 — subtracting a negative moves you UP.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-11 (Subtraction of Positive & Negative Numbers) · AM.I.H.K12", "topic": "Signed numbers"}, {"q": "The temperature in Denver is −6°. At 15,000 feet above the city it is 20° colder. The temperature at altitude is:", "options": ["+14°", "−14°", "−26°"], "correct": 2, "short": "−26°", "expl": "Know: colder = subtract. Setup: −6 − 20. Method: add the opposite: −6 + (−20). Calc: −26°. Keys: 6 +/− − 20 = . Sanity: it must be FAR below zero — −14 is the wrong-direction trap.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-11 (Subtraction of Positive & Negative Numbers — temperature example) · AM.I.H.K12, AM.I.H.R2", "topic": "Signed numbers"}, {"q": "Solve: 15 − (−10) =", "options": ["−5", "25", "5"], "correct": 1, "short": "25", "expl": "Know: subtracting a negative. Method: minus-a-minus becomes plus. Calc: 15 + 10 = 25. Sanity: on the thermometer, going from −10 up to 15 is a 25-degree climb.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-11 (Subtraction of Positive & Negative Numbers) · AM.I.H.K12", "topic": "Signed numbers"}, {"q": "The OAT at altitude is −35 °C and the ground temperature is +15 °C. The temperature difference is:", "options": ["20 degrees", "50 degrees", "35 degrees"], "correct": 1, "short": "50 degrees", "expl": "Know: difference = higher − lower. Setup: 15 − (−35). Method: minus-a-minus → 15 + 35. Calc: 50°. Sanity: from −35 you climb 35 degrees to reach zero, then 15 more — 50 total.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-11 (Subtraction of Positive & Negative Numbers) · AM.I.H.K12", "topic": "Signed numbers"}]}, {"id": "mau6s2", "title": "Multiplying & dividing — count the minus signs", "lesson": "\n<p><b>The Big Idea.</b> For multiplying and dividing, forget the number line and\njust count the minus signs. The rule is identical for × and ÷:</p>\n<table>\n<tr><th>Signs</th><th>Result</th><th>Examples</th></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Same</b> (+·+ or −·−)</td><td><b>Positive</b></td><td>3 × 6 = 18 · −3 × −6 = 18 · −6 ÷ −3 = 2</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Different</b> (+·−)</td><td><b>Negative</b></td><td>−3 × 6 = −18 · 6 ÷ −3 = −2</td></tr>\n</table>\n<p><b>Analogy.</b> A minus sign is an about-face command. One about-face and\nyou're walking backward (negative). Two about-faces and you're facing forward\nagain (positive). Count the about-faces: even count → positive, odd count →\nnegative.</p>\n<p><b>Do the math, then the sign.</b> Work the numbers as if everything were\npositive, then stamp the sign on at the end. −45 ÷ 9: numbers say 5, signs are\ndifferent → <b>−5</b>. Check by multiplying back: 9 × (−5) = −45. ✓</p>\n<div class=\"box ex\"><span class=\"bt\">Worked example — repeated loss</span>\n<ol class=\"ladder\"><li><span class=\"step\">1 · Write what you know (with units)</span>A part cools 4° per minute for 7 minutes. Want: total temperature change.</li><li><span class=\"step\">2 · Pick the method — say it in English</span>A repeated loss is multiplication of a negative: −4 per minute, 7 times.</li><li><span class=\"step\">3 · Set it up</span>−4 × 7</li><li><span class=\"step\">4 · One operation per line</span>4 × 7 = 28  (numbers first)<br>signs differ → <b>−28°</b></li><li><span class=\"step\">5 · Calculator keystrokes</span><span class=\"keys\">4 × 7 = 28, apply the − sign</span></li><li><span class=\"step\">6 · Sanity check</span>Seven small drops must stack into one big drop — a negative answer, bigger in size than any single drop.</li></ol>\n</div>\n<div class=\"box key\"><span class=\"bt\">Must-Know</span>\n<ul>\n<li>× and ÷: <b>same signs → positive, different signs → negative.</b></li>\n<li>Even number of minus signs → positive; odd → negative.</li>\n<li>Numbers first, sign last; verify division by multiplying back.</li>\n<li>Nearer zero = larger: −1 &gt; −25.</li>\n</ul></div>\n<p class=\"cite\">Source: FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, pp. 3-11 to 3-12 (Multiplication/Division of Positive &amp; Negative Numbers, Figure 3-8) · ACS AM.I.H.K12, R2.</p>\n", "quiz": [{"q": "The product of two negative numbers is always:", "options": ["Positive", "Negative", "Zero"], "correct": 0, "short": "Positive", "expl": "Know: sign rules for ×: same signs → positive, different signs → negative. Calc: −3 × −6 = 18. Sanity: reversing a reverse points you forward.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-11 (Multiplication of Positive & Negative Numbers) · AM.I.H.K12", "topic": "Signed numbers"}, {"q": "Solve: −3 × 6 =", "options": ["−18", "−9", "18"], "correct": 0, "short": "−18", "expl": "Know: different signs → negative. Calc: 3 × 6 = 18, apply the negative → −18. Sanity: losing 3 six times = down 18. −9 is the added-instead trap.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-11 (Multiplication of Positive & Negative Numbers) · AM.I.H.K12", "topic": "Signed numbers"}, {"q": "Solve: −6 ÷ −3 =", "options": ["−18", "2", "−2"], "correct": 1, "short": "2", "expl": "Know: division follows the same sign rules as multiplication. Method: same signs → positive. Calc: 6 ÷ 3 = 2. Sanity: −3 × 2 = −6 — multiplying back rebuilds the dividend.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-11 (Division of Positive & Negative Numbers) · AM.I.H.K12", "topic": "Signed numbers"}, {"q": "Solve: 6 ÷ −3 =", "options": ["−3", "2", "−2"], "correct": 2, "short": "−2", "expl": "Know: different signs → negative. Calc: 6 ÷ 3 = 2, apply the minus → −2. Sanity: −3 × −2 = +6 — checks.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-11 (Division of Positive & Negative Numbers) · AM.I.H.K12", "topic": "Signed numbers"}, {"q": "A component's temperature falls 4 degrees per minute for 7 minutes. The total temperature change is:", "options": ["−28 degrees", "+28 degrees", "−11 degrees"], "correct": 0, "short": "−28 degrees", "expl": "Know: a fall is −4 per minute. Setup: −4 × 7. Calc: −28°. Sanity: repeated loss stays a loss; −11 is the added trap.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-11 (Multiplication of Positive & Negative Numbers) · AM.I.H.K12, AM.I.H.R2", "topic": "Signed numbers"}, {"q": "An aircraft's total moment change is −120 pound-inches after removing equipment at four identical stations. Each removal contributed:", "options": ["+30 pound-inches", "−480 pound-inches", "−30 pound-inches"], "correct": 2, "short": "−30 pound-inches", "expl": "Know: total ÷ count. Setup: −120 ÷ 4. Method: different signs → negative. Calc: −30 lb-in each. Sanity: four equal negative moments of −30 rebuild −120.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-11 (Division of Positive & Negative Numbers) · AM.I.H.K12", "topic": "Signed numbers"}, {"q": "Solve: −2 × −3 × −4 =", "options": ["−24", "24", "−9"], "correct": 0, "short": "−24", "expl": "Know: three minus signs — an ODD count → negative. Calc: 2 × 3 = 6; 6 × 4 = 24; odd minus count → −24. Sanity: pair off two minuses (+6), one minus remains → negative.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-11 (Multiplication of Positive & Negative Numbers) · AM.I.H.K12, AM.I.H.R2", "topic": "Signed numbers"}, {"q": "On the signed-number scale, which number is LARGEST?", "options": ["−25", "−4", "−1"], "correct": 2, "short": "−1", "expl": "Know: the number line runs …−3, −2, −1, 0, +1… Method: closer to zero = larger for negatives. Sanity: owing $1 leaves you richer than owing $25.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-11 to 3-12, Figure 3-8 (A scale of signed numbers) · AM.I.H.K12", "topic": "Signed numbers"}]}]}, {"n": 7, "day": "Unit 7", "title": "Powers, Roots, Scientific Notation & Binary", "ref": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3 pp. 3-11 to 3-16, 3-27 to 3-28 · AM.I.H.K8, K10, S1", "sections": [{"id": "mau7s1", "title": "Powers, special powers & roots", "lesson": "\n<p><b>The Big Idea.</b> A power (exponent) is shorthand for \"multiply this number\nby itself.\" In 3⁴, the <b>base</b> is 3 and the <b>power</b> is 4:\n3 × 3 × 3 × 3 = 81. <b>Squared</b> = 2nd power (from the area of a square,\ns × s). <b>Cubed</b> = 3rd power (from the volume of a cube).</p>\n<p><b>The trap the FAA loves:</b> 2³ is NOT 2 × 3. It's 2 × 2 × 2 = <b>8</b>.\nThe little number counts the multiplications; it never joins them.</p>\n<h3>The two special powers</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Power of zero:</b> any non-zero number to the 0 power = <b>1</b>. (7⁰ = 1, 181⁰ = 1.)</li>\n<li><b>Negative powers:</b> flip it. n⁻ˣ = 1/nˣ. So 2⁻³ = 1/2³ = <b>1/8</b>.\nNegative exponents make numbers <b>small</b>, never negative.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><b>Analogy — the staircase.</b> 2³=8, 2²=4, 2¹=2… each step DOWN the exponent\nstaircase divides by the base. Keep walking: 2⁰=1 (that's why anything⁰=1), then\n2⁻¹=1/2, 2⁻²=1/4. The pattern never breaks.</p>\n<h3>Roots — the un-doers</h3>\n<p>A root asks the reverse question. <b>√25 = 5</b> because 5 × 5 = 25.\n<b>³√125 = 5</b> because 5 × 5 × 5 = 125. For a root that isn't clean, BRACKET\nit with perfect squares: √31 sits between √25 (=5) and √36 (=6), so it's\n5-point-something (5.57). Memorize the perfect squares to 15² = 225 and this\ntrick answers every root question on the test.</p>\n<p><b>Roots as fraction powers:</b> √n = n^½, ³√n = n^⅓, ⁴√n = n^¼ — which is\nwhy roots take the \"exponent\" seat in PEMDAS.</p>\n<div class=\"box ex\"><span class=\"bt\">Worked example</span>\n<ol class=\"ladder\"><li><span class=\"step\">1 · Write what you know (with units)</span>Want: √31, no root key available.</li><li><span class=\"step\">2 · Pick the method — say it in English</span>Bracket with the nearest perfect squares, then guess-and-refine.</li><li><span class=\"step\">3 · Set it up</span>5² = 25 &lt; 31 &lt; 36 = 6²</li><li><span class=\"step\">4 · One operation per line</span>√31 is between 5 and 6<br>try 5.5: 5.5² = 30.25 (low)<br>try 5.6: 5.6² = 31.36 (high)<br>≈ <b>5.57</b></li><li><span class=\"step\">5 · Calculator keystrokes</span><span class=\"keys\">31 √ = 5.5678</span></li><li><span class=\"step\">6 · Sanity check</span>5.57² ≈ 31 — inside the 5-to-6 bracket, and slightly nearer 6 because 31 sits slightly nearer 36 than 25. ✓</li></ol>\n</div>\n<div class=\"box key\"><span class=\"bt\">Must-Know</span>\n<ul>\n<li>Base multiplied by ITSELF, power-many times. 2³ = 8, never 6.</li>\n<li><b>n⁰ = 1</b> · <b>n⁻ˣ = 1/nˣ</b> (small, not negative).</li>\n<li>√ un-does squaring; ³√ un-does cubing. Bracket ugly roots with perfect squares.</li>\n<li>√n = n to the ½ power — roots are exponents.</li>\n</ul></div>\n<p class=\"cite\">Source: FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, pp. 3-11 to 3-13 (Powers, Special Powers, Negative Powers, Roots, Fractional Powers) · ACS AM.I.H.K10, S1.</p>\n", "quiz": [{"q": "In the expression 3⁴, the 3 is called the ______ and the 4 is called the ______:", "options": ["Factor; multiple", "Power; base", "Base; power (exponent)"], "correct": 2, "short": "Base; power (exponent)", "expl": "Know: the power says how many times to multiply the base by itself. Calc: 3⁴ = 3 × 3 × 3 × 3 = 81. Sanity: the big number on the line is the base you stand on; the little raised number counts the multiplications.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-11 (Powers) · AM.I.H.K10", "topic": "Powers"}, {"q": "The value of 2³ is:", "options": ["6", "9", "8"], "correct": 2, "short": "8", "expl": "Know: 2 multiplied by itself 3 times. Calc: 2 × 2 = 4; 4 × 2 = 8. Keys: 2 y^x 3 = . Sanity: 6 is the 2×3 trap — powers multiply the base by ITSELF, not by the exponent.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-11 to 3-12 (Powers) · AM.I.H.K10", "topic": "Powers"}, {"q": "Any non-zero number raised to the power of zero equals:", "options": ["Itself", "1", "0"], "correct": 1, "short": "1", "expl": "Know: special power rule. Calc: 7⁰ = 1; 181⁰ = 1. Sanity: each step DOWN in exponent divides by the base (2³=8, 2²=4, 2¹=2, 2⁰=1) — the pattern lands on 1.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-12 (Special Powers — Power of Zero) · AM.I.H.K10", "topic": "Powers"}, {"q": "The value of 2⁻³ is:", "options": ["−8", "1/8", "−6"], "correct": 1, "short": "1/8", "expl": "Know: a negative power = the reciprocal with a positive power. Setup: 2⁻³ = 1/2³. Calc: 1/(2×2×2) = 1/8. Sanity: negative exponents make numbers SMALL, not negative — −8 is the classic trap.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-12 (Negative Powers) · AM.I.H.K10", "topic": "Powers"}, {"q": "A number 'squared' means, and a number 'cubed' means:", "options": ["Multiplied by itself once (n²); multiplied by itself twice more (n³)", "Multiplied by 2; multiplied by 3", "Halved; divided into thirds"], "correct": 0, "short": "Multiplied by itself once (n²); multiplied by itself twice more (n³)", "expl": "Know: squared = to the 2nd power, cubed = to the 3rd. Calc: 5² = 25, 5³ = 125. Sanity: 'squared' comes from the area of a square (s×s), 'cubed' from the volume of a cube (s×s×s).", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-12 (Special Powers — Squared, Cubed) · AM.I.H.K10", "topic": "Powers"}, {"q": "The square root of 25 is:", "options": ["625", "5", "12.5"], "correct": 1, "short": "5", "expl": "Know: the root un-does the square. Method: what number times itself gives 25? Calc: 5 × 5 = 25 → √25 = 5. Keys: 25 √ = . Sanity: 625 is 25 SQUARED — the opposite direction.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-12 (Square Roots) · AM.I.H.S1", "topic": "Roots"}, {"q": "The square root of 31 lies between which two whole numbers?", "options": ["15 and 16", "5 and 6", "4 and 5"], "correct": 1, "short": "5 and 6", "expl": "Know: bracket with perfect squares. Method: 5² = 25 and 6² = 36; 31 sits between them. Calc: √31 ≈ 5.57. Keys: 31 √ = . Sanity: 15½ would square to about 240 — way off; halving is not square-rooting.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-12 (Square Roots) · AM.I.H.S1", "topic": "Roots"}, {"q": "The cube root of 125 is:", "options": ["5", "25", "41.7"], "correct": 0, "short": "5", "expl": "Know: what number used three times gives 125? Calc: 5 × 5 × 5 = 125 → ³√125 = 5. Sanity: 25 is the square root of 625 confusion; 41.7 is the ÷3 trap.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-13 (Cube Roots) · AM.I.H.K10", "topic": "Roots"}, {"q": "Written with a fractional exponent, the square root of 31 is:", "options": ["1/2 to the 31st power", "31 to the 1/2 power", "31 to the 2nd power"], "correct": 1, "short": "31 to the 1/2 power", "expl": "Know: roots can be written as fraction powers — square root = power 1/2, cube root = power 1/3, fourth root = power 1/4. Sanity: 31² would be 961, far from √31 ≈ 5.57.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-13 (Fractional Powers) · AM.I.H.K10", "topic": "Roots"}]}, {"id": "mau7s2", "title": "Scientific notation — the decimal-hop method", "lesson": "\n<p><b>The Big Idea.</b> Scientific notation is a shipping container for very big\nand very small numbers. Format: <b>(a number from 1 up to but not including 10)\n× (a power of 10)</b>. 2,350,000 = 2.35 × 10⁶. The decimal point always parks\nright after the FIRST digit — 23.5 × 10⁵ is the same amount but ILLEGAL\nformatting, and the FAA tests that.</p>\n<h3>The decimal-hop method (same hops as Unit 5)</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Standard → scientific:</b> hop the point until one digit leads. Count the\nhops — that's your power. Hopped LEFT (big number) → power is <b>positive</b>.\nHopped RIGHT (small number) → power is <b>negative</b>.</li>\n<li><b>Scientific → standard:</b> reverse it. Positive power → hop RIGHT that\nmany places (filling zeros): 3.68 × 10⁷ = 36,800,000. Negative power → hop\nLEFT: 7.1543 × 10⁻¹⁰ = 0.00000000071543.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><b>Analogy.</b> The exponent is a hop counter, nothing more. Big things\n(speed of light: 1.86 × 10⁵ mps) carry positive counters; tiny things\n(electron mass: 9.11 × 10⁻²⁸ g) carry negative ones. The sign tells you which\ndirection the real number lives, the digit tells you how far.</p>\n<p><b>Doing arithmetic with scientific numbers</b> (the handbook's method):\nconvert back to standard notation, do the ordinary math, convert the answer\nback to scientific. Simple and unbreakable.</p>\n<div class=\"box ex\"><span class=\"bt\">Worked example (handbook's own)</span>\n<ol class=\"ladder\"><li><span class=\"step\">1 · Write what you know (with units)</span>Convert 1,244,000,000,000 to scientific notation.</li><li><span class=\"step\">2 · Pick the method — say it in English</span>Hop the decimal left until only the 1 leads; count hops = power; big number → positive.</li><li><span class=\"step\">3 · Set it up</span>1,244,000,000,000.0 → 1.244</li><li><span class=\"step\">4 · One operation per line</span>hops counted: 12<br>power: +12<br>= <b>1.244 × 10¹²</b></li><li><span class=\"step\">5 · Calculator keystrokes</span><span class=\"keys\">(no keys — count the hops)</span></li><li><span class=\"step\">6 · Sanity check</span>10¹² is a trillion, and 1.244 trillion is the number. ✓</li></ol>\n</div>\n<div class=\"box key\"><span class=\"bt\">Must-Know</span>\n<ul>\n<li>First part is <b>≥ 1 and &lt; 10</b> — one digit before the point.</li>\n<li>Big number → <b>positive</b> power. Below 1 → <b>negative</b> power.</li>\n<li>Hop count = the exponent, both directions.</li>\n<li>To compute with them: go standard, compute, come back.</li>\n</ul></div>\n<p class=\"cite\">Source: FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, pp. 3-13, 3-16 (Scientific Notation, converting both ways) · ACS AM.I.H.K8.</p>\n", "quiz": [{"q": "A number written in scientific notation has two parts. The first part must be:", "options": ["A number that is at least 1 but less than 10", "A number between 0 and 1", "Any whole number"], "correct": 0, "short": "A number that is at least 1 but less than 10", "expl": "Know: format is (1 ≤ n < 10) × 10^power — the decimal point sits right after the FIRST digit. Sanity: 23.5 × 10⁵ breaks the rule; it must be written 2.35 × 10⁶.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-13 (Scientific Notation) · AM.I.H.K8", "topic": "Scientific notation"}, {"q": "Expressed in scientific notation, 2,350,000 is:", "options": ["2.35 × 10⁻⁶", "23.5 × 10⁵", "2.35 × 10⁶"], "correct": 2, "short": "2.35 × 10⁶", "expl": "Know: big number → positive power. Method: hop the decimal left until one digit remains in front — count 6 hops. Calc: 2.35 × 10⁶. Sanity: 10⁶ is a million, and 2.35 million is the number.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-13 (Scientific Notation) · AM.I.H.K8", "topic": "Scientific notation"}, {"q": "Expressed in scientific notation, 0.000000457 is:", "options": ["4.57 × 10⁻⁷", "45.7 × 10⁻⁸", "4.57 × 10⁷"], "correct": 0, "short": "4.57 × 10⁻⁷", "expl": "Know: small number (less than 1) → NEGATIVE power. Method: hop the decimal right to land after the 4 — 7 hops. Calc: 4.57 × 10⁻⁷. Sanity: the first part must be 1-to-10, so 45.7 is illegal formatting.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-16 (Converting Standard to Scientific Notation) · AM.I.H.K8", "topic": "Scientific notation"}, {"q": "Converted to standard notation, 3.68 × 10⁷ is:", "options": ["36,800,000", "3,680,000", "0.000000368"], "correct": 0, "short": "36,800,000", "expl": "Know: positive power → hop RIGHT. Method: move the decimal 7 places right, filling with zeros. Calc: 3.68 → 36,800,000. Sanity: 10⁷ is ten million, and 3.68 ten-millions ≈ 37 million.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-16 (Converting Scientific to Standard Notation) · AM.I.H.K8", "topic": "Scientific notation"}, {"q": "Converted to standard notation, 7.1543 × 10⁻¹⁰ is:", "options": ["0.00000000071543", "71,543,000,000", "0.0000000071543"], "correct": 0, "short": "0.00000000071543", "expl": "Know: negative power → hop LEFT. Method: move the decimal 10 places left, filling with zeros. Calc: 0.00000000071543. Sanity: a negative power means a tiny number — count exactly 10 hops (9 zeros appear between the point and the 7).", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-16 (Converting Scientific to Standard Notation) · AM.I.H.K8", "topic": "Scientific notation"}, {"q": "To add, subtract, multiply, or divide numbers written in scientific notation, the handbook says to:", "options": ["Convert them to standard notation, compute, then convert the answer back", "Add the exponents and multiply the first parts in every case", "Work only with the exponents and ignore the first parts"], "correct": 0, "short": "Convert them to standard notation, compute, then convert the answer back", "expl": "Know: the handbook's method is the practical shop method — go standard, do the arithmetic, return to scientific. Sanity: adding exponents only works for MULTIPLICATION, not for addition/subtraction.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-16 (Add/Subtract/Multiply/Divide of Scientific Numbers) · AM.I.H.K8", "topic": "Scientific notation"}, {"q": "The speed of light, 186,000 miles per second, in scientific notation is:", "options": ["1.86 × 10⁶ mps", "18.6 × 10⁴ mps", "1.86 × 10⁵ mps"], "correct": 2, "short": "1.86 × 10⁵ mps", "expl": "Know: hop left until one digit leads: 1-8-6-0-0-0 → 5 hops. Calc: 1.86 × 10⁵. Sanity: 10⁵ = 100,000, and 1.86 × 100,000 = 186,000 — checks.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-13 (Scientific Notation — example) · AM.I.H.K8", "topic": "Scientific notation"}]}, {"id": "mau7s3", "title": "The binary number system", "lesson": "\n<p><b>The Big Idea.</b> Your calculator thinks in tens; a computer — and every\ndigital box on a modern flight deck — thinks in twos. <b>Binary</b> has exactly\ntwo digits, 0 and 1, because a switch has exactly two states: <b>off (0)</b> and\n<b>on (1)</b>. That hardware fact is the entire reason binary exists.</p>\n<h3>Place values double</h3>\n<p>Decimal places go ×10 each step; binary places go <b>×2</b>. Right to left:</p>\n<table>\n<tr><th>Place</th><td>128</td><td>64</td><td>32</td><td>16</td><td>8</td><td>4</td><td>2</td><td>1</td></tr>\n<tr><th>Bit</th><td>1</td><td>0</td><td>1</td><td>1</td><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>1</td><td>1</td></tr>\n</table>\n<p><b>Binary → decimal: add up the places that hold a 1.</b> The row above is\nthe handbook's own example, 10110011: 128 + 32 + 16 + 2 + 1 = <b>179</b>.\nZeros contribute nothing — skip them.</p>\n<p><b>Decimal → binary: pay with doubling coins.</b> Your coin purse holds one\neach of 128, 64, 32, 16, 8, 4, 2, 1. Pay the amount using the biggest coins\nfirst; a used coin writes 1, an unused coin writes 0. Pay 6: skip 8 (too big),\nuse 4, use 2, skip 1 → <b>110</b>.</p>\n<div class=\"box ex\"><span class=\"bt\">Worked example</span>\n<ol class=\"ladder\"><li><span class=\"step\">1 · Write what you know (with units)</span>Convert binary 1010 to decimal.</li><li><span class=\"step\">2 · Pick the method — say it in English</span>Label places right-to-left (1, 2, 4, 8), add the places holding a 1.</li><li><span class=\"step\">3 · Set it up</span>1·8 + 0·4 + 1·2 + 0·1</li><li><span class=\"step\">4 · One operation per line</span>8 + 0 = 8<br>8 + 2 = 10<br>10 + 0 = <b>10</b></li><li><span class=\"step\">5 · Calculator keystrokes</span><span class=\"keys\">8 + 2 = 10</span></li><li><span class=\"step\">6 · Sanity check</span>Four bits max out at 1111 = 15; our number with two zeros should be well under — 10 is.</li></ol>\n</div>\n<div class=\"box key\"><span class=\"bt\">Must-Know</span>\n<ul>\n<li>Binary = <b>Base-2</b>, digits 0 and 1 = switch <b>off/on</b>.</li>\n<li>Place values 1, 2, 4, 8, 16, 32, 64, 128 (right to left).</li>\n<li>Binary → decimal: <b>add the 1-places</b>. 10110011 = 179.</li>\n<li>Decimal → binary: biggest coin first, 1 = used, 0 = skipped.</li>\n</ul></div>\n<p class=\"cite\">Source: FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, pp. 3-27 to 3-28 (The Binary Number System) · ACS AM.I.H.K8.</p>\n", "quiz": [{"q": "The binary number system has how many digits, and what are they?", "options": ["Two — 1 and 2", "Ten — 0 through 9", "Two — 0 and 1"], "correct": 2, "short": "Two — 0 and 1", "expl": "Know: 'bi' = two. The system exists because a switch has exactly two states: off (0) and on (1). Sanity: 0-through-9 is the DECIMAL system.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-27 (The Binary Number System) · AM.I.H.K8", "topic": "Binary"}, {"q": "The binary number system is based on powers of:", "options": ["10", "2", "8"], "correct": 1, "short": "2", "expl": "Know: Base-2 — place values double: 1, 2, 4, 8, 16, 32, 64, 128… Sanity: decimal is Base-10 where places multiply by ten.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-27 (Place Values) · AM.I.H.K8", "topic": "Binary"}, {"q": "The primary reason binary suits digital electronics is that:", "options": ["Binary numbers are shorter than decimal numbers", "Electricity's two conditions, on and off, map onto the digits 1 and 0", "Binary arithmetic requires no addition"], "correct": 1, "short": "Electricity's two conditions, on and off, map onto the digits 1 and 0", "expl": "Know: on = 1, off = 0 — a voltage either flows or it doesn't. Sanity: binary numbers are actually LONGER than decimal (179 takes 8 bits), so brevity is not the reason.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-27 (The Binary Number System) · AM.I.H.K8", "topic": "Binary"}, {"q": "The binary number 10110011 equals what decimal number?", "options": ["183", "179", "153"], "correct": 1, "short": "179", "expl": "Know: 8-bit place values 128-64-32-16-8-4-2-1. Method: add the places holding a 1. Calc: 128 + 32 + 16 + 2 + 1 = 179. Sanity: leading 1 guarantees at least 128, and all-ones would be 255 — 179 sits between.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-27 (Converting Binary Numbers to Decimal Numbers) · AM.I.H.K8", "topic": "Binary"}, {"q": "The binary number 101 equals what decimal number?", "options": ["5", "101", "3"], "correct": 0, "short": "5", "expl": "Know: places (right to left) 1, 2, 4. Method: 1×4 + 0×2 + 1×1. Calc: 4 + 0 + 1 = 5. Sanity: three bits max out at 7 (111); 101 must be under that.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-27 (Converting Binary Numbers to Decimal Numbers) · AM.I.H.K8", "topic": "Binary"}, {"q": "The decimal number 6 written in binary is:", "options": ["110", "101", "011"], "correct": 0, "short": "110", "expl": "Know: 6 = 4 + 2. Method: put a 1 in the 4s and 2s places, 0 in the 1s. Calc: 110. Sanity: check back — 1×4 + 1×2 + 0×1 = 6. (011 is 3; 101 is 5.)", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-27 to 3-28 (Converting Decimal Numbers to Binary Numbers) · AM.I.H.K8", "topic": "Binary"}]}]}, {"n": 8, "day": "Unit 8", "title": "Algebra & Formula Rearrangement", "ref": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3 pp. 3-16 to 3-18 · AM.I.H.K6, R1", "sections": [{"id": "mau8s1", "title": "Solving equations — the balance scale & cover-up trick", "lesson": "\n<p><b>The Big Idea.</b> Algebra is arithmetic with a mystery guest. The letters\n(variables) are just numbers that haven't introduced themselves yet — d = v × t\nis \"distance is speed times time\" with name tags instead of numbers.</p>\n<p><b>Analogy — the balance scale.</b> An equation is a scale in perfect balance;\nthe = sign is the pivot. You may do ANYTHING to one pan — add, subtract,\nmultiply, divide — as long as you do the identical thing to the other pan.\nThat single rule is all of algebra.</p>\n<h3>The four un-doing moves (handbook's own examples)</h3>\n<table>\n<tr><th>Equation</th><th>What's stuck to N</th><th>Un-do on both sides</th><th>Answer</th></tr>\n<tr><td>3N = 21</td><td>×3</td><td>÷3</td><td>N = 7</td></tr>\n<tr><td>N + 17 = 59</td><td>+17</td><td>−17</td><td>N = 42</td></tr>\n<tr><td>N − 22 = 100</td><td>−22</td><td>+22</td><td>N = 122</td></tr>\n<tr><td>N ÷ 5 = 50</td><td>÷5</td><td>×5</td><td>N = 250</td></tr>\n</table>\n<p><b>Always plug the answer back in</b>: 3 × 7 = 21 ✓. Ten seconds of checking\nkills 100% of algebra slip-ups.</p>\n<h3>The cover-up trick (your formula X-ray)</h3>\n<p>For any three-letter formula like <b>E = I × R</b>: put your thumb over the\nletter you want. What's left shows the math. Cover I → you see E and R sitting\nas E over-and-beside R → <b>I = E ÷ R</b>. Cover E → I and R stand together →\n<b>E = I × R</b>. Works for d = v × t, P = E × I, a = l × w… every\nproduct-style formula in your training.</p>\n<div class=\"box ex\"><span class=\"bt\">Worked example — cover-up in action</span>\n<ol class=\"ladder\"><li><span class=\"step\">1 · Write what you know (with units)</span>Trip of 480 miles at 120 mph. Want: time. Formula: d = v × t.</li><li><span class=\"step\">2 · Pick the method — say it in English</span>Cover up t → left with d and v; they were multiplied, so divide: t = d ÷ v.</li><li><span class=\"step\">3 · Set it up</span>t = 480 ÷ 120</li><li><span class=\"step\">4 · One operation per line</span>480 ÷ 120 = <b>4 hours</b></li><li><span class=\"step\">5 · Calculator keystrokes</span><span class=\"keys\">480 ÷ 120 = 4</span></li><li><span class=\"step\">6 · Sanity check</span>Plug back: 120 mph × 4 hr = 480 miles. ✓</li></ol>\n</div>\n<div class=\"box key\"><span class=\"bt\">Must-Know</span>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Do the same thing to both sides</b> — the only rule.</li>\n<li>Un-do what's stuck to the variable: × with ÷, + with −.</li>\n<li><b>Cover-up trick:</b> hide the wanted letter; the survivors show the math.</li>\n<li>Always plug the answer back in to check.</li>\n</ul></div>\n<p class=\"cite\">Source: FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-16 (Algebra, Equations, Algebraic Rules, Solving for a Variable) · ACS AM.I.H.K6.</p>\n", "quiz": [{"q": "Algebra is the branch of mathematics that:", "options": ["Uses letters or symbols to represent variables in formulas and equations", "Studies the relationships between the sides and angles of triangles", "Deals only with whole numbers and their factors"], "correct": 0, "short": "Uses letters or symbols to represent variables in formulas and equations", "expl": "Know: in d = v × t, the letters d, v, t are variables holding places for real numbers. Sanity: side/angle relationships are TRIGONOMETRY.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-16 (Algebra) · AM.I.H.K6", "topic": "Algebra"}, {"q": "The golden rule when solving an equation for a variable is:", "options": ["Always move the variable to the right side", "Multiply everything by the variable first", "Whatever you do to one side of the equals sign, do to the other side too"], "correct": 2, "short": "Whatever you do to one side of the equals sign, do to the other side too", "expl": "Know: an equation is a balanced scale. Method: add, subtract, multiply, or divide BOTH pans and the balance holds. Sanity: doing it to one side only tips the scale — the equality breaks.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-16 (Algebraic Rules) · AM.I.H.K6", "topic": "Algebra"}, {"q": "Solve for N: 3N = 21", "options": ["N = 7", "N = 63", "N = 18"], "correct": 0, "short": "N = 7", "expl": "Know: N is multiplied by 3 — undo with division. Setup: divide both sides by 3. Calc: 21 ÷ 3 = 7. Keys: 21 ÷ 3 = . Sanity: 3 × 7 = 21 — plug it back and it balances. 63 is the multiplied trap.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-16 (Algebraic Rules) · AM.I.H.K6", "topic": "Algebra"}, {"q": "Solve for N: N + 17 = 59", "options": ["N = 42", "N = 76", "N = 3.47"], "correct": 0, "short": "N = 42", "expl": "Know: 17 is added — undo with subtraction. Setup: subtract 17 from both sides. Calc: 59 − 17 = 42. Sanity: 42 + 17 = 59 — balances. 76 is the added trap.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-16 (Algebraic Rules) · AM.I.H.K6", "topic": "Algebra"}, {"q": "Solve for N: N − 22 = 100", "options": ["N = 4.55", "N = 78", "N = 122"], "correct": 2, "short": "N = 122", "expl": "Know: 22 is subtracted — undo with addition. Setup: add 22 to both sides. Calc: 100 + 22 = 122. Sanity: 122 − 22 = 100 — balances.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-16 (Algebraic Rules) · AM.I.H.K6", "topic": "Algebra"}, {"q": "Solve for N: N ÷ 5 = 50", "options": ["N = 10", "N = 250", "N = 45"], "correct": 1, "short": "N = 250", "expl": "Know: N is divided by 5 — undo with multiplication. Setup: multiply both sides by 5. Calc: 50 × 5 = 250. Sanity: 250 ÷ 5 = 50 — balances. 10 is the divided-again trap.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-16 (Algebraic Rules) · AM.I.H.K6", "topic": "Algebra"}, {"q": "Using the formula d = v × t, how far does an aircraft travel at 220 knots for 3 hours?", "options": ["73.3 nautical miles", "660 nautical miles", "223 nautical miles"], "correct": 1, "short": "660 nautical miles", "expl": "Know: d = v × t; v = 220, t = 3. Setup: d = 220 × 3. Calc: 660 NM. Keys: 220 × 3 = . Sanity: 200 kt for 3 hr would be 600 — a bit more fits.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-16 (Algebra — d = v × t) · AM.I.H.K6", "topic": "Algebra"}, {"q": "Using d = v × t rearranged, how LONG does a 480-mile trip take at 120 mph?", "options": ["4 hours", "2.5 hours", "57,600 hours"], "correct": 0, "short": "4 hours", "expl": "Know: solve d = v × t for t. Method: cover up t — you're left with d and v, and since they were multiplied, divide: t = d ÷ v. Calc: 480 ÷ 120 = 4 hr. Keys: 480 ÷ 120 = . Sanity: 120 mph for 4 hr = 480 mi. 57,600 is the multiplied trap.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-16 (Solving for a Variable) · AM.I.H.K6", "topic": "Algebra"}, {"q": "Using Ohm's law E = I × R, the rearrangement that solves for R is:", "options": ["R = E ÷ I", "R = I ÷ E", "R = E × I"], "correct": 0, "short": "R = E ÷ I", "expl": "Know: R is multiplied by I. Method: cover-up trick — hide R in E = I × R; E sits alone on one side, I beside the hidden R, so R = E ÷ I. Sanity: units check — volts ÷ amps = ohms.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-16 (Solving for a Variable) · AM.I.H.K6", "topic": "Algebra"}]}, {"id": "mau8s2", "title": "PEMDAS in algebra & peeling formulas", "lesson": "\n<p><b>The Big Idea.</b> Real aviation formulas come with layers — parentheses,\nsquares, roots. Two skills finish the job: PEMDAS inside algebra, and peeling a\nformula to free the letter you want.</p>\n<h3>PEMDAS wears a hard hat here</h3>\n<p>The handbook's algebraic order: 1. Parentheses · 2. Exponents (roots too) ·\n3. Multiplication · 4. Division · 5. Addition · 6. Subtraction. Work ONE\noperation per line and you cannot get lost:</p>\n<div class=\"box ex\"><span class=\"bt\">Worked example</span>\n<ol class=\"ladder\"><li><span class=\"step\">1 · Write what you know (with units)</span>Solve N = (12 − 4)² ÷ 8 + 3.</li><li><span class=\"step\">2 · Pick the method — say it in English</span>PEMDAS, one line per move.</li><li><span class=\"step\">3 · Set it up</span>N = (12 − 4)² ÷ 8 + 3</li><li><span class=\"step\">4 · One operation per line</span>(12 − 4) = 8<br>8² = 64<br>64 ÷ 8 = 8<br>8 + 3 = <b>11</b></li><li><span class=\"step\">5 · Calculator keystrokes</span><span class=\"keys\">12 − 4 = 8 · x² = 64 · ÷ 8 = 8 · + 3 = 11</span></li><li><span class=\"step\">6 · Sanity check</span>Each line did exactly one thing — nothing skipped, nothing doubled.</li></ol>\n</div>\n<h3>Peeling a dressed-up formula (un-dress outside-in)</h3>\n<p>To solve <b>a = πr²</b> for r: the r is wearing two layers — squared, then\nmultiplied by π. Take them off in reverse order, doing each move to both sides:\ndivide by π (r² = a/π), then square-root both sides\n(<b>r = √(a/π)</b>). Like undressing: jacket off before shirt.</p>\n<p><b>The temperature formula is the classic exam trap.</b>\nC = 5/9 × (F − 32): the parentheses go FIRST. At 212 °F: 212 − 32 = 180, then\n5/9 × 180 = <b>100 °C</b> — boiling water, sanity confirmed. Skipping the\nparentheses gives 117.8 — the test's favorite wrong option. Going the other way:\nF = (9/5 × °C) + 32.</p>\n<div class=\"box key\"><span class=\"bt\">Must-Know</span>\n<ul>\n<li>Algebraic order: <b>P, E(+roots), M, D, A, S</b> — one operation per line.</li>\n<li>Peel formulas <b>outside-in</b>: un-do the last-applied layer first.</li>\n<li>a = πr² → <b>r = √(a/π)</b>.</li>\n<li>C = 5/9 × (F − 32) — <b>parentheses first</b>. 212 °F = 100 °C.</li>\n</ul></div>\n<p class=\"cite\">Source: FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, pp. 3-16 to 3-18 (Solving for a Variable, Order of Operation) + Figure 3-23 (temperature) · ACS AM.I.H.K6, R1.</p>\n", "quiz": [{"q": "In the order of operation for algebraic equations, roots (such as square roots) are treated as:", "options": ["Parentheses", "Exponents", "Division"], "correct": 1, "short": "Exponents", "expl": "Know: PEMDAS step 2 covers exponents AND roots — a root is just a fractional exponent (√ = power 1/2). Sanity: that is why √(9+16) forces the parentheses first (25), then the root (5).", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-17 to 3-18 (Order of Operation) · AM.I.H.R1", "topic": "Order of operations"}, {"q": "Solve: N = (12 − 4)² ÷ 8 + 3", "options": ["N = 19", "N = 5", "N = 11"], "correct": 2, "short": "N = 11", "expl": "Know: P, E, D, A in order. Calc line-by-line: (12−4) = 8; 8² = 64; 64 ÷ 8 = 8; 8 + 3 = 11. Keys: 12 − 4 = 8, x², ÷ 8 = 8, + 3 = 11. Sanity: skipping the parentheses gives a different (wrong) number — order matters.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-17 to 3-18 (Order of Operation for Algebraic Equations) · AM.I.H.R1", "topic": "Order of operations"}, {"q": "Solve: N = 4 × 3 + 10 ÷ 2", "options": ["N = 11", "N = 9", "N = 17"], "correct": 2, "short": "N = 17", "expl": "Know: × and ÷ before +. Calc: 4 × 3 = 12; 10 ÷ 2 = 5; 12 + 5 = 17. Sanity: doing 3+10 first is the reading-order trap.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-18 (Order of Operation) · AM.I.H.R1", "topic": "Order of operations"}, {"q": "The area formula a = πr² solved for r is:", "options": ["r = (a ÷ π)²", "r = the square root of (a ÷ π)", "r = a ÷ (2π)"], "correct": 1, "short": "r = the square root of (a ÷ π)", "expl": "Know: un-dress r layer by layer, outside-in. Method: r² = a ÷ π (divide both sides by π); then take the square root of both sides. Calc: r = √(a/π). Sanity: plug back — π(√(a/π))² = π · a/π = a. ✓", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-16 (Solving for a Variable) · AM.I.H.K6", "topic": "Formula rearrangement"}, {"q": "Using P = E × I (power = volts × amps), a 500-watt landing light on a 25-volt system draws:", "options": ["20 amps", "12,500 amps", "0.05 amps"], "correct": 0, "short": "20 amps", "expl": "Know: solve for I. Method: cover up I → I = P ÷ E. Setup: 500 ÷ 25. Calc: 20 A. Keys: 500 ÷ 25 = . Sanity: 25 V × 20 A = 500 W — rebuilds the power.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-16 (Solving for a Variable) · AM.I.H.K6", "topic": "Formula rearrangement"}, {"q": "The formula for converting temperature is C = 5/9 × (F − 32). At 212 °F, the Celsius temperature is:", "options": ["117.8 °C", "100 °C", "148 °C"], "correct": 1, "short": "100 °C", "expl": "Know: parentheses first. Calc: 212 − 32 = 180; 5/9 × 180 = 100 °C. Keys: 212 − 32 = 180, × 5 ÷ 9 = 100. Sanity: 212 °F is boiling water = 100 °C — the answer must land exactly there. 117.8 is the skipped-parentheses trap.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-22, Figure 3-23 (Temperature conversion) · AM.I.H.K6, AM.I.H.R1", "topic": "Formula rearrangement"}, {"q": "In the formula CR = V_BDC ÷ V_TDC, solving for V_TDC gives:", "options": ["V_TDC = CR − V_BDC", "V_TDC = V_BDC ÷ CR", "V_TDC = CR × V_BDC"], "correct": 1, "short": "V_TDC = V_BDC ÷ CR", "expl": "Know: V_TDC sits under the divide. Method: multiply both sides by V_TDC, then divide both sides by CR. Calc: V_TDC = V_BDC/CR. Sanity: a 10:1 engine with 100 cu in at BDC → 100 ÷ 10 = 10 cu in at TDC — small, as top-dead-center must be.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-16 (Solving for a Variable) + p. 3-7 (compression ratio) · AM.I.H.K6, AM.I.H.S7", "topic": "Formula rearrangement"}, {"q": "Why do parentheses appear in the expression 8 × (10 − 6)?", "options": ["To force the subtraction to be done before the multiplication", "To show the numbers inside are negative", "For readability only — they do not change the result"], "correct": 0, "short": "To force the subtraction to be done before the multiplication", "expl": "Know: parentheses override normal order. Calc with: 10−6 = 4; 8×4 = 32. Without: 8×10−6 = 74. Sanity: different answers prove the parentheses are load-bearing.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-17 (Use of Parentheses) · AM.I.H.R1", "topic": "Order of operations"}]}]}, {"n": 9, "day": "Unit 9", "title": "Area of Plane Shapes — Wings Included", "ref": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3 pp. 3-18 to 3-20, 3-25 · AM.I.H.K1, K3, S3", "sections": [{"id": "mau9s1", "title": "Rectangle, square, triangle, parallelogram, trapezoid", "lesson": "\n<p><b>The Big Idea.</b> <b>Area is the amount of surface</b> — how much paint,\nsheet metal, or fabric covers a flat shape — always in SQUARE units (sq in,\nsq ft). Distance around the edge is perimeter; that's a different question.</p>\n<p><b>Analogy.</b> Area is floor tiling: a = l × w just counts the 1-inch tiles\nin each row (l) times the number of rows (w). Every other formula below is a\nrectangle in disguise.</p>\n<table>\n<tr><th>Shape</th><th>Formula</th><th>Disguise</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Rectangle</td><td>a = l × w</td><td>the original</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Square</td><td>a = s²</td><td>rectangle with equal sides</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Parallelogram</td><td>a = l × h</td><td>pushed-over rectangle (h is the upright height, not the slanted side)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Triangle</td><td>a = ½(b × h)</td><td>HALF a rectangle (cut on the diagonal)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Trapezoid</td><td>a = ½(b₁ + b₂) × h</td><td>rectangle whose width is the AVERAGE of the two bases</td></tr>\n</table>\n\n<figure class=\"fig\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 360 110\" width=\"360\" height=\"110\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Triangle and trapezoid area diagrams\">\n<g>\n<polygon points=\"20,90 140,90 100,25\" fill=\"#f3f7fc\" stroke=\"#15436e\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n<line x1=\"100\" y1=\"25\" x2=\"100\" y2=\"90\" stroke=\"#d6453d\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" stroke-dasharray=\"4,3\"/>\n<text x=\"80\" y=\"104\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"12\" fill=\"#1c2330\">base b</text>\n<text x=\"112\" y=\"62\" font-size=\"12\" fill=\"#d6453d\">h</text>\n<text x=\"80\" y=\"16\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"12\" fill=\"#15436e\">a = ½ (b × h)</text>\n</g>\n<g>\n<polygon points=\"220,90 340,90 315,30 250,30\" fill=\"#f3f7fc\" stroke=\"#15436e\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n<line x1=\"262\" y1=\"30\" x2=\"262\" y2=\"90\" stroke=\"#d6453d\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" stroke-dasharray=\"4,3\"/>\n<text x=\"282\" y=\"104\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"12\" fill=\"#1c2330\">base₁ (bottom)</text>\n<text x=\"282\" y=\"26\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"12\" fill=\"#1c2330\">base₂ (top)</text>\n<text x=\"252\" y=\"64\" font-size=\"12\" fill=\"#d6453d\">h</text>\n</g>\n</svg>\n<figcaption>Triangle: half a rectangle. Trapezoid: average the two bases, then times height.</figcaption></figure>\n\n<p><b>Triangle vocabulary (ACS K3):</b> by sides — <b>equilateral</b> (3 equal),\n<b>isosceles</b> (2 equal), <b>scalene</b> (none equal). By angles —\n<b>acute</b> (all under 90°), <b>right</b> (one exactly 90°), <b>obtuse</b> (one\nover 90°). The three angles of ANY triangle total 180°.</p>\n<p><b>Units:</b> 1 square foot = 12 in × 12 in = <b>144 square inches</b>;\n1 square yard = 9 square feet. Convert LENGTHS to one unit BEFORE multiplying —\nmixed feet-and-inches is the #1 area error.</p>\n<div class=\"box ex\"><span class=\"bt\">Worked example — triangle (handbook's own)</span>\n<ol class=\"ladder\"><li><span class=\"step\">1 · Write what you know (with units)</span>Triangle base 2 ft 6 in, height 3 ft 2 in. Want: area in square inches.</li><li><span class=\"step\">2 · Pick the method — say it in English</span>Convert both to inches first, then a = ½(b × h).</li><li><span class=\"step\">3 · Set it up</span>b = 30 in, h = 38 in → a = ½ × 30 × 38</li><li><span class=\"step\">4 · One operation per line</span>2 ft 6 in = 30 in<br>3 ft 2 in = 38 in<br>30 × 38 = 1,140<br>1,140 ÷ 2 = <b>570 sq in</b></li><li><span class=\"step\">5 · Calculator keystrokes</span><span class=\"keys\">30 × 38 = 1140 ÷ 2 = 570</span></li><li><span class=\"step\">6 · Sanity check</span>The full 30 × 38 rectangle is 1,140; a triangle is half of it. 570 ✓</li></ol>\n</div>\n<div class=\"box key\"><span class=\"bt\">Must-Know</span>\n<ul>\n<li>Area = surface, in <b>square units</b>.</li>\n<li>Triangle = <b>½</b> (b × h); trapezoid = <b>average the bases</b> × h.</li>\n<li>Scalene/isosceles/equilateral = 0/2/3 equal sides; angles total 180°.</li>\n<li><b>1 sq ft = 144 sq in.</b> Convert lengths before multiplying.</li>\n</ul></div>\n<p class=\"cite\">Source: FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, pp. 3-18 to 3-20 (Computing Area, Units of Area) · ACS AM.I.H.K1, K3.</p>\n", "quiz": [{"q": "Area is a measurement of:", "options": ["The amount of surface of an object, in square units", "The distance around the outside of an object", "The space an object occupies, in cubic units"], "correct": 0, "short": "The amount of surface of an object, in square units", "expl": "Know: area = surface = SQUARE units (sq in, sq ft). Sanity: distance around = perimeter/circumference; cubic units = VOLUME.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-18 (Computing Area of Two-Dimensional Solids) · AM.I.H.K1", "topic": "Area"}, {"q": "An aircraft floor panel is a rectangle 24 inches long and 12 inches wide. Its area is:", "options": ["72 square inches", "36 square inches", "288 square inches"], "correct": 2, "short": "288 square inches", "expl": "Know: rectangle: a = l × w. Setup: 24 × 12. Calc: 288 sq in. Keys: 24 × 12 = . Sanity: 36 is the ADDED trap (perimeter thinking); a 2-ft by 1-ft panel is 2 sq ft = 288 sq in. ✓", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-18 (Rectangle — floor panel example) · AM.I.H.K1", "topic": "Area"}, {"q": "The area of a square access plate whose side measures 25 inches is:", "options": ["50 square inches", "625 square inches", "100 square inches"], "correct": 1, "short": "625 square inches", "expl": "Know: square: a = s². Setup: 25 × 25. Calc: 625 sq in. Keys: 25 x² = . Sanity: 100 is the perimeter (4 × 25) trap.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-18 (Square — access plate example) · AM.I.H.K1", "topic": "Area"}, {"q": "The formula for the area of a triangle is:", "options": ["1/3 × (base × height)", "base × height", "1/2 × (base × height)"], "correct": 2, "short": "1/2 × (base × height)", "expl": "Know: a triangle is half a rectangle — draw the diagonal of any rectangle and you get two equal triangles. Method: a = ½(b × h). Sanity: b × h alone is the FULL rectangle.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-18 (Triangle) · AM.I.H.K1", "topic": "Area"}, {"q": "A triangle has a base of 2 ft 6 in and a height of 3 ft 2 in. Its area is:", "options": ["570 square inches", "285 square inches", "1,140 square inches"], "correct": 0, "short": "570 square inches", "expl": "Know: convert to inches first: 2'6\" = 30 in; 3'2\" = 38 in. Setup: ½ × 30 × 38. Calc: 30 × 38 = 1,140; ÷ 2 = 570 sq in. Keys: 30 × 38 ÷ 2 = . Sanity: 1,140 is the forgot-the-half trap.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-18 (Triangle — worked example, Figure 3-17) · AM.I.H.K1", "topic": "Area"}, {"q": "A triangle with all three sides of different lengths is called:", "options": ["Scalene", "Equilateral", "Isosceles"], "correct": 0, "short": "Scalene", "expl": "Know: by sides — equilateral (all 3 equal), isosceles (2 equal), scalene (none equal). Sanity: 'scale-ene' = all different, like unequal pans of a scale.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-18 (Triangle — classification) · AM.I.H.K3", "topic": "Geometry terms"}, {"q": "The area of a parallelogram is:", "options": ["sum of the four sides", "length × height", "1/2 × (length × height)"], "correct": 1, "short": "length × height", "expl": "Know: a parallelogram is a pushed-over rectangle — same base, same vertical height, same area. Method: a = l × h (h is the PERPENDICULAR height, not the slanted side). Sanity: the ½ belongs to triangles.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-18 (Parallelogram) · AM.I.H.K1", "topic": "Area"}, {"q": "A trapezoid has bases of 14 inches and 10 inches and a height of 6 inches. Its area is:", "options": ["840 square inches", "144 square inches", "72 square inches"], "correct": 2, "short": "72 square inches", "expl": "Know: trapezoid: a = ½(b1 + b2) × h — 'average the two bases, times height.' Setup: ½(14 + 10) × 6. Calc: 24 ÷ 2 = 12; 12 × 6 = 72 sq in. Keys: 14 + 10 = 24, ÷ 2 = 12, × 6 = 72. Sanity: it should land between the 10-base rectangle (60) and the 14-base rectangle (84) — 72 does.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-18 to 3-19 (Trapezoid — worked example) · AM.I.H.K1", "topic": "Area"}, {"q": "One square foot equals how many square inches?", "options": ["12 square inches", "1,728 square inches", "144 square inches"], "correct": 2, "short": "144 square inches", "expl": "Know: a square foot is 12 in × 12 in. Calc: 12 × 12 = 144 sq in. Sanity: 12 is the linear-inches trap; 1,728 is the CUBIC-inches-per-cubic-foot number.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-20 (Units of Area) · AM.I.H.K1", "topic": "Area"}]}, {"id": "mau9s2", "title": "Circle, wing area & the Pythagorean theorem", "lesson": "\n<p><b>The Big Idea.</b> Circles run the whole airplane — cylinder bores,\ninstrument faces, rivet holes. Three words, one number:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Radius (r)</b> — center to edge.</li>\n<li><b>Diameter (d)</b> — all the way across through the center = <b>2r</b>.</li>\n<li><b>Circumference (c)</b> — the distance around = <b>π × d</b>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><b>π (pi) ≈ 3.1416</b> — wrap a string around any circle and it measures about\n3.14 diameters. Area of a circle: <b>a = πr²</b>. <b>The one trap:</b> the\nformula wants the RADIUS. Given a bore (diameter) of 5 in, halve it first:\nr = 2.5; a = 3.1416 × 2.5² = <b>19.635 sq in</b>. Forgetting to halve gives an\nanswer exactly 4× too big — and that wrong number is always among the options.</p>\n<h3>Wing area (ACS skill S3 — they WILL make you compute one)</h3>\n<p><b>a = span × mean chord</b> — wingtip-to-wingtip length times the average\nfront-to-back width. Handbook example: span 50 ft, chord 6'8\" (= 6.67 ft):\n50 × 6.67 = <b>333.5 sq ft</b>. It's a rectangle formula because the \"mean\"\nchord has already averaged out the taper.</p>\n<h3>The Pythagorean theorem — the diagonal machine</h3>\n<p>In any RIGHT triangle, <b>a² + b² = c²</b>, where c is the <b>hypotenuse</b> —\nthe longest side, facing the 90° angle. It answers every \"will it fit\ndiagonally?\" question in the shop.</p>\n<div class=\"box ex\"><span class=\"bt\">Worked example — cargo door (handbook's own)</span>\n<ol class=\"ladder\"><li><span class=\"step\">1 · Write what you know (with units)</span>Door opening 7 ft wide × 5.5 ft tall. Will an 8-ft-square plate pass through diagonally?</li><li><span class=\"step\">2 · Pick the method — say it in English</span>The diagonal is the hypotenuse: c = √(a² + b²). Compare c with 8 ft.</li><li><span class=\"step\">3 · Set it up</span>c² = 7² + 5.5²</li><li><span class=\"step\">4 · One operation per line</span>7² = 49<br>5.5² = 30.25<br>49 + 30.25 = 79.25<br>√79.25 = <b>8.9 ft</b></li><li><span class=\"step\">5 · Calculator keystrokes</span><span class=\"keys\">7 x² + 5.5 x² = 79.25 √ = 8.9</span></li><li><span class=\"step\">6 · Sanity check</span>8.9 &gt; 8 → the plate fits with an inch to spare. And 8.9 beats the longest side (7) but is under 7 + 5.5 — a legal hypotenuse.</li></ol>\n</div>\n<div class=\"box key\"><span class=\"bt\">Must-Know</span>\n<ul>\n<li>c = πd · a = πr² · <b>halve the diameter first</b>.</li>\n<li>π ≈ 3.1416. d = 2r.</li>\n<li><b>Wing area = span × mean chord.</b></li>\n<li>Pythagorean: <b>a² + b² = c²</b>; hypotenuse = longest side, opposite the right angle. Don't forget the final √.</li>\n</ul></div>\n<p class=\"cite\">Source: FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, pp. 3-13, 3-19 to 3-20, 3-25 to 3-26 (Circle, Wing Area, Pythagorean Theorem) · ACS AM.I.H.K1, K3, S3.</p>\n", "quiz": [{"q": "The diameter of a circle is:", "options": ["The distance across the circle through its center — twice the radius", "The distance from the center to the edge", "The distance around the outside of the circle"], "correct": 0, "short": "The distance across the circle through its center — twice the radius", "expl": "Know: d = 2r. Center-to-edge = RADIUS; distance around = CIRCUMFERENCE. Sanity: 'dia' = through.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-19 (Circle) · AM.I.H.K3", "topic": "Geometry terms"}, {"q": "Pi (π) is the number used to:", "options": ["Measure angles in any triangle", "Convert square inches to square feet", "Relate a circle's circumference and area to its diameter or radius — approximately 3.1416"], "correct": 2, "short": "Relate a circle's circumference and area to its diameter or radius — approximately 3.1416", "expl": "Know: c = πd and a = πr², with π ≈ 3.1416. Sanity: wrap a string around ANY circle — it is always about 3.14 diameters long. That constant is π.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-13 (Functions of Numbers — circumference) and p. 3-19 (Circle) · AM.I.H.K3", "topic": "Geometry terms"}, {"q": "A circle has a diameter of 10 inches. Its circumference is:", "options": ["15.7 inches", "78.5 inches", "31.4 inches"], "correct": 2, "short": "31.4 inches", "expl": "Know: c = π × d. Setup: 3.1416 × 10. Calc: 31.416 ≈ 31.4 in. Keys: 3.1416 × 10 = . Sanity: about 3 diameters around — right. 78.5 is the AREA of this circle (πr²) — different question.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-13 (Circumference example) · AM.I.H.K1, AM.I.H.K3", "topic": "Circle"}, {"q": "The bore (inside diameter) of an engine cylinder is 5 inches. The cross-sectional area is:", "options": ["19.635 square inches", "15.708 square inches", "78.54 square inches"], "correct": 0, "short": "19.635 square inches", "expl": "Know: a = πr²; radius = 5 ÷ 2 = 2.5 in. Setup: 3.1416 × 2.5². Calc: 2.5² = 6.25; × 3.1416 = 19.635 sq in. Keys: 2.5 x² × 3.1416 = . Sanity: 78.54 is the used-the-diameter trap (π × 5²) — always halve to the radius first.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-19 (Circle — cylinder bore example) · AM.I.H.K1", "topic": "Circle"}, {"q": "The formula for wing area is:", "options": ["area = span × mean chord", "area = 1/2 × span × chord", "area = span × aspect ratio"], "correct": 0, "short": "area = span × mean chord", "expl": "Know: a = s × c — wingtip-to-wingtip length times average front-to-back width. Sanity: it is a rectangle formula because the mean chord already averages out the taper.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-19 to 3-20 (Wing Area, Figure 3-22) · AM.I.H.S3", "topic": "Wing area"}, {"q": "Find the area of a tapered wing whose span is 50 feet and whose mean chord is 6 feet 8 inches:", "options": ["300 square feet", "333.5 square feet", "340 square feet"], "correct": 1, "short": "333.5 square feet", "expl": "Know: convert 8 in to feet: 8 ÷ 12 = 0.67 ft, so chord = 6.67 ft. Setup: a = 50 × 6.67. Calc: 333.5 sq ft. Keys: 50 × 6.67 = . Sanity: 50 × 6 = 300 and 50 × 7 = 350 — the answer must land between.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-20 (Wing Area — tapered wing example) · AM.I.H.S3", "topic": "Wing area"}, {"q": "The hypotenuse of a right triangle is:", "options": ["The longest side, opposite the 90° angle", "Either of the two sides forming the right angle", "The height measured from the base"], "correct": 0, "short": "The longest side, opposite the 90° angle", "expl": "Know: the hypotenuse faces the right angle and is always the longest side. Sanity: in the 3-4-5 triangle, 5 (the longest) is the hypotenuse.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-25 (Pythagorean Theorem, Figure 3-31) · AM.I.H.K3", "topic": "Geometry terms"}, {"q": "The two shorter sides of a right triangle measure 7 inches and 9 inches. The hypotenuse is:", "options": ["16 inches", "130 inches", "11.4 inches"], "correct": 2, "short": "11.4 inches", "expl": "Know: a² + b² = c². Setup: 7² + 9² = c². Calc: 49 + 81 = 130; c = √130 = 11.4 in. Keys: 7 x² + 9 x² = 130, √ = 11.4. Sanity: it must beat the longest side (9) but be less than 7+9=16. 130 is the forgot-the-root trap.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-25 (Pythagorean Theorem) · AM.I.H.K1, AM.I.H.K3", "topic": "Pythagorean theorem"}, {"q": "A cargo door opening is 7 feet wide by 5.5 feet tall. Can an 8-foot-square steel plate pass through diagonally?", "options": ["No — the diagonal of the opening is only 7.9 feet", "Yes — the diagonal of the opening is 8.9 feet, so the 8-foot plate fits", "No — a plate can never exceed the door's width"], "correct": 1, "short": "Yes — the diagonal of the opening is 8.9 feet, so the 8-foot plate fits", "expl": "Know: the diagonal is the hypotenuse. Setup: c² = 7² + 5.5². Calc: 49 + 30.25 = 79.25; √79.25 = 8.9 ft. Keys: 7 x² + 5.5 x² = 79.25, √ = 8.9. Sanity: 8.9 > 8, so the plate clears with an inch of sky.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-25 to 3-26 (Pythagorean Theorem — cargo door example) · AM.I.H.K1", "topic": "Pythagorean theorem"}]}]}, {"n": 10, "day": "Unit 10", "title": "Volume — Cylinder Displacement & Fuel Tanks", "ref": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3 pp. 3-20 to 3-24 · AM.I.H.K2, S2, S4", "sections": [{"id": "mau10s1", "title": "Boxes, cubes & the 231 / 1,728 / 7.5 constants", "lesson": "\n<p><b>The Big Idea.</b> <b>Volume is what a 3-D shape HOLDS</b>, in CUBIC units.\nLength × width gave you the floor (area); multiplying by height stacks\nidentical floors into a box: <b>v = l × w × h</b>.</p>\n<p><b>Analogy.</b> Volume is sugar cubes in a box: 3 long, 2 wide, 2 tall =\none layer of 6, stacked 2 high = 12 cubes = 12 cubic units. A <b>cube</b> is the\nspecial box with all sides equal: v = s³ (the handbook's 3-ft carton holds\n3 × 3 × 3 = 27 one-cubic-foot boxes).</p>\n<h3>The three magic volume numbers</h3>\n<table>\n<tr><th>Fact</th><th>Use</th></tr>\n<tr><td><b>1 gallon = 231 cubic inches</b></td><td>tank cu in ÷ 231 = gallons</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>1 cubic foot = 1,728 cubic inches</b></td><td>12 × 12 × 12</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>1 cubic foot ≈ 7.5 gallons</b></td><td>1,728 ÷ 231</td></tr>\n</table>\n<p>Don't mix them with the AREA number (144 sq in per sq ft) — 144 is flat,\n1,728 has depth.</p>\n<div class=\"box ex\"><span class=\"bt\">Worked example — baggage compartment (handbook's own)</span>\n<ol class=\"ladder\"><li><span class=\"step\">1 · Write what you know (with units)</span>Compartment 5 ft 6 in × 3 ft 4 in × 2 ft 3 in. Want: cubic feet.</li><li><span class=\"step\">2 · Pick the method — say it in English</span>Convert every dimension to decimal feet FIRST, then v = l × w × h.</li><li><span class=\"step\">3 · Set it up</span>5.5 × 3.33 × 2.25</li><li><span class=\"step\">4 · One operation per line</span>5 ft 6 in = 5.5 ft · 3 ft 4 in = 3.33 ft · 2 ft 3 in = 2.25 ft<br>5.5 × 3.33 = 18.33<br>18.33 × 2.25 = <b>41.25 cu ft</b></li><li><span class=\"step\">5 · Calculator keystrokes</span><span class=\"keys\">5.5 × 3.33 × 2.25 = 41.25</span></li><li><span class=\"step\">6 · Sanity check</span>Ballpark 5 × 3 × 2.5 ≈ 37 — a touch more with the real numbers. ✓</li></ol>\n</div>\n<div class=\"box ex\"><span class=\"bt\">Worked example — fuel tank capacity (ACS skill S4)</span>\n<ol class=\"ladder\"><li><span class=\"step\">1 · Write what you know (with units)</span>Rectangular tank 60 × 30 × 24 inches. Want: gallons.</li><li><span class=\"step\">2 · Pick the method — say it in English</span>Volume in cubic inches, THEN divide by 231.</li><li><span class=\"step\">3 · Set it up</span>(60 × 30 × 24) ÷ 231</li><li><span class=\"step\">4 · One operation per line</span>60 × 30 = 1,800<br>1,800 × 24 = 43,200 cu in<br>43,200 ÷ 231 = <b>187 gallons</b></li><li><span class=\"step\">5 · Calculator keystrokes</span><span class=\"keys\">60 × 30 × 24 = 43200 ÷ 231 = 187</span></li><li><span class=\"step\">6 · Sanity check</span>About 25 cubic feet × 7.5 gal ≈ 187. Cross-checked two ways. ✓</li></ol>\n</div>\n<div class=\"box key\"><span class=\"bt\">Must-Know</span>\n<ul>\n<li>v = l × w × h (box) · v = s³ (cube). Cubic units.</li>\n<li><b>231 cu in = 1 gal · 1,728 cu in = 1 cu ft · 1 cu ft ≈ 7.5 gal.</b></li>\n<li>1 cubic yard = 27 cubic feet.</li>\n<li>Convert dimensions to ONE unit before multiplying.</li>\n</ul></div>\n<p class=\"cite\">Source: FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, pp. 3-20 to 3-21 (Rectangular Solid, Cube) + Figure 3-23 (volume conversions) · ACS AM.I.H.K2, S4.</p>\n", "quiz": [{"q": "Volume is measured in:", "options": ["Square units, such as square inches", "Linear units, such as inches", "Cubic units, such as cubic inches or cubic feet"], "correct": 2, "short": "Cubic units, such as cubic inches or cubic feet", "expl": "Know: volume = how much a 3-D shape HOLDS — three dimensions multiplied gives units³. Sanity: square units measure flat surface (area); linear units measure length.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-20 (Computing Volume of Three-Dimensional Solids) · AM.I.H.K2", "topic": "Volume"}, {"q": "The formula for the volume of a rectangular solid is:", "options": ["2 × (length + width + height)", "length × width × height", "length × width"], "correct": 1, "short": "length × width × height", "expl": "Know: v = l × w × h — a box of unit cubes, counted in layers. Sanity: l × w alone is only the floor (area); the height stacks the layers.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-20 (Rectangular Solid) · AM.I.H.K2", "topic": "Volume"}, {"q": "A rectangular baggage compartment measures 5 ft 6 in long, 3 ft 4 in wide, and 2 ft 3 in high. It holds:", "options": ["30.25 cubic feet", "49.5 cubic feet", "41.25 cubic feet"], "correct": 2, "short": "41.25 cubic feet", "expl": "Know: convert to decimal feet: 5.5, 3.33, 2.25. Setup: v = 5.5 × 3.33 × 2.25. Calc: 5.5 × 3.33 = 18.33; × 2.25 = 41.25 cu ft. Keys: 5.5 × 3.33 × 2.25 = . Sanity: roughly 5 × 3 × 2.5 ≈ 37 — 41 fits the slightly bigger real numbers.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-20 (Rectangular Solid — baggage compartment example) · AM.I.H.S4", "topic": "Volume"}, {"q": "A cube-shaped carton measures 3 ft on each side. How many 1-cubic-foot boxes fit inside?", "options": ["27 boxes", "9 boxes", "18 boxes"], "correct": 0, "short": "27 boxes", "expl": "Know: cube: v = s³. Setup: 3 × 3 × 3. Calc: 27 cu ft → 27 boxes. Keys: 3 y^x 3 = . Sanity: 9 is one FLOOR of boxes (3×3); three floors of 9 = 27.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-21 (Cube — carton example) · AM.I.H.K2", "topic": "Volume"}, {"q": "One U.S. gallon equals:", "options": ["128 cubic inches", "231 cubic inches", "1,728 cubic inches"], "correct": 1, "short": "231 cubic inches", "expl": "Know: the fuel-tank conversion constant. 1 gal = 231 cu in. Sanity: 128 is fluid OUNCES per gallon; 1,728 is cubic inches per cubic FOOT.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-22, Figure 3-23 (Conversion Factors — Volume) · AM.I.H.K2", "topic": "Units of volume"}, {"q": "One cubic foot equals how many cubic inches, and approximately how many gallons?", "options": ["1,728 cubic inches; about 7.5 gallons", "144 cubic inches; about 1 gallon", "231 cubic inches; about 7.5 gallons"], "correct": 0, "short": "1,728 cubic inches; about 7.5 gallons", "expl": "Know: 12 × 12 × 12 = 1,728 cu in. Calc: 1,728 ÷ 231 = 7.48 ≈ 7.5 gal. Sanity: 144 is SQUARE inches per square foot — the 2-D cousin.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-22, Figure 3-23 (Conversion Factors — Volume) · AM.I.H.K2", "topic": "Units of volume"}, {"q": "A rectangular fuel tank measures 60 inches by 30 inches by 24 inches. Its capacity is approximately:", "options": ["187 gallons", "25 gallons", "43,200 gallons"], "correct": 0, "short": "187 gallons", "expl": "Know: volume first, then gallons. Setup: 60 × 30 × 24 = 43,200 cu in. Calc: 43,200 ÷ 231 = 187.0 gal. Keys: 60 × 30 × 24 = 43200, ÷ 231 = 187. Sanity: 43,200 is cubic INCHES, not gallons — the ÷231 step is the whole game.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-20 (Rectangular Solid) + Figure 3-23 (1 gal = 231 cu in) · AM.I.H.S4", "topic": "Volume"}, {"q": "One cubic yard equals:", "options": ["3 cubic feet", "27 cubic feet", "9 cubic feet"], "correct": 1, "short": "27 cubic feet", "expl": "Know: 1 yd = 3 ft, so 3 × 3 × 3 = 27 cu ft. Sanity: 9 is SQUARE feet per square yard; 3 is linear feet per yard — pick the right dimension.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-22, Figure 3-23 (Conversion Factors — Volume) · AM.I.H.K2", "topic": "Units of volume"}]}, {"id": "mau10s2", "title": "Cylinders & piston displacement (bore ÷ 2!)", "lesson": "\n<p><b>The Big Idea.</b> A cylinder is a circle given height:\n<b>v = πr² × h</b> — the round floor (πr²) stacked h tall. This one formula IS\nthe ACS skill \"compute the volume of a cylinder\" (S2), and it unlocks the most\naviation-flavored math question there is: <b>piston displacement</b>.</p>\n\n<figure class=\"fig\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 460 130\" width=\"460\" height=\"130\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Cylinder bore and stroke diagram\">\n<rect x=\"60\" y=\"20\" width=\"90\" height=\"95\" fill=\"#f3f7fc\" stroke=\"#15436e\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/>\n<rect x=\"66\" y=\"72\" width=\"78\" height=\"20\" fill=\"#ffce4d\" stroke=\"#15436e\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n<line x1=\"60\" y1=\"14\" x2=\"150\" y2=\"14\" stroke=\"#d6453d\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n<text x=\"105\" y=\"10\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"12\" fill=\"#d6453d\">bore (diameter)</text>\n<line x1=\"160\" y1=\"30\" x2=\"160\" y2=\"72\" stroke=\"#1f9d57\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n<text x=\"168\" y=\"55\" font-size=\"12\" fill=\"#1f9d57\">stroke</text>\n<text x=\"105\" y=\"86\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#1c2330\">piston</text>\n<text x=\"255\" y=\"45\" font-size=\"13\" fill=\"#15436e\" font-weight=\"bold\">V = π r² × stroke</text>\n<text x=\"255\" y=\"66\" font-size=\"12\" fill=\"#1c2330\">r = bore ÷ 2</text>\n<text x=\"255\" y=\"87\" font-size=\"12\" fill=\"#1c2330\">× number of cylinders</text>\n<text x=\"255\" y=\"108\" font-size=\"12\" fill=\"#1c2330\">= engine displacement</text>\n</svg>\n<figcaption>Bore = width of the hole; stroke = how far the piston sweeps. Halve the bore before squaring.</figcaption></figure>\n\n<h3>The vocabulary</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Bore</b> = inside diameter of the cylinder (you BORE a hole).</li>\n<li><b>Stroke</b> = distance the piston travels (you STROKE along a path). The\nstroke is the h in the formula.</li>\n<li><b>Piston displacement</b> = total volume swept by ALL pistons in ONE\ncrankshaft revolution = π × (bore ÷ 2)² × stroke × number of cylinders.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"box ex\"><span class=\"bt\">Worked example — displacement (handbook's own)</span>\n<ol class=\"ladder\"><li><span class=\"step\">1 · Write what you know (with units)</span>Bore 5.5 in, stroke 5.4 in, 8 cylinders. Want: total displacement.</li><li><span class=\"step\">2 · Pick the method — say it in English</span>One cylinder = π r² × stroke with r = bore ÷ 2, then × 8.</li><li><span class=\"step\">3 · Set it up</span>r = 5.5 ÷ 2 = 2.75 → v = 3.1416 × 2.75² × 5.4</li><li><span class=\"step\">4 · One operation per line</span>2.75² = 7.5625<br>7.5625 × 3.1416 = 23.758<br>23.758 × 5.4 = 128.29 cu in (one cylinder)<br>128.29 × 8 = <b>1,026.32 cu in total</b></li><li><span class=\"step\">5 · Calculator keystrokes</span><span class=\"keys\">2.75 x² × 3.1416 × 5.4 = 128.29 × 8 = 1026.3</span></li><li><span class=\"step\">6 · Sanity check</span>Real engines: an O-360 is 360 cu in from 4 cylinders — 1,026 from 8 big 5.5-in jugs is the right neighborhood.</li></ol>\n</div>\n<p><b>The 4× trap, one more time.</b> Squaring the BORE instead of the radius\ninflates the answer exactly 4×  (512.9 instead of 128.29). The FAA plants that\nnumber in the options every single time. <b>Halve, then square.</b></p>\n<div class=\"box key\"><span class=\"bt\">Must-Know</span>\n<ul>\n<li>Cylinder: <b>v = πr²h</b>. For engines, h = stroke.</li>\n<li><b>Bore = diameter → r = bore ÷ 2</b> BEFORE squaring.</li>\n<li>Displacement = per-cylinder volume × number of cylinders, per one crank revolution.</li>\n<li>Tank in cu in → gallons: <b>÷ 231</b>.</li>\n</ul></div>\n<p class=\"cite\">Source: FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, pp. 3-21 to 3-22 (Cylinder, Piston Displacement, bore &amp; stroke) · ACS AM.I.H.S2, S7.</p>\n", "quiz": [{"q": "The formula for the volume of a cylinder is:", "options": ["π × diameter² × height", "2 × π × radius × height", "π × radius² × height"], "correct": 2, "short": "π × radius² × height", "expl": "Know: v = πr²h — the circular end's area (πr²) times the length. Sanity: using the diameter squared gives 4× too much; 2πrh is the wall's SURFACE area.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-21 (Cylinder) · AM.I.H.S2", "topic": "Cylinder volume"}, {"q": "On a reciprocating engine, the BORE is the ______ and the STROKE is the ______:", "options": ["Piston diameter; connecting-rod length", "Inside diameter of the cylinder; distance the piston travels inside the cylinder", "Length of the cylinder; diameter of the piston"], "correct": 1, "short": "Inside diameter of the cylinder; distance the piston travels inside the cylinder", "expl": "Know: bore = the hole's width; stroke = the piston's travel. Memory hook: you BORE a hole (diameter), you STROKE along a path (travel). Sanity: in '5.5 bore × 5.4 stroke,' both are inches of a cylinder.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-22 (Cylinder — bore and stroke) · AM.I.H.S7", "topic": "Piston displacement"}, {"q": "Piston displacement of an engine is defined as:", "options": ["The cylinder volume divided by the compression ratio", "The total volume swept by all pistons in one revolution of the crankshaft", "The volume of one cylinder at top dead center"], "correct": 1, "short": "The total volume swept by all pistons in one revolution of the crankshaft", "expl": "Know: displacement = π × (bore/2)² × stroke × number of cylinders — the swept volume of every piston for ONE crank revolution. Sanity: bigger displacement = bigger air pump = more power potential.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-21 (Cylinder — piston displacement) · AM.I.H.S2", "topic": "Piston displacement"}, {"q": "An engine cylinder has a bore of 5.5 inches and a stroke of 5.4 inches. The displacement of ONE cylinder is:", "options": ["512.9 cubic inches", "128.29 cubic inches", "93.31 cubic inches"], "correct": 1, "short": "128.29 cubic inches", "expl": "Know: v = πr²h; r = bore ÷ 2 = 2.75 in; h = stroke = 5.4. Setup: 3.1416 × 2.75² × 5.4. Calc: 2.75² = 7.5625; × 3.1416 = 23.758; × 5.4 = 128.29 cu in. Keys: 2.75 x² × 3.1416 × 5.4 = . Sanity: 512.9 is the whole-bore-squared trap (4× too big).", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-22 (Cylinder — displacement worked example) · AM.I.H.S2", "topic": "Piston displacement"}, {"q": "Using 128.29 cubic inches per cylinder, the total displacement of the eight-cylinder engine is:", "options": ["513.16 cubic inches", "128.29 cubic inches", "1,026.32 cubic inches"], "correct": 2, "short": "1,026.32 cubic inches", "expl": "Know: total = per-cylinder × number of cylinders. Setup: 128.29 × 8. Calc: 1,026.32 cu in. Keys: 128.29 × 8 = . Sanity: that is how an 'O-1026-class' displacement number would be born — engine model numbers often carry it.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-22 (Cylinder — 8-cylinder total) · AM.I.H.S2", "topic": "Piston displacement"}, {"q": "A cylinder has a bore of 4 inches and a stroke of 4 inches. Its displacement is approximately:", "options": ["16 cubic inches", "50.27 cubic inches", "201.06 cubic inches"], "correct": 1, "short": "50.27 cubic inches", "expl": "Know: r = 2 in. Setup: 3.1416 × 2² × 4. Calc: 2² = 4; × 3.1416 = 12.566; × 4 = 50.27 cu in. Keys: 2 x² × 3.1416 × 4 = . Sanity: 201 is the diameter-squared trap; 16 forgot π entirely.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-21 to 3-22 (Cylinder volume) · AM.I.H.S2", "topic": "Piston displacement"}, {"q": "A cylindrical oil tank is 24 inches in diameter and 36 inches tall. Its volume is approximately:", "options": ["2,714 cubic inches", "65,144 cubic inches", "16,286 cubic inches"], "correct": 2, "short": "16,286 cubic inches", "expl": "Know: r = 12 in, h = 36 in. Setup: 3.1416 × 12² × 36. Calc: 144 × 3.1416 = 452.39; × 36 = 16,286 cu in. Keys: 12 x² × 3.1416 × 36 = . Sanity: ÷231 ≈ 70.5 gallons — believable for a big shop tank. 65,144 used the diameter squared.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-21 (Cylinder) · AM.I.H.S2, AM.I.H.S4", "topic": "Cylinder volume"}, {"q": "To convert a fuel tank's volume in cubic inches to gallons, you:", "options": ["Multiply by 231", "Divide by 231", "Divide by 1,728"], "correct": 1, "short": "Divide by 231", "expl": "Know: 1 gal = 231 cu in, so gallons = cu in ÷ 231. Sanity: multiplying would make the number of gallons LARGER than the cubic inches — backwards. ÷1,728 gives cubic FEET.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-22, Figure 3-23 (Conversion Factors — Volume) · AM.I.H.S4", "topic": "Units of volume"}]}, {"id": "mau10s3", "title": "Sphere, cone & surface area", "lesson": "\n<p><b>The Big Idea.</b> Two last solids, plus the difference between what a tank\nHOLDS and what it takes to BUILD it.</p>\n<h3>Sphere and cone</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Sphere:</b> v = <b>4/3 × π × r³</b> — radius CUBED (it grows in all three\ndirections). Handbook example: 34-in-diameter pressure tank → r = 17;\n1.33 × 3.1416 × 17³ = <b>20,528 cu in</b>. Halve the diameter FIRST, as always.</li>\n<li><b>Cone:</b> v = <b>1/3 × π × r² × h</b> — exactly one-third of its cylinder.\nPour three cones of water into the matching can and it fills perfectly.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Surface area — the sheet-metal question</h3>\n<p><b>Surface area = the sum of the areas of every face</b>, in SQUARE units.\nVolume asks \"how much fuel fits inside?\" Surface area asks \"how much aluminum\ndo I buy to build it?\" Two different questions about the same tank.</p>\n<table>\n<tr><th>Solid</th><th>Volume</th><th>Surface area</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Rectangular solid</td><td>l × w × h</td><td>2 × [(w×l) + (w×h) + (l×h)]</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Cube</td><td>s³</td><td><b>6 × s²</b> (six identical faces)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Cylinder</td><td>πr²h</td><td>2πr² + πdh (two lids + the wrap)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Sphere</td><td>4/3 πr³</td><td><b>4πr²</b></td></tr>\n</table>\n<div class=\"box ex\"><span class=\"bt\">Worked example — cube skin (handbook's own)</span>\n<ol class=\"ladder\"><li><span class=\"step\">1 · Write what you know (with units)</span>Cube-shaped box, 8-inch sides. Want: surface area.</li><li><span class=\"step\">2 · Pick the method — say it in English</span>A cube has 6 identical square faces: SA = 6 × s².</li><li><span class=\"step\">3 · Set it up</span>6 × 8²</li><li><span class=\"step\">4 · One operation per line</span>8² = 64 sq in (one face)<br>64 × 6 = <b>384 sq in</b></li><li><span class=\"step\">5 · Calculator keystrokes</span><span class=\"keys\">8 x² × 6 = 384</span></li><li><span class=\"step\">6 · Sanity check</span>Volume would be 8³ = 512 CUBIC inches — different number, different units, different question. Ours is square inches: skin, not contents.</li></ol>\n</div>\n<div class=\"box key\"><span class=\"bt\">Must-Know</span>\n<ul>\n<li>Sphere v = <b>4/3 πr³</b> · sphere skin = <b>4πr²</b>.</li>\n<li>Cone v = <b>1/3 πr²h</b> — a third of its cylinder.</li>\n<li>Cube skin = <b>6s²</b>.</li>\n<li>Volume = holds (cubic) · surface area = builds (square).</li>\n</ul></div>\n<p class=\"cite\">Source: FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, pp. 3-22 to 3-24 (Sphere, Cone, Surface Area, Figure 3-30) · ACS AM.I.H.K2.</p>\n", "quiz": [{"q": "The formula for the volume of a sphere is:", "options": ["1/3 × π × radius² × height", "4 × π × radius²", "4/3 × π × radius³"], "correct": 2, "short": "4/3 × π × radius³", "expl": "Know: sphere: v = 4/3 πr³ — radius CUBED because it grows in three directions. Sanity: 4πr² is the sphere's SURFACE area; 1/3 πr²h is the CONE.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-22 (Sphere) · AM.I.H.K2", "topic": "Volume"}, {"q": "A spherical pressure tank has a diameter of 34 inches. Its volume is approximately:", "options": ["3,632 cubic inches", "41,056 cubic inches", "20,528 cubic inches"], "correct": 2, "short": "20,528 cubic inches", "expl": "Know: r = 34 ÷ 2 = 17 in. Setup: 4/3 × 3.1416 × 17³. Calc: 17³ = 4,913; × 3.1416 = 15,434.7; × 4 ÷ 3 = 20,579 ≈ 20,528 (handbook uses 1.33 × 3.1416 × 4,913 = 20,528). Keys: 17 y^x 3 × 3.1416 × 1.33 = . Sanity: halving to the radius first is the step most people skip.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-22 to 3-23 (Sphere — pressure tank example) · AM.I.H.K2", "topic": "Volume"}, {"q": "The formula for the volume of a cone is:", "options": ["1/2 × π × radius² × height", "1/3 × π × radius² × height", "π × radius² × height"], "correct": 1, "short": "1/3 × π × radius² × height", "expl": "Know: a cone is exactly ONE-THIRD of the cylinder that shares its base and height — pour three cones of water into the cylinder and it fills. Sanity: πr²h with no fraction is the full cylinder.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-23 (Cone) · AM.I.H.K2", "topic": "Volume"}, {"q": "Surface area of a three-dimensional solid is:", "options": ["The area of its largest single face only", "The sum of the areas of all its faces or surfaces, in square units", "The same as its volume, in cubic units"], "correct": 1, "short": "The sum of the areas of all its faces or surfaces, in square units", "expl": "Know: surface area = the sheet metal needed to BUILD the tank; volume = what the tank HOLDS. Sanity: surface area is square units — it is an area, just wrapped around a solid.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-23 (Computing Surface Area of Three-Dimensional Solids) · AM.I.H.K2", "topic": "Surface area"}, {"q": "The surface area of a cube whose side measures 8 inches is:", "options": ["512 square inches", "64 square inches", "384 square inches"], "correct": 2, "short": "384 square inches", "expl": "Know: a cube has 6 identical square faces. Setup: 6 × 8². Calc: 64 × 6 = 384 sq in. Keys: 8 x² × 6 = . Sanity: 512 is the VOLUME (8³); 64 is one face only.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-23 to 3-24 (Surface Area — Cube) · AM.I.H.K2", "topic": "Surface area"}, {"q": "The formula for the surface area of a sphere is:", "options": ["2 × π × radius²", "4 × π × radius²", "4/3 × π × radius³"], "correct": 1, "short": "4 × π × radius²", "expl": "Know: sphere skin = 4πr² (exactly four circles of the same radius). Sanity: r² (an area) — the r³ version is the volume.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-24 and Figure 3-30 (Surface area formulas) · AM.I.H.K2", "topic": "Surface area"}, {"q": "A cylindrical fuel tank holds 4,620 cubic inches. In gallons, that is:", "options": ["20 gallons", "46.2 gallons", "2 gallons"], "correct": 0, "short": "20 gallons", "expl": "Know: gallons = cu in ÷ 231. Setup: 4,620 ÷ 231. Calc: 20 gal. Keys: 4620 ÷ 231 = . Sanity: 231 × 20 = 4,620 — exact.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-22, Figure 3-23 (1 gal = 231 cu in) · AM.I.H.S4", "topic": "Units of volume"}]}]}, {"n": 11, "day": "Unit 11", "title": "Measurement Systems & Conversions", "ref": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3 pp. 3-22, 3-26 to 3-27 · AM.I.H.K7, K11, S8", "sections": [{"id": "mau11s1", "title": "Metric vs. conventional & the prefix ladder", "lesson": "\n<p><b>The Big Idea.</b> Two measurement languages share your toolbox. The\n<b>conventional (U.S./English) system</b> grew from body parts and royal\ndecrees — 12 in to a foot, 5,280 ft to a mile, no pattern, all memorized. The\n<b>metric system (SI)</b> was engineered on purpose: everything scales by clean\npowers of ten, which is why it dominates worldwide and why ACS K7 expects you to\nconvert when a metric aircraft (Airbus and friends) rolls into a U.S. shop.</p>\n<p><b>Metric's whole trick is the prefix.</b> One base unit (meter, gram, liter)\nplus a prefix that says how many decimal hops:</p>\n<table>\n<tr><th>Prefix</th><th>Symbol</th><th>Means</th><th>Hops</th></tr>\n<tr><td>mega</td><td>M</td><td>× 1,000,000</td><td>6 right</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>kilo</b></td><td>k</td><td><b>× 1,000</b></td><td>3 right</td></tr>\n<tr><td>hecto</td><td>h</td><td>× 100</td><td>2 right</td></tr>\n<tr><td>deci</td><td>d</td><td>÷ 10</td><td>1 left</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>centi</b></td><td>c</td><td><b>÷ 100</b></td><td>2 left</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>milli</b></td><td>m</td><td><b>÷ 1,000</b></td><td>3 left</td></tr>\n<tr><td>micro</td><td>µ</td><td>÷ 1,000,000</td><td>6 left</td></tr>\n</table>\n<p><b>Analogy.</b> Prefixes are gear ranges on the same bicycle. The unit never\nchanges — you just shift the decimal point up or down the cassette. 2.5 kg →\ngrams: kilo means the grams number is 1,000× bigger → 3 hops right →\n<b>2,500 g</b>. 4,700 Ω → kΩ: 3 hops left → <b>4.7 kΩ</b>.</p>\n<div class=\"box ex\"><span class=\"bt\">Worked example</span>\n<ol class=\"ladder\"><li><span class=\"step\">1 · Write what you know (with units)</span>A wire measures 0.035 amps. Want: milliamps.</li><li><span class=\"step\">2 · Pick the method — say it in English</span>milli = thousandths, so the mA number is 1,000× bigger — 3 decimal hops right.</li><li><span class=\"step\">3 · Set it up</span>0.035 A → mA</li><li><span class=\"step\">4 · One operation per line</span>0.035 → 0.35 → 3.5 → <b>35 mA</b></li><li><span class=\"step\">5 · Calculator keystrokes</span><span class=\"keys\">.035 × 1000 = 35</span></li><li><span class=\"step\">6 · Sanity check</span>Milliamps are smaller units, so the number must grow. It did.</li></ol>\n</div>\n<div class=\"box key\"><span class=\"bt\">Must-Know</span>\n<ul>\n<li>Metric = <b>SI</b>; meter/liter/gram + prefixes; everything ×10s.</li>\n<li><b>kilo ×1,000 · hecto ×100 · centi ÷100 · milli ÷1,000 · mega ×1M · micro ÷1M.</b></li>\n<li>Smaller unit → bigger number (and vice versa) — direction-check every hop.</li>\n<li>Conventional system facts: 12 in = 1 ft · 3 ft = 1 yd · <b>5,280 ft = 1 mi</b> = 1,760 yd.</li>\n</ul></div>\n<p class=\"cite\">Source: FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, pp. 3-26 to 3-27 (Measurement Systems, Metric System, Figure 3-33) · ACS AM.I.H.K7, K11.</p>\n", "quiz": [{"q": "The metric system is also known as:", "options": ["The conventional system", "The English gravitational system", "The International System of Units (SI)"], "correct": 2, "short": "The International System of Units (SI)", "expl": "Know: metric = SI — the dominant world measurement language, built on decimals. Sanity: 'conventional' is the FAA's name for the U.S./English system (inches, feet, gallons, pounds).", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-26 (Metric System) · AM.I.H.K11", "topic": "Measurement systems"}, {"q": "Compared with the metric system, the conventional (U.S./English) system is:", "options": ["Used only outside the United States", "Based entirely on powers of ten", "A random collection of conversions (12 in/ft, 5,280 ft/mi) rather than a systematic decimal scheme"], "correct": 2, "short": "A random collection of conversions (12 in/ft, 5,280 ft/mi) rather than a systematic decimal scheme", "expl": "Know: the English system grew from body parts and royal decrees — 1 ft = 12 in but 1 mi = 5,280 ft; no pattern. Metric scales by clean tens. Sanity: that randomness is exactly why conversion factors must be memorized.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-26 (Conventional System / Metric System) · AM.I.H.K11", "topic": "Measurement systems"}, {"q": "In the metric system, the prefix 'kilo' means:", "options": ["One thousandth of", "One thousand times", "One hundred times"], "correct": 1, "short": "One thousand times", "expl": "Know: kilo = ×1,000 (1 km = 1,000 m). Sanity: hecto = ×100; MILLI = ÷1,000 — kilo and milli are mirror twins.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-26 to 3-27, Figure 3-33 (Metric prefixes) · AM.I.H.K11", "topic": "Metric prefixes"}, {"q": "In the metric system, the prefix 'centi' means:", "options": ["One tenth of", "One hundredth of", "One hundred times"], "correct": 1, "short": "One hundredth of", "expl": "Know: centi = ÷100 — one centimeter is a hundredth of a meter, like a cent is a hundredth of a dollar. Sanity: deci = ÷10; hecto = ×100.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-26 to 3-27, Figure 3-33 (Metric prefixes) · AM.I.H.K11", "topic": "Metric prefixes"}, {"q": "In the metric system, the prefix 'milli' means:", "options": ["One thousandth of", "One thousand times", "One millionth of"], "correct": 0, "short": "One thousandth of", "expl": "Know: milli = ÷1,000 (1 gram = 1,000 milligrams). Sanity: one MILLIONTH is 'micro' (µ); ×1,000 is kilo.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-26 to 3-27, Figure 3-33 (Metric prefixes) · AM.I.H.K11", "topic": "Metric prefixes"}, {"q": "The metric prefix 'mega' (M) means:", "options": ["One million times", "One thousand times", "One billionth of"], "correct": 0, "short": "One million times", "expl": "Know: mega = ×1,000,000 (a megohm is a million ohms). Sanity: kilo is only ×1,000; 'nano' is the billionth.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-27, Figure 3-33 (Metric prefixes) · AM.I.H.K11", "topic": "Metric prefixes"}, {"q": "2.5 kilograms equals how many grams?", "options": ["250 grams", "0.0025 grams", "2,500 grams"], "correct": 2, "short": "2,500 grams", "expl": "Know: kilo = ×1,000 → grams are smaller, so the number grows. Method: 3 decimal hops right. Calc: 2.5 → 2,500 g. Sanity: a bag of flour (~2.5 kg) is thousands of grams, not fractions.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-26 to 3-27 (Metric System, Figure 3-33) · AM.I.H.K11", "topic": "Metric prefixes"}, {"q": "A resistor measures 4,700 ohms. In kilohms that is:", "options": ["47 kΩ", "4.7 kΩ", "0.47 kΩ"], "correct": 1, "short": "4.7 kΩ", "expl": "Know: kilo = 1,000, so divide by 1,000 — 3 hops left. Calc: 4,700 → 4.7 kΩ. Sanity: 4.7 thousand = 4,700 — round trip checks.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-27, Figure 3-33 (Metric prefixes) · AM.I.H.K11", "topic": "Metric prefixes"}]}, {"id": "mau11s2", "title": "Crossing systems — length, weight, temperature & torque", "lesson": "\n<p><b>The Big Idea.</b> Crossing between systems is one multiplication with the\nright constant — the whole skill is knowing the constant and the direction.\nFrom the handbook's Figure 3-23 formula sheet:</p>\n<table>\n<tr><th>Convert</th><th>Constant</th><th>Example</th></tr>\n<tr><td>inches → millimeters</td><td><b>× 25.4</b></td><td>20 in = 508 mm (handbook's own)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>millimeters → inches</td><td>÷ 25.4</td><td>120 mm = 4.72 in</td></tr>\n<tr><td>ounces → grams</td><td>× 28.35</td><td>12 oz = 340.2 g (handbook's own)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>kilograms → pounds</td><td><b>× 2.2</b></td><td>70 kg = 154 lb</td></tr>\n<tr><td>gallons → liters</td><td>× 3.785</td><td>10 gal = 37.85 L</td></tr>\n<tr><td>°F → °C</td><td>C = 5/9 × (F − 32)</td><td>86 °F = 30 °C</td></tr>\n<tr><td>°C → °F</td><td>F = (9/5 × °C) + 32</td><td>40 °C = 104 °F</td></tr>\n</table>\n<p><b>The direction check beats memorization.</b> Millimeters are tiny, so any\nlength owns MORE of them than inches: in → mm must multiply. If your converted\nbolt comes out 3,048 inches long, you multiplied when you should have divided —\nthe absurdity is the alarm.</p>\n<h3>Torque — the ACS skill S8</h3>\n<p>Torque units are just length × force, so the 12-inches-per-foot fact does the\nwork: <b>ft-lb → in-lb: × 12</b> (10 ft-lb = 120 in-lb) ·\n<b>in-lb → ft-lb: ÷ 12</b> (420 in-lb = 35 ft-lb). Smaller unit, bigger\nnumber — same direction rule. You'll use this every time a torque wrench is\ncalibrated in one unit and the maintenance manual quotes the other.</p>\n<div class=\"box ex\"><span class=\"bt\">Worked example — mixed units on one wrench</span>\n<ol class=\"ladder\"><li><span class=\"step\">1 · Write what you know (with units)</span>The manual calls for 25 ft-lb; your small wrench reads inch-pounds. Want: the wrench setting.</li><li><span class=\"step\">2 · Pick the method — say it in English</span>Foot-pounds to inch-pounds — smaller unit, bigger number — multiply by 12.</li><li><span class=\"step\">3 · Set it up</span>25 × 12</li><li><span class=\"step\">4 · One operation per line</span>25 × 12 = <b>300 in-lb</b></li><li><span class=\"step\">5 · Calculator keystrokes</span><span class=\"keys\">25 × 12 = 300</span></li><li><span class=\"step\">6 · Sanity check</span>300 in-lb ÷ 12 lands back on 25 ft-lb — round trip closes.</li></ol>\n</div>\n<div class=\"box key\"><span class=\"bt\">Must-Know</span>\n<ul>\n<li><b>1 in = 25.4 mm</b> · 1 oz = 28.35 g · <b>1 kg = 2.2 lb</b> · 1 gal = 3.785 L.</li>\n<li>C = 5/9 × (F − 32) — parentheses first. F = (9/5 × C) + 32.</li>\n<li><b>Torque: ft-lb × 12 = in-lb; in-lb ÷ 12 = ft-lb.</b></li>\n<li>Direction check: smaller unit → bigger number, always.</li>\n</ul></div>\n<p class=\"cite\">Source: FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, pp. 3-22 (Figure 3-23), 3-26 (Conversions) · AIM BLK01 slides p.155-156 (torque) · ACS AM.I.H.K7, S8.</p>\n", "quiz": [{"q": "To convert inches to millimeters, multiply by:", "options": ["0.394", "2.54", "25.4"], "correct": 2, "short": "25.4", "expl": "Know: 1 in = 25.4 mm (memorize). Example from the handbook: 20 in × 25.4 = 508 mm. Sanity: 2.54 is CENTIMETERS per inch; 0.0394 in is one millimeter going the other way.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-26 (Measurement Systems & Conversions) + Figure 3-23 · AM.I.H.K7", "topic": "Conversions"}, {"q": "20 inches equals how many millimeters?", "options": ["788 mm", "50.8 mm", "508 mm"], "correct": 2, "short": "508 mm", "expl": "Know: mm = in × 25.4. Setup: 20 × 25.4. Calc: 508 mm. Keys: 20 × 25.4 = . Sanity: each inch is about 25 mm, and 20 × 25 = 500 — close to 508.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-26 (Conversions — worked example) · AM.I.H.K7", "topic": "Conversions"}, {"q": "A metric bolt measures 120 millimeters. In inches, that is approximately:", "options": ["4.72 inches", "12 inches", "3,048 inches"], "correct": 0, "short": "4.72 inches", "expl": "Know: going metric → English, DIVIDE by 25.4. Setup: 120 ÷ 25.4. Calc: 4.72 in. Keys: 120 ÷ 25.4 = . Sanity: multiplying (3,048) makes a bolt longer than a car — direction check catches it.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-26 (Conversions) + AIM BLK01 slides p.155 · AM.I.H.K7", "topic": "Conversions"}, {"q": "12 ounces equals how many grams?", "options": ["425 grams", "340.2 grams", "34.02 grams"], "correct": 1, "short": "340.2 grams", "expl": "Know: 1 oz = 28.35 g. Setup: 12 × 28.35. Calc: 340.2 g. Keys: 12 × 28.35 = . Sanity: this is the handbook's own worked example — about a third of a kilogram for a soda-can's weight of material.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-26 (Conversions — worked example) + Figure 3-23 · AM.I.H.K7", "topic": "Conversions"}, {"q": "One kilogram equals approximately:", "options": ["2.2 pounds", "0.45 pounds", "1.6 pounds"], "correct": 0, "short": "2.2 pounds", "expl": "Know: 1 kg = 2.2 lb (and 1 lb = 0.4536 kg — the same fact in reverse). Sanity: metric people are not 2× heavier; a 70-kg pilot is 154 lb.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-22, Figure 3-23 (Conversion Factors — Weight) · AM.I.H.K7", "topic": "Conversions"}, {"q": "Using Celsius = 5/9 × (F − 32), a ramp temperature of 86 °F is:", "options": ["30 °C", "16.7 °C", "47.8 °C"], "correct": 0, "short": "30 °C", "expl": "Know: parentheses first. Setup: 5/9 × (86 − 32). Calc: 86 − 32 = 54; 54 × 5 = 270; 270 ÷ 9 = 30 °C. Keys: 86 − 32 = 54, × 5 ÷ 9 = 30. Sanity: 30 °C is a hot day, 86 °F is a hot day — matches.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-22, Figure 3-23 (Temperature conversion) · AM.I.H.K7", "topic": "Conversions"}, {"q": "To convert foot-pounds of torque to inch-pounds:", "options": ["Multiply by 25.4", "Multiply by 12", "Divide by 12"], "correct": 1, "short": "Multiply by 12", "expl": "Know: 1 foot = 12 inches, so 1 ft-lb = 12 in-lb. Calc: 10 ft-lb × 12 = 120 in-lb. Sanity: inch-pounds are smaller units, so the NUMBER must grow. 25.4 is the millimeter constant — wrong lane.", "cite": "AIM BLK01 slides p.156 (Computing Torque Value) · AM.I.H.S8 · FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3 p. 3-22 (12 in = 1 ft)", "topic": "Torque conversion"}, {"q": "A torque wrench reads 420 inch-pounds. In foot-pounds, that is:", "options": ["42 ft-lb", "35 ft-lb", "5,040 ft-lb"], "correct": 1, "short": "35 ft-lb", "expl": "Know: in-lb → ft-lb = ÷12. Setup: 420 ÷ 12. Calc: 35 ft-lb. Keys: 420 ÷ 12 = . Sanity: foot-pounds are BIGGER units so the number must shrink — 5,040 went the wrong way.", "cite": "AIM BLK01 slides p.156 (Computing Torque Value) · AM.I.H.S8", "topic": "Torque conversion"}, {"q": "One mile equals:", "options": ["5,820 feet", "5,280 feet", "1,760 feet"], "correct": 1, "short": "5,280 feet", "expl": "Know: 1 mi = 5,280 ft = 1,760 YARDS. Sanity: 1,760 is the yards number wearing a feet costume — 1,760 × 3 = 5,280.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-22, Figure 3-23 (Conversion Factors — Length) · AM.I.H.K11", "topic": "Conversions"}]}]}, {"n": 12, "day": "Unit 12", "title": "The Mock Math Gauntlet", "ref": "Everything · FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3 pp. 3-1 to 3-28 · all AM.I.H elements", "sections": [{"id": "mau12s1", "title": "The Gauntlet — 51 mixed questions at exam difficulty", "lesson": "\n<p><b>This is the dress rehearsal.</b> Below the brief sits a mixed,\nGeneral-written-difficulty test covering every unit — whole numbers through\nconversions, in scrambled order, FAA 3-option format. The real General written\nis 60 questions across ALL subjects with a <b>70% pass mark</b> and this\nGauntlet gates at exactly that mark. Pass it and the math portion of the\nGeneral holds no surprises for you.</p>\n<h3>Exam habits that earn points</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Write the ladder, even for easy ones.</b> Know → method → setup → one\noperation per line. Most wrong answers on math questions are rushed setups, not\nbad arithmetic.</li>\n<li><b>The distractors are your own likely errors.</b> The wrong options are\nbuilt from forgetting the ½ (triangle), squaring the bore (cylinder),\nmultiplying instead of dividing (find-the-whole percent), left-to-right PEMDAS,\nand forgotten square roots. If your answer matches an option built from a trap,\nre-run the ladder.</li>\n<li><b>Sanity check with a ballpark.</b> Before picking, ask \"should this be\nbigger or smaller than what I started with?\" — it kills half the wrong options\ninstantly.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>The pocket formula card</h3>\n<table>\n<tr><th>Tool</th><th>Formula</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Rectangle / wing</td><td>a = l × w · wing = span × mean chord</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Triangle / trapezoid</td><td>½(b × h) · ½(b₁+b₂) × h</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Circle</td><td>a = πr² · c = πd · π ≈ 3.1416</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Box / cube / cylinder</td><td>lwh · s³ · πr²h (r = bore÷2)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Sphere / cone</td><td>4/3 πr³ · 1/3 πr²h</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Pythagorean</td><td>a² + b² = c²</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Volume constants</td><td>231 cu in/gal · 1,728 cu in/cu ft · ≈7.5 gal/cu ft</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Conversions</td><td>25.4 mm/in · 2.2 lb/kg · ft-lb × 12 = in-lb</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Temperature</td><td>C = 5/9(F−32) · F = 9/5·C + 32</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Percent</td><td>of = × · part ÷ whole · part ÷ percent</td></tr>\n</table>\n<p><b>No shame, no rush.</b> If the Gauntlet sends you back, it will tell you\nexactly which units to re-read — that's the system working, not you failing.\nTake it as many times as it takes; the real exam only ever sees the version of\nyou that passes.</p>\n<div class=\"box key\"><span class=\"bt\">Must-Know</span>\n<ul>\n<li>Real-exam pass mark: <b>70%</b> — the same gate as this Gauntlet.</li>\n<li>Ladder every problem: know → method → setup → one op per line → sanity.</li>\n<li>Distractors = your predictable traps. Ballpark first, then pick.</li>\n</ul></div>\n<p class=\"cite\">Source: FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, pp. 3-1 to 3-28 (all sections) · ACS AM.I.H (all K/R/S elements).</p>\n", "quiz": [{"q": "Solve: 8 + 4 × 6 =", "options": ["72", "32", "38"], "correct": 1, "short": "32", "expl": "PEMDAS: 4 × 6 = 24 first, then 8 + 24 = 32. 72 is the left-to-right trap.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-17 to 3-18 (Order of Operation) · AM.I.H.R1", "topic": "Gauntlet · Order of ops"}, {"q": "Solve: (8 + 4) × 6 =", "options": ["48", "72", "32"], "correct": 1, "short": "72", "expl": "Parentheses first: 8 + 4 = 12; then 12 × 6 = 72.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-17 (Use of Parentheses) · AM.I.H.R1", "topic": "Gauntlet · Order of ops"}, {"q": "The LCD for adding 1/4 + 1/6 is:", "options": ["12", "10", "24"], "correct": 0, "short": "12", "expl": "Multiples of 4: 4,8,12…; of 6: 6,12… — smallest shared is 12. (24 works but is not least.) Then 3/12 + 2/12 = 5/12.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-2 (Finding the Least Common Denominator) · AM.I.H.K13", "topic": "Gauntlet · Fractions"}, {"q": "Solve: 5/8 + 1/4 =", "options": ["6/12", "3/4", "7/8"], "correct": 2, "short": "7/8", "expl": "LCD 8: 1/4 = 2/8; 5/8 + 2/8 = 7/8. Never add straight across.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-2 (Addition of Fractions) · AM.I.H.K13", "topic": "Gauntlet · Fractions"}, {"q": "Solve: 2 1/2 − 1 3/4 =", "options": ["3/4", "1/2", "1 1/4"], "correct": 0, "short": "3/4", "expl": "Quarters: 2 1/2 = 2 2/4 = 1 6/4 (borrowed); 1 6/4 − 1 3/4 = 3/4. Check: 1 3/4 + 3/4 = 2 1/2. ✓", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-4 (Subtraction of Mixed Numbers) · AM.I.H.K13", "topic": "Gauntlet · Fractions"}, {"q": "Solve: 3/16 × 4 =", "options": ["12/64", "3/4", "3/64"], "correct": 1, "short": "3/4", "expl": "Straight across: 3 × 4 = 12 over 16 = 12/16 = 3/4. (12/64 multiplied the denominator too.)", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-3 (Multiplication of Fractions) · AM.I.H.K13", "topic": "Gauntlet · Fractions"}, {"q": "Solve: 1/2 ÷ 1/8 =", "options": ["1/4", "4", "1/16"], "correct": 1, "short": "4", "expl": "Keep-change-flip: 1/2 × 8/1 = 8/2 = 4 — there are four eighths in a half. 1/16 forgot to flip.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-3 (Division of Fractions) · AM.I.H.K13", "topic": "Gauntlet · Fractions"}, {"q": "Solve: 0.7 + 2.35 + 0.05 =", "options": ["3.01", "3.1", "2.47"], "correct": 1, "short": "3.1", "expl": "Line up the points: 0.70 + 2.35 + 0.05 = 3.10.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-4 (Addition of Decimal Numbers) · AM.I.H.K13", "topic": "Gauntlet · Decimals"}, {"q": "Solve: 4.2 × 0.35 =", "options": ["14.7", "1.47", "0.147"], "correct": 1, "short": "1.47", "expl": "42 × 35 = 1,470; count 1 + 2 = 3 decimal places → 1.470. Sanity: 4 × 0.35 ≈ 1.4.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-5 (Multiplication of Decimal Numbers) · AM.I.H.K13", "topic": "Gauntlet · Decimals"}, {"q": "Solve: 6.75 ÷ 2.25 =", "options": ["30", "0.33", "3"], "correct": 2, "short": "3", "expl": "Hop both decimals two places: 675 ÷ 225 = 3. Check: 2.25 × 3 = 6.75. ✓", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-6 (Division of Decimal Numbers) · AM.I.H.K13", "topic": "Gauntlet · Decimals"}, {"q": "Round 3.7468 to the nearest hundredth:", "options": ["3.74", "3.75", "3.7"], "correct": 1, "short": "3.75", "expl": "Hundredths digit 4; next digit 6 ≥ 5 → round up to 3.75.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-6 (Rounding Off Decimal Numbers) · AM.I.H.K9, AM.I.H.R3", "topic": "Gauntlet · Rounding"}, {"q": "Expressed as a decimal, 9/16 is:", "options": ["0.5625", "0.5265", "0.625"], "correct": 0, "short": "0.5625", "expl": "9 ÷ 16 = 0.5625. Sanity: 9/16 is just over half. (0.625 is 10/16 = 5/8.)", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-7 (Converting Fractions to Decimals) · AM.I.H.S5", "topic": "Gauntlet · Fraction ↔ decimal"}, {"q": "Expressed as a fraction in 16ths, 0.8125 is:", "options": ["13/16", "8/16", "12/16"], "correct": 0, "short": "13/16", "expl": "0.8125 × 16 = 13 exactly → 13/16. Check: 13 ÷ 16 = 0.8125. ✓", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-6 to 3-7 (Converting Decimal Numbers to Fractions) · AM.I.H.S5", "topic": "Gauntlet · Fraction ↔ decimal"}, {"q": "A 12-tooth pinion drives a 36-tooth spur gear. The gear ratio, in lowest terms, is:", "options": ["3:1", "1:3", "12:36 (cannot be reduced)"], "correct": 1, "short": "1:3", "expl": "12:36 ÷ 12 both sides → 1:3. The SPEED ratio would be the inverse, 3:1 — read what the question asks.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-8 (Ratio — gear ratio) · AM.I.H.K4, AM.I.H.S6", "topic": "Gauntlet · Ratio"}, {"q": "A cylinder holds 120 cubic inches at bottom dead center and 15 cubic inches at top dead center. The compression ratio is:", "options": ["1:8", "15:1", "8:1"], "correct": 2, "short": "8:1", "expl": "CR = BDC ÷ TDC = 120 ÷ 15 = 8 → 8:1. Big volume first — the mixture is squeezed INTO one-eighth the space.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-7 (Ratio — compression ratio) · AM.I.H.S7", "topic": "Gauntlet · Compression ratio"}, {"q": "Solve for X: 2/3 = X/12", "options": ["6", "8", "18"], "correct": 1, "short": "8", "expl": "Cross-multiply: 2 × 12 = 24; 24 ÷ 3 = 8. Check: 8/12 = 2/3. ✓", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-8 (Solving Proportions) · AM.I.H.K5", "topic": "Gauntlet · Proportion"}, {"q": "An aircraft flies 400 miles on 32 gallons. At the same rate, 900 miles requires:", "options": ["112.5 gallons", "72 gallons", "64 gallons"], "correct": 1, "short": "72 gallons", "expl": "400/32 = 900/G → G = (900 × 32) ÷ 400 = 28,800 ÷ 400 = 72 gal. Sanity: 12.5 mpg; 900 ÷ 12.5 = 72. ✓", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-8 to 3-9 (Solving Proportions) · AM.I.H.K5", "topic": "Gauntlet · Proportion"}, {"q": "25% of 480 is:", "options": ["12", "192", "120"], "correct": 2, "short": "120", "expl": "0.25 × 480 = 120. Sanity: 25% is a quarter, and 480 ÷ 4 = 120.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-10 (Finding a Percentage of a Given Number) · AM.I.H.K5", "topic": "Gauntlet · Percentage"}, {"q": "45 is what percent of 60?", "options": ["75%", "133%", "66.7%"], "correct": 0, "short": "75%", "expl": "Part ÷ whole: 45 ÷ 60 = 0.75 → 75%. Sanity: 45 is three-quarters of 60.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-10 (Finding What Percentage One Number is of Another) · AM.I.H.K5", "topic": "Gauntlet · Percentage"}, {"q": "36 is 30% of what number?", "options": ["108", "10.8", "120"], "correct": 2, "short": "120", "expl": "Whole = part ÷ percent = 36 ÷ 0.30 = 120. Check: 0.30 × 120 = 36. ✓ (10.8 multiplied instead.)", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-11 (Finding a Number When a Percentage of it is Known) · AM.I.H.K5", "topic": "Gauntlet · Percentage"}, {"q": "During a magneto check, rpm falls from 2,700 to 2,538. The percent drop is:", "options": ["16.2%", "6%", "9.4%"], "correct": 1, "short": "6%", "expl": "Drop = 162; 162 ÷ 2,700 (the ORIGINAL) = 0.06 → 6%.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-10 (Finding What Percentage One Number is of Another) · AM.I.H.K5", "topic": "Gauntlet · Percentage"}, {"q": "Solve: −8 + 3 =", "options": ["−11", "−5", "5"], "correct": 1, "short": "−5", "expl": "Debt picture: $8 in debt, pay $3 → still $5 in debt. Opposite signs: subtract sizes, keep the larger's sign.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-11 (Addition of Positive & Negative Numbers) · AM.I.H.K12", "topic": "Gauntlet · Signed numbers"}, {"q": "Solve: 12 − (−9) =", "options": ["3", "21", "−21"], "correct": 1, "short": "21", "expl": "Minus-a-minus becomes plus: 12 + 9 = 21.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-11 (Subtraction of Positive & Negative Numbers) · AM.I.H.K12", "topic": "Gauntlet · Signed numbers"}, {"q": "Solve: −5 × −4 =", "options": ["−20", "−9", "20"], "correct": 2, "short": "20", "expl": "Same signs multiply to POSITIVE: 5 × 4 = 20.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-11 (Multiplication of Positive & Negative Numbers) · AM.I.H.K12", "topic": "Gauntlet · Signed numbers"}, {"q": "Solve: −45 ÷ 9 =", "options": ["−4", "−5", "5"], "correct": 1, "short": "−5", "expl": "Different signs divide to NEGATIVE: 45 ÷ 9 = 5 → −5. Check: 9 × −5 = −45. ✓", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-11 (Division of Positive & Negative Numbers) · AM.I.H.K12", "topic": "Gauntlet · Signed numbers"}, {"q": "The value of 4³ is:", "options": ["12", "64", "81"], "correct": 1, "short": "64", "expl": "4 × 4 × 4 = 64. (12 is the 4×3 trap; 81 is 3⁴.)", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-11 to 3-12 (Powers) · AM.I.H.K10", "topic": "Gauntlet · Powers"}, {"q": "The value of 10⁻² is:", "options": ["0.01", "−20", "−100"], "correct": 0, "short": "0.01", "expl": "Negative power = reciprocal: 1/10² = 1/100 = 0.01. Negative exponents make numbers SMALL, not negative.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-12 (Negative Powers) · AM.I.H.K10", "topic": "Gauntlet · Powers"}, {"q": "The square root of 64 is:", "options": ["32", "8", "4,096"], "correct": 1, "short": "8", "expl": "8 × 8 = 64 → √64 = 8. (32 halved; 4,096 squared.)", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-12 (Square Roots) · AM.I.H.S1", "topic": "Gauntlet · Roots"}, {"q": "The square root of 50 lies between:", "options": ["24 and 26", "6 and 7", "7 and 8"], "correct": 2, "short": "7 and 8", "expl": "7² = 49 and 8² = 64 bracket 50 → √50 ≈ 7.07.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-12 (Square Roots) · AM.I.H.S1", "topic": "Gauntlet · Roots"}, {"q": "Expressed in scientific notation, 45,000 is:", "options": ["45 × 10³", "4.5 × 10⁻⁴", "4.5 × 10⁴"], "correct": 2, "short": "4.5 × 10⁴", "expl": "Hop left 4 places to leave one digit in front: 4.5 × 10⁴. The first part must be 1-to-10, so 45 × 10³ is illegal formatting.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-13 (Scientific Notation) · AM.I.H.K8", "topic": "Gauntlet · Scientific notation"}, {"q": "Expressed in scientific notation, 0.00271 is:", "options": ["2.71 × 10³", "2.71 × 10⁻³", "27.1 × 10⁻⁴"], "correct": 1, "short": "2.71 × 10⁻³", "expl": "Hop right 3 places; small numbers take NEGATIVE powers: 2.71 × 10⁻³.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-16 (Converting Standard to Scientific Notation) · AM.I.H.K8", "topic": "Gauntlet · Scientific notation"}, {"q": "The binary number 1010 equals what decimal number?", "options": ["10", "5", "12"], "correct": 0, "short": "10", "expl": "Places 8-4-2-1: 1×8 + 0×4 + 1×2 + 0×1 = 10.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-27 (Converting Binary to Decimal) · AM.I.H.K8", "topic": "Gauntlet · Binary"}, {"q": "Solve for N: 5N = 65", "options": ["N = 325", "N = 13", "N = 60"], "correct": 1, "short": "N = 13", "expl": "Divide both sides by 5: 65 ÷ 5 = 13. Check: 5 × 13 = 65. ✓", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-16 (Algebraic Rules) · AM.I.H.K6", "topic": "Gauntlet · Algebra"}, {"q": "Solve for N: N ÷ 4 = 12", "options": ["N = 48", "N = 16", "N = 3"], "correct": 0, "short": "N = 48", "expl": "Multiply both sides by 4: 12 × 4 = 48. Check: 48 ÷ 4 = 12. ✓", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-16 (Algebraic Rules) · AM.I.H.K6", "topic": "Gauntlet · Algebra"}, {"q": "Using E = I × R, the current in a 24-volt circuit with 6 ohms of resistance is:", "options": ["0.25 amps", "4 amps", "144 amps"], "correct": 1, "short": "4 amps", "expl": "Cover-up: I = E ÷ R = 24 ÷ 6 = 4 A. Check: 4 × 6 = 24 V. ✓ (144 multiplied instead.)", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-16 (Solving for a Variable) · AM.I.H.K6", "topic": "Gauntlet · Algebra"}, {"q": "Solve: 18 − 6 ÷ 2 =", "options": ["15", "12", "6"], "correct": 0, "short": "15", "expl": "Divide first: 6 ÷ 2 = 3; then 18 − 3 = 15. (6 is the left-to-right trap.)", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-18 (Order of Operation) · AM.I.H.R1", "topic": "Gauntlet · Order of ops"}, {"q": "Solve: (5 + 3)² ÷ 4 =", "options": ["8.5", "16", "11"], "correct": 1, "short": "16", "expl": "Parentheses: 8; exponent: 64; divide: 64 ÷ 4 = 16.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-17 to 3-18 (Order of Operation for Algebraic Equations) · AM.I.H.R1", "topic": "Gauntlet · Order of ops"}, {"q": "An inspection panel measures 15 inches by 9 inches. Its area is:", "options": ["48 square inches", "135 square inches", "24 square inches"], "correct": 1, "short": "135 square inches", "expl": "Rectangle: a = l × w = 15 × 9 = 135 sq in. (48 is the perimeter.)", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-18 (Rectangle) · AM.I.H.K1", "topic": "Gauntlet · Area"}, {"q": "A triangular gusset has a base of 10 inches and a height of 8 inches. Its area is:", "options": ["18 square inches", "80 square inches", "40 square inches"], "correct": 2, "short": "40 square inches", "expl": "a = ½(b × h) = ½ × 80 = 40 sq in. (80 forgot the half.)", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-18 (Triangle) · AM.I.H.K1", "topic": "Gauntlet · Area"}, {"q": "A trapezoidal panel has bases of 12 and 8 inches and a height of 5 inches. Its area is:", "options": ["50 square inches", "100 square inches", "60 square inches"], "correct": 0, "short": "50 square inches", "expl": "a = ½(12 + 8) × 5 = 10 × 5 = 50 sq in — average the bases, times height.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-18 to 3-19 (Trapezoid) · AM.I.H.K1", "topic": "Gauntlet · Area"}, {"q": "A round inspection hole is 8 inches in diameter. Its area is approximately:", "options": ["50.27 square inches", "201.06 square inches", "25.13 square inches"], "correct": 0, "short": "50.27 square inches", "expl": "r = 4 in; a = πr² = 3.1416 × 16 = 50.27 sq in. (201 used the diameter squared; 25.13 is the circumference.)", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-19 (Circle) · AM.I.H.K1", "topic": "Gauntlet · Area"}, {"q": "A wing has a span of 36 feet and a mean chord of 5.5 feet. Its area is:", "options": ["99 square feet", "198 square feet", "41.5 square feet"], "correct": 1, "short": "198 square feet", "expl": "a = span × mean chord = 36 × 5.5 = 198 sq ft. (41.5 added; 99 halved for no reason.)", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-19 to 3-20 (Wing Area) · AM.I.H.S3", "topic": "Gauntlet · Wing area"}, {"q": "The two legs of a right triangle are 6 and 8 inches. The hypotenuse is:", "options": ["10 inches", "100 inches", "14 inches"], "correct": 0, "short": "10 inches", "expl": "c = √(6² + 8²) = √(36 + 64) = √100 = 10 in — the classic 3-4-5 doubled. (100 forgot the root.)", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-25 (Pythagorean Theorem) · AM.I.H.K1, AM.I.H.K3", "topic": "Gauntlet · Pythagorean"}, {"q": "A baggage compartment measures 4 ft × 3 ft × 2 ft. Its volume is:", "options": ["24 cubic feet", "9 cubic feet", "52 cubic feet"], "correct": 0, "short": "24 cubic feet", "expl": "v = l × w × h = 4 × 3 × 2 = 24 cu ft.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-20 (Rectangular Solid) · AM.I.H.S4", "topic": "Gauntlet · Volume"}, {"q": "A cylinder has a bore of 4 inches and a stroke of 4 inches. For a SIX-cylinder engine of these dimensions, total displacement is approximately:", "options": ["50.3 cubic inches", "1,206 cubic inches", "301.6 cubic inches"], "correct": 2, "short": "301.6 cubic inches", "expl": "One cylinder: π × 2² × 4 = 50.27 cu in; × 6 cylinders = 301.6 cu in. (50.3 is one cylinder; 1,206 used the diameter squared.)", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-21 to 3-22 (Piston displacement) · AM.I.H.S2", "topic": "Gauntlet · Displacement"}, {"q": "A rectangular fuel tank measures 36 × 18 × 12 inches. Its capacity is approximately:", "options": ["33.7 gallons", "4.5 gallons", "7,776 gallons"], "correct": 0, "short": "33.7 gallons", "expl": "Volume: 36 × 18 × 12 = 7,776 cu in; ÷ 231 = 33.66 ≈ 33.7 gal. (7,776 is cubic inches, not gallons.)", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-20 (Rectangular Solid) + Figure 3-23 (231 cu in/gal) · AM.I.H.S4", "topic": "Gauntlet · Volume"}, {"q": "A spherical oxygen bottle is 6 inches in diameter. Its volume is approximately:", "options": ["904.8 cubic inches", "37.7 cubic inches", "113.1 cubic inches"], "correct": 2, "short": "113.1 cubic inches", "expl": "r = 3 in; v = 4/3 × π × 3³ = 4/3 × 3.1416 × 27 = 113.1 cu in. (904.8 used the diameter cubed.)", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-22 (Sphere) · AM.I.H.K2", "topic": "Gauntlet · Volume"}, {"q": "Convert 15 inches to millimeters:", "options": ["381 mm", "38.1 mm", "590.6 mm"], "correct": 0, "short": "381 mm", "expl": "15 × 25.4 = 381 mm. Sanity: about 25 mm per inch → 15 × 25 = 375ish. ✓", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-26 (Conversions) + Figure 3-23 · AM.I.H.K7", "topic": "Gauntlet · Conversions"}, {"q": "A torque of 24 foot-pounds equals:", "options": ["2 inch-pounds", "96 inch-pounds", "288 inch-pounds"], "correct": 2, "short": "288 inch-pounds", "expl": "ft-lb → in-lb = × 12: 24 × 12 = 288 in-lb. Smaller units, bigger number.", "cite": "AIM BLK01 slides p.156 (Computing Torque Value) · AM.I.H.S8", "topic": "Gauntlet · Torque"}, {"q": "Using Fahrenheit = (9/5 × °C) + 32, a battery-shop temperature of 40 °C is:", "options": ["72 °F", "104 °F", "129.6 °F"], "correct": 1, "short": "104 °F", "expl": "9/5 × 40 = 72; + 32 = 104 °F. Sanity: 40 °C is a very hot day — 104 °F agrees. (72 forgot the +32.)", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-22, Figure 3-23 (Temperature conversion) · AM.I.H.K7", "topic": "Gauntlet · Conversions"}, {"q": "One cubic foot of fuel is approximately how many gallons?", "options": ["1.7 gallons", "231 gallons", "7.5 gallons"], "correct": 2, "short": "7.5 gallons", "expl": "1 cu ft = 1,728 cu in; 1,728 ÷ 231 ≈ 7.5 gal. (231 is cubic inches per gallon, not gallons per cubic foot.)", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, p. 3-22, Figure 3-23 (Conversion Factors — Volume) · AM.I.H.K2, AM.I.H.K7", "topic": "Gauntlet · Conversions"}]}]}]};
